4001:
** πεντᾰκόσιοι**,
_Epic dialect_ **πεντηκόσιοι**, αι, α, **five hundred**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.7, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.7, etc.: in _sing._, πεντακοσία ἵππος}} **five hundred** horse, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Longus Scriptor Eroticus 3.1.}}
**II** at Athens, οἱ π. **the senate of** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|500, τὴν βουλὴν τοὺς π. “IG” 22.1629.243, 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 37, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.2}}; ἡ βουλὴ τῶν π. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 47.18.}}

4005:
Included with: ** πεντηκοστ-ός**, ή, όν,
**fiftieth**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 175b.}}
**II** _substantive_ **πεντηκοστή**, ἡ,
**II.1** (i.e. μερίς), **fiftieth part**: hence, **tax of two per century.** on exports and imports, at Athens, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.133, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.27: _plural_, [prev. author] 24.120; ἐς Ἀθάνας πεντηκοστὰ τῶν λίθων “IG” 42(1).103.46 (Epid.); at Delos, [prev. work]22.1635.38 (4th c.BC), 11(2).161 A 26 (3rd c.BC)}}; at Halicarnassus, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“OGI” 46.12 (3rd c.BC)}}; τῶν πρηθέντων τελείτω π. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“SIG” 229.5 (Erythrae, 4th c.BC)}}; = Latin _quinquagesima_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 55.31 (assuming variant): _metaphorically_, ταύτην ευὕρηκε Μειδίας καινὴν ἱππικήν τινα πεντηκοστήν}} invented a new sort of **composition of two per century.** in lieu of his cavalry service, i. e. paid this instead of it, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.166.}}
**II.2** (i.e. ἡμέρα), **fiftieth day** (after the Passover), {{LXX|“Pentecost”, LXX.2Mac.12.32}}; ἡ ἡμέρα τῆς Π. {{NT|NT.Act.2.1.}}

4007:
** περ**,
_enclitic_ Particle, adding force to the word to which it is added, probably a shortened form of περί (which see) in the sense of
**A** **very much, however much.**—Chiefly _Epic dialect_ and Lyric poetry; also in Trag. with relats. and parts.:
**1** in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus frequently with}} _adjective_ and _participle_ ὤν, ἐπεί μ᾽ ἔτεκές γε μινυνθάδιόν π. ἐόντα **all** shortlived as I am, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.352}}; φίλην π. ἐοῦσαν[{{Refs|[prev. work] 587; Ἰθάκης κραναῆς π. ἐούσης 3.201: mostly concessive like{καίπερ} (which see), ἀγαθός π. ἐών}}] **however** brave thou art, {{Refs|1.131, +others}}; κρατερός π. ἐών {{Refs|15.164; κύνεός π. ἐών 9.373}}; δουρικτητήν π. ἐοῦσαν{{Refs|[prev. work] 343; μέγαν π. ἐόντα 5.625}}: so in Trag., ἄελπτά π. ὄντα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 55 (Lyric poetry); γενναῖός π. ὤν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1068}}: with a _substantive_, ἀλόχῳ π. ἐούσῃ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.546; γυνή π. οὖσα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 1043}}: with _adjective_ and _substantive_, λιγύς π. ἐὼν ἀγορητής {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.246: with καί}} preceding, καὶ κρατερός π. ἐών {{Refs|15.195, etc.: with the}}_participle_ ὤν omitted, φράδμων π. ἀνήρ **however** shrewd,{{Refs|16.638; κρατερός π. 21.63; χερείονά π. 17.539}}; καὶ θεός π. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1203, compare 1084: with parts., ἱεμένων π}}. **however** eager, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.292}}; καὶ ἀχνύμενός π. ἑταίρου grieved **though** he was for.., {{Refs|8.125.}}
**2** _intensive_, ἐλεεινότερός π. more pitiable **by far**, {{Refs|24.504}}; μίνυνθά π. for a **very** little, {{Refs|1.416, 13.573; ὀλίγον π. 11.391}}; πρῶτόν π. first **of all**, {{Refs|14.295; ὑστάτιόν π. 8.353}}; ὀψέ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 3.80: to strengthen a negation, οὐδὲ.. π}}. not **even**, not **at all**, οὐδ᾽ ὑμῖν ποταμός π. ἐΰρροος ἀρκέσει {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.130, compare 8.201, 11.841, 21.410, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.59, 3.236; μή ποτε καὶ σὺ γυναικί π. ἤπιος εἶναι 11.441}}; ἢν μή π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.57.}}
**3** to give emphasis, ἀλλὰ καὶ αὐτοί π. πονεώμεθα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.70; ἡμεῖς δ᾽ αὐτοί π. φραζώμεθα 17.712; σθένος ἀνέρος ἀμφότεροί π. σχῶμεν 21.308}}: especially **at any rate**, τιμήν π. μοι ὄφελλεν ἐλλυαλίξαι honour (**whatever else**) he owed me, {{Refs|1.353, compare 2.236, 17.121, 239}}; τόδε π. μοι ἐπικρήηνον ἐέλδωρ {{Refs|8.242: in _imperative_ clauses with the _person_ _pronoun_, ἀλλὰ σύ π. μιν τῖσον}} **at all events**, {{Refs|1.508: in the apodosis of a conditional sentence, εἰ δέ τοι Ἀτρεΐδης μὲν ἀπήχθετο.., σὺ δ᾽ ἄλλους π... ἐλέαιρε 9.301, compare 11.796, 12.349.}}
**II** after Conjs. and _relative_ words, with which it commonly coalesces:
**II.1** after hypothetical Conjs., see at {εἴπερ}.
**II.2** after temporal Conjs., ὅτε π. **just** when, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.259, 5.802, etc.; ἦμος.. π. 11.86}}; ὅταν π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 301, etc.}}; πρίν π. before **even**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.588.}}
**II.3** after causal Conjs., see at {ἐπείπερ, ἐπειδήπε; δι᾽ ὅ τι π}. **just** because, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.186.}}
**II.4** after relats., see at {ὅσπερ, οἷός περ, ὅσοσπερ, ἔνθαπερ, ὅθιπερ, οὗπερ, ᾗπερ, ὥσπερ}.
**II.5** after the _comparative_ particle, see at {ἤπερ, ἠέπερ}.
**II.6** after καί, see at {καίπερ}.
**B περ** (B), _Aeolic dialect_ for περί.

4008:
** πέρᾱν**,
_Ionic dialect_ and _Epic dialect_ **πέρην**, _adverb_ **on the other side, across**, in early Poets always with _genitive_, especially of water, νήσων αἳ ναίουσι π. ἁλός {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.626; πέρνασχ᾽ ὅν τιν᾽ ἕλεσκε π. ἁλός 24.752 (never in 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey”); π. κλυτοῦ Ὠκεανοῖο 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 215}}; π. Χάεος ζοφεροῖο{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 814; π. πόντοιο 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 5.21; τὰ π. τοῦ Ἴστρου 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.9; πόντου π. τραφεῖσαν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1200; πολιοῦ π. πόντου 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 334 (Lyric poetry)}}; π. τοῦ Ἑλλησπόντου, τοῦ ποταμοῦ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.67, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.3.3}}; π. Ἕβρον is corrupt in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 386 (to be read Ἕβρου).}}
**2** _absolutely_, **on the other side**, especially of water, προσορμίζεσθαι.. π. ἐν τῇ Ῥηναίῃ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.97; π. εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.4.20, 3.5.12, etc.; π. γενέσθαι [prev. work] 6.5.22.}}
**3** with Verbs of motion, followed by εἰς, **over** or **across** to.., π. ἐς τὴν Ἀχαιίην διέπεμψαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.36; π. εἰς τὴν Ἀσίαν διαβῆναι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.2.2; διαπλεύσαντες π. τῆς Ἀκαρνανίας ἐς Οἰνιάδας 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.111}}; also without εἰς, ἐκ Θάσου διαβαλόντες π. having crossed **over** (i.e. ἐς τὴν ἤπειρον), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.44.}}
**4** frequently with _Article_, διαβιβάζεσθαι εἰς τὸ π. τοῦ ποταμοῦ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.5.2}}; διέβη εἰς τὸ π. {{Refs|[prev. author] “HG” 1.3.17}}; ἐν τῷ π. {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 4.3.11}}; τὰ π. things **done on the opposite side**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 4.3.24}}; τὰ π. πράγματα, opposed to τὰ ἐπὶ τάδε, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.97.5}}; οἱ π. those **on the other side**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Marius” 23}}; ἡ ὄχθη ἡ π. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 5.10.2.}}
**II** **over against, opposite**, with _genitive_, π. ἱερῆς Εὐβοίης {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.535: frequently in 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta, 2.22.2, 5.15.8, +others}}
**III** less frequently ={πέρα} (A), **beyond**, with _genitive_, π. Νείλοιο παγᾶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 6(5).23; π. γε πόντου καὶ τόπων Ἀτλαντικῶν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1053, cf. Alcaeus585 (Lyric poetry), “Supp.” 676.}}
**IV** **right through**, καῦσις [ἔστω] μὴ πέρην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Mochl.” 37}}; ἐς τὸ π. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Art.” 11.}}—π. with _genitive_ usually precedes its case, but follows it in A.[same place], {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 5.15.8. (Cf.πέρα (B).)}}

4009:
** πέρᾰς**,
_Epic dialect_ **πεῖραρ** (which see), ᾰτος, τό, **end, limit, boundary**,
**I** in local sense, ἐκ περάτων γᾶς {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 33.1, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.69; π... αὔλ[ε]ιος θύρα ἐλευθέρα γυναικὶ νενόμιστ᾽ οἰκίας 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 546}}; τὸ π. **tip**, τοῦ αἰδοίου {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 773a21}}; [τῆς ῥινός] {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18(2).28}}; τισὶ τὰ π. ἀλγέει the **extremities**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων χρονίων παθῶν” 1.7.}}
**II**generally, **limit**, either opposed to ἀρχή, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 264b27}}, or including it, τελευτή γε καὶ ἀρχὴ π. ἑκάστου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 137d, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 777b29, “Metaphorical” 1022a4; οὐκ ἔχων π. κακῶν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 1216, compare “Or.” 511, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 632, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.88, etc.; π. ἅπασιν ἀνθρώποις ἐστὶ τοῦ βίου θάνατος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.97, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1115a26; εἰ π. μηδὲν ἔσται σφίσι τοῦ ἀπαλλαγῆναι τοῦ κινδύνου 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.42}}; π. ἔχειν, ={περαίνεσθαι}, **come to an end**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.5, 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 60, etc.; π. λαμβάνειν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.31.2}}; ἐπιθεῖναι τῇ γενέσει π. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 776a4; ἐν π. εἶναι 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 5.18.2.}}
**II.2** **perfection** of a thing, τὸ π. τῆς μαγειρικῆς.. εὑρηκέναι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Hegesippus Comicus 1.4, compare 10, 3rd c.BC: Posidippus Comicus 26.17.}}
**II.2.b** Philos., τὸ τῶν ἀγαθῶν π., τὰ π. τῆς ἡδονῆς, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 3p.65U., “Sent.” 20; ἐν τῷ κατὰ φύσιν π. κατακέκλειται τἀγαθόν 4th c.AD: Metrodorus Epigrammaticus “Herc.” 831.8.}}
**II.3** **end, object**, εὐχῆς, ἐλπίδος, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Harm.” 2f}}
**II.4** Philos., **that which limits** or **has limits**, opposed to τὸ ἄπειρον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Pythagoras Philosophus cited in 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 986a23, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 30a, etc.; τὰ ἐλάχιστα καὶ ἀμιγῆ π. [τῆς ἀτόμου] 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.17U.: Comedy texts, of a person, τὸν καλούμενον Π. Philostephanus Comicus 1.3.}}
**III** **final decision**, [οἱ] τὸ π. ἔχοντες τῶν ἐν τῇ πόλει ἁπάντων δικαίων the supreme court, from which there is no appeal, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 3.16.}}
**IV** πέρας as _adverb_, **at length, at last**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.61, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.55.6, etc.; πέρας δ᾽ οὖν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 56.10}}; τὸ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 9.17, 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 261.13}}; but also, τὸ π. 'to cut a long story short', {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 70, 316, 470}}. [In _Aeolic dialect_ the first syllable is short in {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 33, long in 84.]}}

4012:
** περί**,
Thess., Delph. περ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 9(2).517.17 (3rd c.BC), +others, “Schwyzer” 323 A 4 (5th-4th c.BC)}}, also _Aeolic dialect_, see below {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus}} V; Elean παρ {{Refs|[prev. work]413.4}}: _preposition_ with _genitive_, _dative_, and _accusative_:—**round about, all round** (properly different from ἀμφί, **on both sides**). (Cogn. with Sanskrit _pári_ 'round about'.)
**A** WITH GENITIVE,
**A.I** of Place, sometimes in Poets, **round about, around**, τετάνυστο π. σπείους ἡμερίς [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.68; τείχη π. Δαρδανίας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 818 (Lyric poetry, assuming variant); εἴλυμα π. χροός 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 2.1129: rarely, like{ἀμφί}}}], **on both sides**, see at {περιβαίνω} {{Refs|1 near the end}}
**A.I.2** **about near**, π. σοῦ πάντα γένοιτο ῥόδα {{Refs|“IG” 14.2508 (from Nemausus).}}
**A.II** to denote the object **about** or **for** which one does something:
**A.II.1** with Verbs of fighting or contending, π. τινός **for** an object—from the notion of the thing's lying in the middle to be fought **about**, π. τῶνδε **for** these prizes, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.659; π. πτόλιος.. μαχήσεται 18.265; π. Πατρόκλοιο θανόντος [prev. work] 195, compare 17.120; π. σεῖο 3.137; π. νηὸς ἔχον πόνον 15.416; ἀμύνεσθαι π. πάτρης, π. νηῶν, π. τέκνων, 12.243, 142, 170, etc.}}; δόλους καὶ μῆτιν ὕφαινον, ὥς τε π. ψυχῆς since it was **for** my life, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.423; π. ψυχῆς θέον Ἕκτορος 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.161; π. ψυχέων ἐμάχοντο 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.245}}; in Prose, τρέχειν π. ἑωυτοῦ, π. τῆς ψυχῆς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.57, 9.37; ἀγῶνας δραμέονται π. σφέων αὐτῶν [prev. author] 8.102; νεναυμάχηκε τὴν π. τῶν κρεῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 191; [τὸν] π. τοῦ παντὸς δρόμον θέοντες 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.74; κινδυνεύειν π. τινός [prev. passage], etc.; οὐ π. τῶν ἴσων ὁ κίνδυνός ἐστι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.1.7}}; and without a Verb, π. γῆς ὅρων διαφοραί {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.122; π. πάντων ἀγαθῶν ὁ ἀγών 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.3.44, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 936 (Lyric poetry), etc.; μάχη π. τινός 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 179d; ἐπειγόμενοι π. νίκης 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.437, compare 639, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.26; πεῖραν θανάτου π. καὶ ζωᾶς ἀναβάλλεσθαι 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 9.29; π. θανάτου φεύγειν 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.95}}; but ἐρίσσαι π. μύθων contend **about** speaking, i. e. who can speak best, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.284; καὶ ἀθανάτοισιν ἐρίζεσκον π. τόξων 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.225, compare 24.515.}}
**A.II.2** with words which denote care or anxiety, **about, on account of**, π. Τρώων.. μερμηρίζειν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.17; ἄχος π. τινός 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.249; φόνου π. βουλεύειν 16.234; φροντίζειν π. τινός 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.36, etc.; κήδεσθαι π. τ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 621; δεδιέναι π. τ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 320a, etc.; ἀπολογεῖσθαι π. τ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.2.13}}; κρίνειν, διαγιγνώσκειν π. τ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 5.40, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.96}}; π. τ. ψηφίζεσθαι, διαψηφίζεσθαι, ψῆφον φέρειν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 12.57.42, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.3.50, 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 11; βουλεύεσθαι π. τῆς κοινῆς σωτηρίας 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.69; π. Μεθωναίων “IG” 12.57.49}}; διανοεῖσθαι, σκοπεῖν π. τινός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 270d, “Phd.” 65e; μαντεύεσθαι π. τ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.36, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 77}}; π. πότου γοῦν ἐστί σο; so with you it is a question **of** drink? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 87, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.43b.}}
**A.II.3** with Verbs of hearing, knowing, speaking, etc., **about, concerning**, π. νόστου ἄκουσα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.270; οἶδα γὰρ εὖ π. κείνου 17.563; π. πομπῆς μνησόμεθα 7.191; π. πατρὸς ἐρέσθαι 1.135, 3.77}}; π. τινὸς ἐρέειν, λέγειν, λόγον ποιήσασθαι, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.5, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 707, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.13, etc.; λέγειν καὶ ἀκούειν π. ἑκάστου 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.22, etc.; λόγος π. τινός 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 347b, etc.; ἡ π. τινὸς φήμη 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.48}}; π. τινὸς ἀγγεῖλαι, κηρῦξαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1111, “Ant.” 193}}; π. τινὸς διελθεῖν, διεξελθεῖν, διηγεῖσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.2, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 274b, “Euthphr.” 6c, etc.; παίζειν π. τινός 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.3.8; ἐμπειροτέρως ἔχειν π. τινός 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.82; νόμον γράψαι π. τινός 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.3.52, etc.; νόμῳ χρῆσθαι π. τινός 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 214.}}
**A.II.4** of impulse or motive rather than object, ἐμαρνάσθην ἔριδος πέρι fought **for very** enmity, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.301, compare 16.476, 20.253.}}
**A.II.5** **about, in regard to**, μεμηνυμένος π. τινός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.53; οὕτως ἔσχε π. τοῦ πρήγματος τούτου 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.117; τὰ π. τῶν Πλαταιῶν γεγενημένα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.6; τὸ π. τούτου γεγονός 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.54.5}}: in Prose frequently without a Verb, ἡ π. τῶν Μαντινικῶν πρᾶξις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.88}}; τὰ π. τινός **the circumstances** of.., {{Refs|[prev. work] 32, 8.14, 26, etc. (compare infr. C. 1.5)}}; οὕτω δὴ καὶ π. τῶν ἀρετῶν (i.e. ἔχει) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 72c, compare “R.” 534b, 551c, etc.}}; π. τοῦ καταλειφθῆναι τὸν σῖτον **as for** reserving the corn, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PMich.Zen.” 28.5 (3rd c.BC): without the _Article_, ἀριθμοῦ πέρι}} **as to** number, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.102}}; χρηστηρίων δὲ πέρι.. {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.54.}}
**A.III** **before, above, beyond**, of superiority, chiefly in _Epic dialect_, π. πάντων ἔμμεναι ἄλλων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.287; π. δ᾽ ἄλλων φασὶ γενέσθαι 4.375; τετιμῆσθαι π. πάντων 9.38; ὃν π. πάσης τῖεν ὁμηλικίης 5.325; ὃν.. π. πάντων φίλατο παίδων 20.304; π. πάντων ἴδριες ἀνδρῶν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.108; κρατερὸς π. πάντων 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.566, compare 1.417, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.216: in this sense frequently divided from its _genitive_, π. φρένας ἔμμεναι ἄλλων}} in understanding to be **beyond** them, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.171, compare 1.258, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.66; π. μὲν εἶδος, π. δ᾽ ἔργα τέτυκτο τῶν ἄλλων Δαναῶν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.279; π. μὲν κρατέεις, π. δ᾽ αἴσυλα ῥέζεις ἀνδρῶν 21.214; π. δ᾽ ἔγχει Ἀχαιῶν φέρτατός ἐσσι 7.289, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.50, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 25.119.}} —In this sense π. is sometimes adverbial, and the _genitive_ is absent, see below {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus II.}}
**A.IV** in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus and _Attic dialect_ Prose, to denote value, ἡμῖν π. πολλοῦ ἐστι}} it is **of** much **consequence, worth** much, to us, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1. 120, compare 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.3}}; π. πολλοῦ ποιεῖσθαί τινας to reckon them **for**, i.e. **worth**, much, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.73, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.3.10, etc.}}; π. πλείονος, π. πλείστου ποιεῖσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “An.” 7.7.44, “Cyr.” 7.5.60; π. πλείστου ἡγεῖσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.89; π. παντὸς ποιεῖσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.1}}; π. ἐλάττονος ἡγούμενοι, π. οὐδενὸς ἡγήσασθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.71, 31.31.}}
**A.V** _Aeolic dialect_ περί and περ ={ὑπέρ, στροῦθοι περὶ γᾶς.. δίννεντες πτέρα} {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 1.10}}; περ κεφάλας probably in {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 93, compare 18; περρ ἁπαλῶ στύματός σε πεδέρχομαι 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 29.25}}; also Hellenistic, ὃ διέγραψε Προῖτος περί μου paid **on** my **behalf, PCair. Zen.**{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|790.23 (3rd c.BC),compare “UPZ” 57.12 (2nd c.BC).}}
**B** WITH DATIVE (in _Attic dialect_ Prose mostly in _signification_ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|11, especially in 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus)}}
**B.I** of Place, **round about, around**, of close-fitting dresses, armour, etc., ἔνδυνε π. στήθεσσι χιτῶνα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.21; χιτῶνα π. χροῒ δῦνεν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.60; δύσετο τεύχεα καλὰ π. χροΐ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.241; ἕσσαντο π. χροῒ χαλκόν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.467; κνημῖδας.. π. κνήμῃσιν ἔθηκε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.17; βεβλήκει τελαμῶνα π. στήθεσσι 12.401: in Prose, π. τῇσι κεφαλῇσι εἶχον τιάρας 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.61; θώρακα π. τοῖς στέρνοις ἔχειν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.2.13}}; οἱ στρεπτοὶ οἱ π. τῇ δέρῃ καὶ τὰ ψέλια π. ταῖς χερσί{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1.3.2.; π. τῇ χειρὶ δακτύλιον ὄντα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 359e, etc.; χαλκὸς ἔλαμπε π. στήθεσσι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.245; χιτῶνα π. στήθεσσι δαΐξαι 2.416; πήληξ.. κονάβησε π. κροτάφοισι 15.648}}; in other relations, π. δ᾽ ἔγχεϊ χεῖρα καμεῖται will grow weary **by grasping** the spear,{{Refs|2.389; δράκων ἑλισσόμενος π. χειῇ 22.95; κνίση ἑλισσομένη π. καπνῷ 1.317; π. σταχύεσσιν ἐέρση 23.598; μάρναντο π. Σκαιῇσι πύλῃσιν 18.453}}: rarely in Trag., π. βρέτει πλεχθείς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 259 (Lyric poetry); κεῖται νεκρὸς π. νεκρῷ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1240.}}
**B.I.2** in Poets, also, **around** a weapon, i. e. **spitted upon** it, **transfixed by** it, π. δουρὶ πεπαρμένη {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.577; ἐρεικόμενος π. δουρί 13.441; κυλινδόμενος π. χαλκῷ 8.86; π. δουρὶ ἤσπαιρε 13.570; πεπτῶτα π. ξίφει 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 828; αἷμα ἐρωήσει π. δουρί 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.303.}}
**B.I.3** of a warrior **standing over** a dead comrade so as to defend him, ἀμφὶ δ᾽ ἄρ᾽ αὐτῷ βαῖν᾽, ὥς τις π. πόρτακι μήτηρ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|17.4; ἑστήκει, ὥς τίς τε λέων π. οἷσι τέκεσσι [prev. work] 133; Αἴας π. Πατρόκλῳ.. βεβήκει [prev. work] 137, compare 355; π. σκύμνοισι βεβηκώς 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1039.}}
**B.II** of an object **for** or **about** which one struggles (compare above {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 11.1), π. οἷσι μαχειόμενος κτεάτεσσι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.471; μαχήσασθαι π. δαιτί 2.245; π. παιδὶ μάχης πόνος 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.568; ἄνδρα π. ᾗ πατρίδι μαρνάμενον 7th c.BC: Tyrtaeus Elegiacus 10.2; π. τοῖς φιλτάτοις κυβεύειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 314a; π. τῇ Σικελίᾳ ἔσται ὁ ἀγών 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.34 codices; κινδυνεύειν π. αὑτῷ 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.6.}}
**B.II.2** with Verbs denoting care, anxiety, or the opposite (compare above {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 11.2), π. γὰρ δίε ποιμένι λαῶν, μή τι πάθοι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.566; ἔδεισεν δὲ π. ξανθῷ Μενελάῳ 10.240, compare 11.557; δεδιότες π. τῷ χωρίῳ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.60, compare 74, 119, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 27; θαρρεῖν π. τῇ ἑαυτοῦ ψυχῇ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 114d, compare “Tht.” 148c; π. πλέγματι γαθεῖ 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.54.}}
**B.II.3** generally, of the cause or occasion, **on account of, by reason of**, ἀτύζεσθαι π. καπνῷ, variant for{ὑπὸ καπνοῦ} in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.183; μὴ π. Μαρδονίῳ πταίσῃ ἡ Ἑλλάς 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.101; π. σφίσιν αὐτοῖς πταῖσαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.33; π. αὑτῷ σφαλῆναι [prev. author] 1.69: in Poets, π. δείματι}} **for** fear, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 5.58}}; π. τιμᾷ **in** honour or praise, {{Refs|[prev. work] 2.59}}; π. τάρβει, π. φόβῳ, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 696 (Lyric poetry), “Ch.” 35 (Lyric poetry); π. χάρματι “hymnus ad Cererem” 429:}} —but π. θυμῷ is _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.50.}}
**C** WITH ACCUSATIVE,
**C.I** of Place, properly of the object **round about** which motion takes place, π. βόθρον ἐφοίτων came flocking **round** the pit, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.42; π. νεκρὸν ἤλασαν ἵππους 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.13; π. τέρματα ἵπποι τρωχῶσι 22.162; ἄστυ πέρι.. διώκειν [prev. work] 173, 230}}; ἐρύσας π. σῆμα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|24.16, compare 51, etc.; π. φρένας ἤλυθ᾽ ἰωή 10.139; π. φρένας ἤλυθε οἶνος 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.362}}: also of extension **round**, ἑστάμεναι π. τοῖχον, π. βωμόν, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.374, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.187, etc.; λέξασθαι π. ἄστυ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.519; μάρνασθαι π. ἄ. 6.256, etc.; φυλάσσοντας π. μῆλα 12.303; οἳ π. Πηνειὸν.. ναίεσκον, π. Δωδώνην.. οἰκί᾽ ἔθεντο, 2.757, 750; σειρήν κεν π. ῥίον Οὐλύμποιο δησαίμην 8.25, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.67: in Prose, ἰκριῶσαι π. τὼ ἀγάλματε “IG” 12.371.22; φυλακὰς δεῖ π. τὸ στρατόπεδον εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.1.9}}; π. τὴν κρήνην εὕδειν **somewhere near** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 259a, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.2.9; εἶναι π. τὸν λαγώ [prev. author] “Cyn.” 4.4}}; π. λίθον πεσών **upon** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1180}}; π. αὑτὰ καταρρεῖν collapse **upon** themselves, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.10; ταραχθεῖσαι [αἱ νῆες] π. ἀλλήλας 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.23}}; πλεῦνες π. ἕνα many **to** one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.103}}; π. τὸν ἄρξαντα.. τὸ ἀδίκημά ἐστι is **imputable to** him who.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 4.4.2: frequently with a}} _substantive_ only, ἡ π. Λέσβον ναυμαχία the sea-fight **off** Lesbos, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.3.32; οἱ π. τὴν Ἔφεσον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 179e; στρατηγοὶ π. Πελοπόννησον “IG” 12.324.18: strengthened, π. τ᾽ ἀμφί τε τάφρον 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.760; π. τ᾽ ἀμφί τε κύματα 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 848; compare ἀμφί with 1.2.}}
**C.I.2** of persons who are **about** one, ἔχειν τινὰ π. αὑτόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.3.22}}; especially οἱ π. τινά a person's attendants, connexions, associates, or colleagues, οἱ π. τὸν Πείσανδρον πρέσβεις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.63}}; οἱ π. Ἡράκλειτον his school, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 440c, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.5.8, etc.}}; οἱ π. Ἀρχίαν πολέμαρχοι Archias **and his colleagues**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “HG” 5.4.2, compare “An.” 2.4.2, etc.}}; οἱ π. τινά so-and-so **and his family,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|PGrenf. 1.21.16 (2nd c.BC), etc.}}; later οἱ π. τινά, _periphrastic_ for the person himself, οἱ π. Φαβρίκιον Fabricius, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pyrrhus” 20, compare “Tim.” 13, “IGRom.” 3.883.14 (Tarsus, 2nd-3rd c.AD)}}; compare ἀμφί C. 1.3.
**C.I.3** of the object **about** which one is occupied or concerned, π. δόρπα πονεῖσθαι, π. δεῖπνον πένεσθαι, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.444, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.624 (but π. τεύχε᾽ ἕπουσι, tmesis for περιέπουσι, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.555)}}; later mostly εἶναι π. τι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.31, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.2.4; γενέσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.12}}; π. γυναῖκας γενέσθαι Vett.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|Val. 17.20; ὄντων ἡμῶν π. ταύτην τὴν πραγματείαν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 48.6; διατρῖψαι π. τὴν θήραν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.2.11, etc.: less frequently ἔχειν π. τινάς [prev. author] “HG” 7.4.28, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.442}}; in _periphrastic_ phrases, οἱ π. τὴν ποίησιν καὶ τοὺς λόγους ὄντες poets and orators, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.35; οἱ π. τὴν φιλοσοφίαν ὄντες [prev. author] 9.8; οἱ π. τὴν μουσικήν [prev. work]4; οἱ π. τὰς τελετάς}} ministers of the mysteries, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 69c}}; ὁ π. τὸν ἵππον the groom, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 6.3; compare ἀμφί C. 1.6.}}
**C.I.4** **round** or **about** a place, and so **in**, π. νῆσον ἀλώμενοι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.368, compare 90; ἐμέμηκον π. σηκούς 9.439}}; ἃν π. ψυχὰν γάθησεν **in** his heart, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.122; χρονίζειν π. Αἴγυπτον 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.61, compare 7.131; εὕροι ἄν τις [βασιλείας] π. τοὺς βαρβάρους 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 544d, etc.}}; οἱ π. Φωκίδα τόποι {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.24.12, etc.}}
**C.I.5** **about, in the case of**, τὰ π. τὴν Αἴγυπτον γεγονότα, τὰ π. Μίλητον γενόμενα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.13, 6.26; εὐσεβεῖν π. θεούς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 193a; ἀσεβεῖν π. ξένους 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.2.10; ἁμαρτάνειν π. τινάς [prev. author] “An.” 3.2.20; ἀνήρ ἐστιν ἀγαθὸς π. τὸν δῆμον τὸν Ἀθηναίων “IG” 12.59.10; ἄνδρ᾽ ἀγαθὸν ὄντα Μαραθῶνι π. τὴν πόλιν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 696; τοιαύτην γνώμην ἔχειν π. τὸν πατέρα 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 10.21; οὐδεμία συμφορὴ.. ἔσται.. π. οἶκον τὸν σόν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.102}}; ποιέειν or πράττειν τι π. τινά, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.158, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 507a; τὰ π. Πρηξάσπεα πρηχθέντα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.76; καινοτομεῖν π. τὰ θεῖα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 3b; π. θεοὺς μὴ σωφρονεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.1.20}}; σπουδάζειν π. τινά promote his cause, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.10}}: without a Verb, αἱ π. τοὺς παῖδας συμφοραί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.2.20; ἡ π. αὑτὸν ἐπιμέλεια 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.2; ἡ π. ἡμᾶς ἡνιόχησις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 246b}}: generally, of all relations, **about, concerning, in respect of**, π. μὲν τοὺς ἰχθύας οὕτως ἔχει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.93, compare 8.86; πονηρὸν π. τὸ σῶμα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 313d; ἀκόλαστος π. ταῦτα 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.42; γελοῖος π. τὰς διατριβάς [prev. work]126; ξυνηνέχθη θόρυβος π. τὸν Ἀστύοχον 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.84}}; **as to** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare (4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 11.5), π. τὸ παρὸν πάθος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 179c, compare “Phd.” 65a: frequently in place of an}} _adjective_, ὄργανα ὅσα π. γεωργίαν, i.e. γεωργικά{{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 370d; οἱ νόμοι οἱ π. τοὺς γάμους [prev. author] “Cri.” 50d; αἱ π. τὰ μαθήματα ἡδοναί [prev. author] “Phlb.” 51e}}; also in place of a _genitive_, οἱ π. Αυσίαν λόγοι the speeches **of** L., {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phdr.” 279a}}; ἡ π. Φίλιππον τυραννίς the despotism **of** P., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.4.2; ἀκρασίας τῆς π. τὸν θυμόν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1149b19}}: in Prose, to denote circumstances **connected with** any person or thing, τὰ π. Κῦρον, τὰ π. Ἑλένην, τὰ π. Βάττον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.95, 2.113, 4.154}}; τὰ π. τὸν Ἄθων the **works at** Mount Athos, {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.37}}; τὰ π. τὰς ναῦς naval **affairs**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.13}}; τὰ π. τὴν ναυμαχίαν (variant for{τῆς ναυμαχίας}) the **events of**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 8.63; τὰ π. τὸν πόλεμον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 468a; τὰ π. τὸ σῶμα [prev. author] “Phdr.” 246d; τὰ π. τοὺς θεούς 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.1.23, etc.; compare ἀμφί with 1.4.}}
**C.II** of Time, π. λύχνων ἁφάς **about** the time of lamp-lighting, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.215}}; π. μέσας νύκτας **about** midnight, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.7.1; π. πλήθουσαν ἀγοράν [prev. work] 2.1.7}}; π. ἡλίου δυσμάς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 6.5.32; π. τούτους τοὺς χρόνους 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.89, etc.}}
**C.II.2** of round numbers, π. ἑβδομήκοντα **about** seventy, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.54; π. ἑπτακοσίους 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.4.5, etc.}}
**D** Position: π. may follow its _substantive_, when it suffers anastrophe, ἄστυ πέρι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.173; ἔριδος πέρι 16.476: most frequently with _genitive_, τοῦδε πράγματος πέρι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 630; τῶνδε βουλεύειν πέρι [prev. author] “Th.” 248, etc. (5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}} only once uses it **before** its _genitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Aj.” 150 (_anapaest meter_)): in Prose, σφέων αὐτῶν πέρι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.36; σοφίας πέρι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 49a; δικαίων τε πέρι καὶ ἀδίκων [prev. author] “Grg.” 455a, etc.; γραμμάτων εἴπομεν ὡς οὐχ ἱκανῶς ἔχεις πέρι [prev. author] “Lg.” 809e, compare “Ap.” 19c.}}
**E** περί _absolutely_, as _adverb_., **around, about**, also, **near, by**, frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, γέλασσε δὲ πᾶσα π. χθών 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.362, +others: strengthened, περί τ᾽ ἀμφί τε κάλλος ἄητο}}**round about,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|Hymnus in Cererem 276, compare 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hecale” 1.1.13.}}
**E.II** **before** or **above others** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|compare (4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 111)}}, **exceedingly**, only _Epic dialect_, in which case it commonly suffers anastrophe, Τυδεΐδη, πέρι μέν σε τίον Δαναοί {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.161, compare 9.53}}; σε χρὴ πέρι μὲν φάσθαι ἔπος ἠδ᾽ ἐπακοῦσαι{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 100; τοι πέρι δῶκε θεὸς πολεμήϊα ἔργα 13.727; ἅ οἱ πέρι δῶκεν Ἀθήνη 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.116, compare 7.110; πέρι γάρ μιν ὀϊζυρὸν τέκε μήτηρ 3.95; πέρι κέρδεα οἶδεν 2.88; τὸν πέρι Μοῦσ᾽ ἐφίλησεν 8.63.}}
**E.II.2** π. does not suffer anastrophe in the _Epic dialect_ phrase π. κῆρι **right heartily**, π. κῆρι φίλησε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.430, etc. (κῆρι φιλεῖν alone, 9.117); ἀπέχθωνται π. κῆρι 4.53}}; π. κῆρι τιέσκετο{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 46, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.36, 7.69; π. κῆρι.. ἐχολώθη 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.206; also π. φρεσὶν ἄσπετος ἀλκή 16.157; π. φρεσὶν αἴσιμα ᾔδη 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.433; ἀλύσσοντες π. θυμῷ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.70, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.146; π. σθένεϊ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.22.}}
**E.II.3** for περὶ πρό, see at {περιπρό}.
**E.II.4** περὶ κάτω bottom **upwards**, δῖνος π. κάτω τετραμμένος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Strattis Comicus 34, compare 9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc.; τὴν κόγχην στρέψας π. τὰ κάτω 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 9.34.}}
**F** IN COMPOSITION (joined with other words), all its chief senses recur, especially
**F.I** extension in all directions as from a centre, **all round**, as in περιβάλλω, περιβλέπω, περιέχω.
**F.II** completion of an orbit and return to the same point, **about**, as in περιάγω, περιβαίνω, περίειμι (εἶμι **ibo**), περιέρχομαι, περιστρέφω.
**F.III** a going over or beyond, **above, before**, as in περιβαίνω III, περιβάλλω see, περιγίγνομαι, περιεργάζομαι, περιτοξεύω.
**F.IV** generally, a strengthening of the simple notion, **beyond measure, very, exceedingly**, as in περικαλλής, περίκηλος, περιδείδω, like Latin _per_-.
**F.V** the notion of **double**-ness which belongs to ἀμφί is found in only one poetic _compound_, περιδέξιος (which see).
**G** PROSODY: περί never suffers elision in [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” or 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” (περ᾽ ἰγνύσι “hymnus ad Mercurium” 152); once in 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus, περίαχε “Th.” 678 (compare 4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 3.601, 11.382), see.{ἰάχω}}}] near the end; περ᾽ ἰγνύῃσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 25.242; περ᾽ Ἠδάλιον “Inscription Cyprian dialect” 135.27 H.; also in 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus, περάπτων “P.” 3.52; περόδοις “N.” 11.40; περιδαῖος “fragment” 154; περ᾽ αὐτᾶς “P.” 4.265; ταύτας περ᾽ ἀτλάτου πάθας “O.” 6.38: not in Trag. (περεβάλοντο, περεσκήνωσεν are ff. lines in 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1147, “Eu.” 634)}}; in Comedy texts and codices of Prose writers only in _participle_ of περίειμι (εἶμι **ibo**) (which see):—π. stands before a word beginning with a vowel in Comedy texts, περὶ Ἀθηνῶν, περὶ ἐμοῦ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1005}} {περιαγαπάζομαι}:—_Aeolic dialect_ περρ for the sake of meter, see {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 5.}}

4013:
** περιάγω** [ᾰ],
_future_ -άξω {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 532.13, etc.}}:— **lead** or **draw round**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.30, +others; τὰ φορτία ἐν βάρισι περὶ τὸ Δέλτα [prev. author] 2.179; π. τινὰς ἐν ἁμάξῃσι κειμένους [prev. author] 4.73: with _accusative_ loci, περιάγουσι τὴν λίμνην κύκλῳ (i.e. τὴν παρθένον) [prev. work] 180 (assuming variant),compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus}} [same place]; **carry about** for sale, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 313d}}:—_middle_, **lead round with one**, ἐλέφαντα {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Epinicus Comicus 2.4.}}
**b** **cause to revolve**, ψυχὴ π. πάντα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 898d, compare 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 5.1.2}}:—_passive_, **rotate**, οἷον τροχοῦ περιαγομένου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 79b.}}
**2** **lead about with one, have always by one**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.2.28, compare 1.3.3}}:—more frequently in _middle_, ἀκολούθους πολλοὺς περιάγεσθαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Mem.” 1.7.2, compare 4th c.BC: Theopompus Historicus 89 (a), 2nd-1st c.BC: Posidonius Historicus 7 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus, etc.}}
**2.b** _metaphorically_, **lead round and round, perplex**, τὼ θεώ με περιάγουσιν, ὥστε.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.113 (assuming variant), compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nigr.” 8}}:—_passive_, περιαγόμενος τῷ λόγῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 187e.}}
**3** **turn round, turn about**, τὴν κεφαλήν, τὸν τράχηλον, τὸν αὐχένα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 682, “Av.” 176, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 515c, compare 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 18; τινὰ πρὸς τἀριστερά 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 686 (assuming variant); μύλην 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 7.180}}; π. τὴν σκυταλίδα **twist** it **round** in order to tighten a noose, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.60}}; τὼ χεῖρε περιαγαγὼν εἰς τοὔπισθεν καὶ δήσας **twisting back** the hands **behind the back**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.25; simply π. τὼ χεῖρε 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 6.82}}:—_passive_, περιαχθεὶς τὼ χεῖρε {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Heroicus” 10.7}}; so probably περιαχθείς alone, π. κρεμήσεται {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 202.9 (3rd c.BC).}}
**4** **pass round**, τὸ περιαγόμενον ποτήριον {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 10.420a, etc., compare 3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 3.11.}}
**5** **protract**, ἐς ὥραν τινά {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Merc. Cond.” 31.}}
**6** **bring round** to.., [τὴν πολιτείαν] πάλιν εἰς τὴν ἑτέραν πολιτείαν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1265a4; εἰς αὑτὸν τὴν ἀρχήν 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 4.3.1}}:—_passive_, π. εἰς ὁμόνοιαν {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.15.7}}; εἰς τόδε, εἰς ἀνάγκην, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nigr.” 5, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 5.2.8.}}
**7** Rhet., **round** a period, etc., περίοδος, σύνθεσις περιηγμένη, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 19, 30.}}
**II** _intransitive_, **come round**, πάλιν κύκλῳ π. εἰς τὴν ἀρχήν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 356a8; περιφερομένης καὶ περιαγούσης 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “De rerum natura - Wien. Stud.” 11.2.}}
**II.2** with _accusative_ loci, **go round**, π. τὴν ἐσχατιάν {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 42.5; π. τὰς πόλεις NT.Matt.9.35, compare 4.23, etc.}}

4014:
** περιαιρ-έω**,
_aorist_ περιεῖλον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.159, etc.}}:—**take away something that surrounds, strip off, remove**, with _accusative_ _of things_, τεῖχος Hdt.[same place],{{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare 6.46, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.108, 4.51, 133}}; π. τὸν κέραμον **taking off** the earthen jar into which the gold had been run, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.96; π. τὸν χιτῶνα 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 557b20}}; δέρματα σωμάτων π. **strip** skins **off from**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 288e; αὐτοῦ τὰ κοινὰ πάντα περιελόντες [prev. author] “Sph.” 264e; π. τινὰ αὐτοῦ τῆς ἐξουσίας 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 3.11.3}}; simply, **take away from**, τῶν πολεμικῶν τὸ μελετᾶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.1.21, etc.}}:—_middle_, **take off from oneself**, π. τὴν κυνέην, τὴν σφρηγῖδα, **take off one's** helmet, **one's** signet-ring, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.151, 3.41; τὰς ταινίας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 213a}}; βυβλίον περιαιρεόμενος **taking [the cover] off one's** letter, i. e. **opening** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.128; π. τὴν ἐξουσίαν τῆς ἀπολογίας αὑτοῦ 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 35}}: but _middle_ is frequently used like _active_, **strip off, take away**, τὸ περιελέσθαι αὐτῶν τὰ ὅπλα [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.1.47; εἴ τις περιελοιτο τῆς ποιήσεως τὸ μέλος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 502c (variant for{περιέλοι}) τὴν Ἀττικὴν ὑμῶν περιῄρηνται 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.220; ἁπάντων τὴν ἐλευθερίαν περιείλετο [prev. author] 18.65; περιείλοντό μου ὑποζύγια δύο “PCair.Zen.” 659.7 (3rd c.BC)}}]:—_passive_, **to be taken off**, τοὐπίβλημ᾽ ἐπεὶ περιῃρέθη {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Nicostratus Comicus 15}}; τοῦ ἄλλου περιῃρημένου **when** the rest **has been taken away**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.11; περιῃρημένων τοσούτων κακῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 231b; τείχη περιῃρημένα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.65.}}
**2** **make void, cancel** a vow, {{LXX|LXX.Num.30.13.}}
**3** **strike off, cancel** an item in an account, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 147 (3rd c.BC)}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“Sammelb.” 5136.8 (3rd c.AD).}}
**II** _passive_, with _accusative_ _of things_, **to be stripped of** a thing, **have** a thing **taken off** or **away from** one, περιῃρημένοι χρήματα καὶ συμμάχους {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.31; περιαιρεθεὶς τὰ ὄντα [prev. author] 21.138; τοὺς στεφάνους περιῄρηνται [prev. author] 26.5: with _accusative_ understood, περιαιρεθήσεσθαι ἤμελλον 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “De rerum natura - Wien. Stud.” 15.34.}}

4015:
** περιαστράπτω**,
**flash around**, φῶς π. τινά {{NT+4th c.AD+|NT.Act.9.3, compare 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 4.131a; περί τινα NT.Act.22.6: _absolutely_, ἄγγελοι περιαστράπτοντες τοῖς ὅπλοις LXX.4Mac.4.10.}}
**2** **dazzle**, ὁ ἀνὴρ περιαστράπτεται ὑπὸ κάλλους **is dazzled** with beauty, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Juncus Philosophus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 4.50.95, compare 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 19.220.}}

4016:
** περιβάλλω**,
_future_ -βᾰλῶ: _aorist_ περιέβᾰλον (see. below):— **throw round, about**, or **over, put on** or **over**, with _accusative_ _of things_, φίλας περὶ χεῖρε βαλόντε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.211; περὶ πτερὰ πυκνὰ βαλόντες 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.454; περὶ δ᾽ ἄντυγα βάλλε φαεινήν 18.479; π. χέρας 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 914, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1044: frequently with _dative_, χέρας π. τινί [prev. author] “Ph.” 1459, etc.; περὶ δ᾽ ὠλένας δέρᾳ.. βάλοιμι [prev. work] 165 (Lyric poetry)}}; π. τινὶ δεσμά, βρόχους, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 52, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 619; Τροίᾳ ζευκτήριον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 529; κρατὶ π. σκότον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1159}}; π. τινὰ χαλκεύματι **put** him **round** the sword, i. e. stab him, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 576}}; also περὶ τὰ στέρνα θώρηκας π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.215, compare 5.85; αἱμασιὴν π. κατὰ τὸν κύκλον [prev. author] 7.60}}; περὶ ἕρμα π. ναῦν **wreck** it **on**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.25}}:—_middle_, **throw round** or **over oneself, put on**, with _accusative_ _of things_, περιβαλλόμενοι τεύχεα **putting on their** arms, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.148; περὶ δὲ ζώνην βάλετ᾽ ἰξυῖ 5.231; ξίφος περὶ στιβαροῖς βάλετ᾽ ὤμοις 14.528}}; εἷμα, φᾶρος περιβάλλεσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.152, 9.109; φάρεα καὶ πλοκάμους 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1150 (Lyric poetry); κόσμον σώμασιν [prev. author] “HF” 334; κύκλον ὅσον περιβάλλεται αἰθήρ 4th-3rd c.BC: Hermesianax Elegiacus 7.87}}; frequently of defences, τεῖχος καὶ σωτηρίην περιβαλέσθαι τοῖς τε χρήμασι καὶ τοῖς σώμασιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 280; also ὅταν περιβάλωνται χειρις μοὺς παραλλάττοντας 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.8 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus; π. ἕρκος ἔρυμα τῶν νεῶν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.96; τείχεα [prev. author] 1.141, compare 6.46, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.8; ταῖς πόλεσιν ἐρύματα περιβάλλονται 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.14; Πελοποννήσῳ π. ἓν τεῖχος 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1276a27}}; λιμένι τεῖχος, χάρακα τῇ παρεμβολῇ, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.65.11, 5.20.5}}; also περὶ τὴν Πελοπόννησον τεῖχος π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.45: with double _accusative_, τεῖχος περιβαλέσθαι πόλιν}} **build** a wall **round** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.163}}: in _perfect_ _passive_, **have** a thing **put round one**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 216d}}; τὸ τεῖχος περιβεβλημένος **having** his wall **around** him, **encompassed by** it, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Tht.” 174e, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1331a8.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, **put round** or **upon** a person, i. e. **invest** him **with** it, π. τινὶ ἀγαθόν (i. e. βασιληΐην), τυραννίδα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.129, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 829; π. σωτηρίαν [τισί] [prev. author] “HF” 304; ὕδασι δουλείαν [prev. author] “Ph.” 189 (Lyric poetry); οἶκτον [prev. author] “IA” 934}}; τινὶ π. ἀνανδρίαν, i.e. make him faint-hearted, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Or.” 1031}}; π. τὴν αἰτίαν τῷ ἰατρῷ **impute** blame to.., {{Refs|Palladius Medicus “in Hippocratem” 12.283}} C.:—_passive_, with _accusative_, **to be involved in**, μεγίστην ζημίαν τὸ ταμεῖον περιβληθήσεται {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“SIG” 888.87 (Scaptopara, 3rd c.AD).}}
**II** reversely with _dative_ _of things_, **surround, encompass with**.., περιβαλεῖν πλῆθος τῶν ἰχθύων (i.e. τῷ ἀμφιβλήστρῳ) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.141; βρόχῳ π. τὸν αὐχένα [prev. author] 4.60 (tmesis)}}; [Βόσπορον] πέδαις π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 748; π. τινὰ ὑφάσματι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 25; δοραῖσι σῶμα [prev. author] “Cyc.” 330}}; π. τινὰ χερσί embrace, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Or.” 372}}:—_middle_, **surround** or **enclose for one's advantage** or **defence**, τὴν νῆσον π. τείχει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 116a; χωρίον 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.3.30}}; π. θύννους **net** them, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 537a20, compare 533b25.}}
**II.2** _metaphorically_, π. τινὰ κακῷ, συμφοραῖς, **involve** one in evil or calamity, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 906, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.2.12; ἀνηκέστοις πόλιν συμφοραῖς 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.142, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 4.20; ὀνείδει 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 22.35}}; π. τινὰ φυγῇ, i.e. banish him, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.775c; τινὰ κλοπῆς καταδίκῃ [prev. author] “Arist.” 4:}} — _passive_, [συμφοραῖς] {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “de Pietate - Herkulanische Studien” 35b.}}
**III** with _accusative_ only, **encompass, surround**, περιβάλλει με σκότος, νέφος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1453, “HF” 1140}}; π. ἀλλήλους **embrace** each other, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.7.25, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Περικειρομένη” 36, 111}}; also, **clothe**, τινα {{NT|NT.Matt.25.36}}; τὸ περιβεβλημένον **the space enclosed, enclosure**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.91; compare περίβολος 11.2:}} —_middle_, ἤλαυνον περιβαλόμενοι [τὰ ὑποζύγια] **surrounding** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 9.39, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.17.}}
**III.2** **fetch a compass round, double**, ἵπποι περὶ τέρμα βαλοῦσαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.462}}; especially of ships, **round** a cape, π. τὸν Ἄθων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.44; Σούνιον 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.95}}: _absolutely_, of a hare, **double**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 5.29, 6.18.}}
**III.3** **amplify, expand**, λόγον {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “περὶ ἰδεῶν” 1.4, compare 11: _absolutely_, [prev. work] 3, +others}}
**IV** _middle_, **bring into one's power, compass**, ἰδίῃ π. ἑωυτῷ κέρδεα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.71; πολλὰ [χρήματα] [prev. author] 8.8, compare 7.190}}; σωφροσύνης δόξαν π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.2.6; τὰ λοιπὰ τῶν πραγμάτων περιβαλλόμενος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.231; πλῆθος λείας 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.29.7, compare 3.69.7}}: _perfect_ _passive_, **to have come into possession of**.., πόλιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.24; δυναστείας 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.184, compare 2.25.}}
**IV.2** **appropriate mentally, comprehend**, περιβάλλεσθαι τῇ διανοίᾳ τὰς πράξεις {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.118}}; πολλὰ περιβεβλῆσθαι πράγματα **to have aimed at learning** many things, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 683}}; logically, ξύμπαντα τὰ οἰκεῖα.. γένους τινὸς οὐσίᾳ π. **embrace**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 285b.}}
**IV.3** **use circumlocution**, κομψῶς κύκλῳ π. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Smp.” 222c, compare “Phdr.” 272d.}}
**V** **throw beyond, beat in throwing**: hence generally, **excel, surpass**, μνηστῆρας δώροισι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.17}}; π. ἀρετῇ **to be superior** in.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.276.}}
**VI** π. τὸ λουτρόν **take** a bath, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Cassius Medicus “Physici et Medici Graeci Minores” 5; π. πρὸς λουτρόν [prev. passage]}}

4017:
** περι-βλέπω**,
**look round about, gaze around**, περιβλέψας ἔφη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 403; πρὸς τοὺς παρόντας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Eryxias” 395c; μηδαμοῖ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 3.4; πάντῃ 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Sacr.” 9, etc.}}:— _middle_, **look about one**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cat.Mi.” 37, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 3.14.3}}; περιεβλέποντο ζητοῦντες.. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 16.32.}}
**II** _transitive_, **look round at**, πάσας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.1.4:}} —_middle_, ἀλλότρια ἡγεμονικὰ π. {{NT+2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 7.55, cf. NT.Mark.3.5.}}
**II.2** **seek after, covet for oneself**, ἀρχήν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 3.7.}}
**II.3** **look about for**, τινα {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Vit.Auct.” 12; τόπον εὐφυῆ 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.20.5}}:—_middle_, {{LXX|[prev. author] 9.17.6, LXX.Tob.11.5.}}
**II.4** **admire, respect**, τοὔνδικον π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 996 (unless in signification 11.3)}}:—_passive_, περιβλέπεσθαι τίμιον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 551, compare 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Heroicus” 15.}}
**II.5** _middle_, **look up, consult**, βίβλους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd-4th c.AD: Zosimus Alchemista p.138 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}}

4018:
** περιβόλ-αιον**, τό,
(περιβάλλω) **that which is thrown round, covering**, θανάτου περιβόλαια corpse-**clothes**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 549}}; σαρκὸς π. ἡβῶντα youthful **incasements** of flesh, i.e. youth, manhood,{{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. work] 1269, compare “PStrassb.” 91.9 (1st c.BC)}}; frequently in {{LXX|LXX, LXX.Deu.22.12, +others (_metaphorically_, π. χαλκοῦν ἡ ἰσχύς σου LXX.Jer.15.12)}}; woman's **headgear**, {{NT|NT.1Cor.11.15}}; **covering** for the feet, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Aratus” 43}}; **chariot-cover**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Alex.” 67}}; **bedcover**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18(1).103}}; **padding**, περιβόλαια σπληνῶν {{Refs|[prev. work](2)}}.381; **dressing-gown, warm wrap**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Herodotus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 10.18.13, 2nd c.AD: Archigenes Medicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 13.264.}}
**II** ={περίβολος} 11.2, {{Refs|“CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 4590 (from Palestine).}}

4019:
** περιδέω**,
**bind, tie round** or **on**, τινί τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.193, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 127}}:— _middle_, **bind round oneself**, περισφύριον περιδέεται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.176; τὴν ᾤαν π. περὶ τὴν ὀσφύν 5th c.BC: Hermippus Comicus 53}}; λόφον, πώγωνα, στεφάνους π., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1038, “Ec.” 100, 122}}; of pugilists, ἐπισφαίροις π. τὰς χεῖρας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.825e; ἀντὶ ἱμάντων σφαίρας ἂν περιεδούμεθα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 830b.}}
**2** **bind round, bandage** with, ἱμᾶσι πόδα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 13; ἀραχνίοις 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 623a14.}}

4020:
** περιεργ-άζομαι**,
_future_ -εργάσομαι, περίεργος) **take more pains than enough about** a thing, **waste one's labour on** it, with _participle_, περιεργάζοντο δοκέοντες πρῶτοι ἀνθρώπων γεγονέναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.15; Σωκράτης π. ζητῶν τά τε ὑπὸ γῆς καὶ οὐράνια 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 19b; περιείργασμαι μὲν ἐγὼ περὶ τούτων εἰπὼν περιείργασται δ᾽ ἡ πόλις ἡ πεισθεῖσ᾽ ἐμοί 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.72: with _dative_ modi, τῷ θυλάκῳ περιερλάσθαι}} **that they had overdone it** with their 'sack' (i.e. need not have used the word), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.46}}; π. τοῖς σημείοις **overact one's part**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1462a6}}; π. τῷ οἰκιδίῳ **go to a needless expense** with his house, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 4.11}}; οὐδὲ περιείργασται ἐν αὐτοῖς nor has he **lavished useless pains** upon.., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Herod.” 6}} (but _perfect_ in _passive_ sense, πλέον οὐδὲν περιείρλασται τῷ Θέωνι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 2.44).}}
**2** with _accusative_, π. τι καινόν **to be busy about** 'some new thing', {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 220; αἱ μέλιτται π. τὸ παιδίον 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Philostrati majoris imagines” 2.12}}; **meddle, interfere with**, τὰ ἀλλότρια Chiloap.{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 3.1.172; τῶν κατὰ τὴν Ἰταλίαν οὐδέν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 18.51.2}}: _absolutely_, **to be a busybody**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 26.15, 32.28, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 358, 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Epistulae” 1068.3.}}
**3** **bargain, haggle**, περὶ τῆς τιμῆς {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 393.5 (3rd c.BC).}}
**4** in good sense, **elaborate**,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|Men.Rh. p.394 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, +others}}
**5** **investigate thoroughly**, τὰ λεληθότα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 7.217c, compare 4th-5th c.AD: Eunapius Historicus “Fragmenta Historica - HGM” p.250 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}; **seek diligently**, π. πόθεν ἡ εἴσοδος Zos.Alch.p.111 {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}}
**6** ταῦτα π. **have** this **effect**, of substances, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18(1).484.}}

4021:
** περίεργος**, ον,
**taking needless trouble**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.35; γραμματικῶν π. γένη “Anthologia Graeca” 11.322 (1st c.AD: Antiphanes Macedo Epigrammaticus)}}. _adverb_ -γως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ εὐσχημοσύνης” 7.}}
**2** **officious, meddlesome**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.98, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.3.1, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Σαμία” 85}}; π. εἰμι I am **a busy-body**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Epit.” 45}}; περίεργα βλέπειν look **curiously** at, with _accusative_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 12.175 (2nd c.AD: Strato Epigrammaticus), compare 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 5.3.8 (_comparative_).}}
**3** **of an inquiring mind**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Respiratione” 480b27}}; **inquisitive, curious**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 4.12.3 (_superlative_)}}; π. παιδία {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.635}}; τὸ π. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Alex.” 4}}. _adverb_ -γως, ἔχειν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Astrampsychus Onirocriticus “Oracula” p.1}} H.: _comparative_ -ότερον, ἔχειν πρός τινα {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 4.130d.}}
**II** _passive_, **overwrought, elaborate**, ὀδμή (perfume) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “παραγγελίαι” 10; φορήματα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 321; ζωγράφημα 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.64a}}; τὸ τῆς κόμης π. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nigr.” 13}}; especially of language or style, ὀνόματα, λόγοι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.229, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Lysia” 14; τὸ π. Θουκυδίδου [prev. author] “Vett.Cens.” 3.2}}: _comparative_, - οτέρα λέξις {{Refs|[prev. author] “Is.” 3}}. _adverb_ **-γως**{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Antyllus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 9.14.7}}: _comparative_ -ότερον, ἠσκημένος τὴν κόμην {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 3.1.1; ἐξορχεῖσθαι 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 5.5.3.}}
**II.2** **superfluous**, π. καὶ μακρὰ λέγειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 286c}}; ὅπως εἴ τις π. ἀφαιρεθῇ (i.e. δαπάνη) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1359b27; π. ἐστί τι 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 3.33, compare 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 1.31; π. [ἐστι] τὸ λέγειν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1315a40, compare “Rh.” 1369a8}}; **futile, useless**, πόλεμος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.117}}. _adverb_ **-γως**{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Timocles Comicus 13.4, etc.}}
**II.3** **curious, superstitious**, ἱερουργίαι {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alexander” 2}}; τὰ π. **curious arts, magic,** {{NT|NT.Acts.19.19.}}

4022:
** περιέρχομαι**,
_imperfect_ περιηρχόμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 504}} codex:—**go round, go about**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.36, etc.; πάντοθεν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.225; κατὰ τὴν ἀγοράν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 558; κατὰ πᾶσαν χώραν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.139; ἐν κύκλῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 283b}}; **go about** like a beggar, πρὸς τοὺς φίλους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.2.16}}; like a stranger seeing sights, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Oec.” 10.10}}: with _participle_, **go about** doing a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 504 codex, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 30a, 5th c.BC: Critias Philosophus, Tragicus, Elegiacus 37 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 13.19: with _accusative_ _cognate_, π. στάδια χίλια 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 6; π. ἀπέραντον ὁδόν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 147c; δὔ ἢ τρεῖς δρόμους [prev. author] “Euthd.” 273a}}; [πρεσβείας καὶ κατηγορίας] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.72; ποικίλως τὸν βίον π. Vett. Val. 40.28: with _accusative_ loci, π. [τὸν βωμόν] 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 958; βωμοὺς ἅπαντας ἐν κύκλῳ [prev. author] “Pl.” 679; τὴν πόλιν 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.99; τὴν ἀλορὰν κύκλῳ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.225}}; τὴν χώραν π. **survey** it, {{Refs|[prev. author] 18.150.}}
**2** with _accusative_ _person_, in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus (only in tmesi)}}, **encompass**, of sounds, τὸν.. περὶ φρένας ἤλυθ᾽ ἰωή {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.139, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.261; τὸν.. περὶ κτύπος ἦλθε ποδοῖιν 19.444}}; of the effect of wine, Κύκλωπα περὶ φρένας ἤλυθεν οἶνος {{Refs|9.362.}}
**3** **overreach, cheat**, σοφίῃ π. τινά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.4, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1142 (Lyric poetry), 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Nicias” 10.}}
**4** later, in literal sense, **surround**, κύκλῳ π. τοὺς πολεμίους {{Refs|[prev. author] “Publ.” 22, compare “Ages.” 38.}}
**II** **go round and return** to a point, **come round**, αὖτις ἐς τυραννίδας περιῆλθον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.96}}; περιῆλθεν ἐς ὀκτὼ ὁμιλητὰς ἐς τεττάρων καὶ τριάκοντα **came down** to.., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 4.37}}; of things, events, etc., ἡ ἡγεμονίη περιῆλθε.. ἐς τὸ γένος, ἡ βασιληΐη περιῆλθε ἔς τινα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.7, 187, +others; περιελήλυθε ὁ πόλεμος καὶ ἀπῖκται ἐς ὑμέας [prev. author] 7.158}}; ἐς φθίσιν περιῆλθε ἡ νοῦσος the disease **ended** in..,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 88; π. εἰς ἅπαντας ὁ λόγος 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.151a, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 866b; ἐπειδὰν οἱ προκείμενοι λόγοι περιέλθωσι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 4.20: with _accusative_, Πανιώνιον ἡ τίσις περιῆλθε}} vengeance **came at last upon** him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.106}}; ταῦτα ἰσχυρῶς περιελήλυθε τοὺς πολλούς **has taken** strong **hold upon** them, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Luct.” 10}}; τὸ πάθος.. τοὺς πολλοὺς.. π. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Hist.Conscr.” 2.}}
**II.2** of Time, **come round**, περιῆλθεν ὁ ἐνιαυτός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.6.19.}}
**II.3** of the heavenly bodies, **rotate, revolve**, ὁ οὐρανὸς π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Caelo” 272b14; ὁπόταν ὁ ἥλιος τὸν ἑαυτον περιέλθῃ κύκλον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 39c.}}

4023:
** περιέχω**,
also **περιεργ-ίσχω**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.71}}; _Aeolic dialect_ **περρέχω** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 25.9, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 30.3:}}_future_ περιέξω (and περισχήσω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.7):}}_aorist_ περιέσχον, _infinitive_ περισχεῖν: _aorist_ _middle_ περιεσχόμην, _infinitive_ περισχέσθαι:—**encompass, embrace, surround**, κυκλόθεν ὁδὸς π. [τὸ χωρίον] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 7.28; ἡ περιέχουσα [πέλαγος] γῆ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 25a, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 354a6; γραμμαὶ περιέχουσαι τὸ χωρίον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 85a, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Mechanica” 851a14; ἡ περιέχουσα [ἶρις] [prev. author] “Mete.” 375a31; τόπον κύκλῳ πέτραις περιεχόμενον “IG” 42(1).122.21 (Epid.)}}; τὸ περιέχον the **envelope** of a seed, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 1.11.1.}}
**b** especially of that which **encompasses** the earth or the universe, τὸν κόσμον πνεῦμα καὶ ἀὴρ π{{Refs 5th c.BC+|. Anaxim. 2; ὁ περὶ χθόν᾽ ἔχων αἰθήρ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 919 (assuming variant), compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 3.17.4; τὸ περιέχον πάντα ὁπόσα νοητὰ ζῷα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 31a, compare 33b}}; τὸ περιέχον the **environment**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “De rerum natura - Wien. Stud.” 79 G., +others, 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 2.3.14; τὸ περιέχον ἡμᾶς ἅπαντας καὶ γῆν καὶ θάλατταν, ὃ καλοῦμεν οὐρανόν 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 16.2.35}}; ὁ περιέχων ἀήρ ἠήρ) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “νόμος” 3, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 379a28, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 3.47, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.333f, etc.}}; ὁ περιέχων alone, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cor.” 38}}; but usually τὸ περιέχον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Anaxagoras Philosophus 2, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Juventute” 468a3, 1st c.BC: Ptolemaeus Epigrammaticus “Phas.” p.10 H., 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 8.286}}; τὸ ἄπειρον καὶ τὸ π. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione et Corruptione” 332a25, compare “Ph.” 253a13, 259b11; φαμὲν τὸ μὲν π. τοῦ εἴδους εἶναι, τὸ δὲ περιεχόμενον τῆς ὕλης [prev. author] “Cael.” 312a12, compare “Ph.” 211b12.}}
**c** τὸ π. the **atmosphere**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.37.9, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 4.38, etc.}}; δυσκρασίαι τοῦ π. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alexander” 58.}}
**2** **embrace**, τινὰ ταῖς χερσίν Id {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Ant.” 79, compare “Alex.” 51, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 2.5.3; πατρὸς περὶ ἔχοντος 4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 115.1.}}
**3** **surround** so as to guard, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Caesar” 16, etc.}}:—but, _passive_, **to be shut in, beleaguered**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.10; ὑπὸ τῶν πολεμίων κύκλῳ [prev. work] 79}}; πανταχόθεν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 80, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.1.24}}: _metaphorically_, **to be hard pressed**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 289; περισχομένη κακότητι 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 3.95.}}
**4** **embrace, comprise, comprehend**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 87d, etc.; πλείω γένη 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1285a2; περιέχεται ὑπὸ τοῦ ὅλου τὰ πάντα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 145c}}; **contain**, βίβλος π. τὰς πράξεις {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 2.1; λόγος π. ἐγκώμιον 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 660}}; of a letter, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 12.4.11: _impersonal_, περιέχει ἐν γραφῇ}}, followed by a quotation, {{NT|NT.1Pet.2.6}}; καθὼς ἡ ὠνὴ π. as **is contained in** the deed of sale, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Supp.epigram” 3.421.33 (_Locrian dialect_, 2nd c.AD).}}
**4.b** in Logic, τὸ περιέχον **universal**, opposed to τὰ περιεχόμενα, the **individuals** or **particulars**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1023b27, compare “APr.” 43b23}}; ὀνόματα περιέχοντα **generic** terms, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Rh.” 1407a31}}; καλοῦσι δ᾽ αὐτοὺς πλάτακας ἀπὸ τοῦ περιέχοντος from the **generic name**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 7.309a.}}
**5** in Mathematics texts, ὁ ὑπὸ δύο ἀριθμῶν περιεχόμενος [ἀριθμός] the **product** of two numbers, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra 7 “Def.” 19}}; but π. ἑαυτόν, of a number of which a higher power terminates in the same digit, {{Refs|“Theologumena Arithmeticae” 33.}}
**6** τὸν ἔλεγχον π. **to be involved in, open to** criticism, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.49 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}
**II** **surpass, excel**, πάντα περρέχοισ᾽ ἄστρα, of the moon, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 25.9}}; **overcome, gain the victory** or **advantage**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.7, 8.105.}}
**II.2** **outflank** the enemy, {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.71; περιέσχον τῷ κέρᾳ οἱ Πελοποννήσιοι [prev. author] 3.108,compare 5.73.}}
**III** _middle_, **hold one's hands round** or **over** another: hence, **protect, defend**, with _genitive_ _person_, περίσχεο (_Ionic dialect_ _imperative_ _aorist 2_ _middle_) παιδὸς ἐῆος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.393: with _accusative_, οὕνεκά μιν περισχόμεθα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.199.}}
**III.2** **hold fast by, cling to**, with _genitive_, γούνων περισχομένη {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.82 (but with _accusative_, περίσχετο γούνατα χερσίν [prev. author] 3.706); περιίσχετο κούρης 2nd c.BC: Moschus Bucolicus 2.11}}: hence, **cleave to, be fond of** a person or thing, γενσάμενοι τῶν ἡμετέρων ἀγαθῶν περιέξονται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.71, compare 3.53, 5.40, 7.39, 160, etc.}}; τὠυτοῦ περιεχόμεθα **we are compassing, aiming at** the same end, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. author] 3.72, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Themistocles” 9}}; κρίσιν.. ἧς μᾶλλον περιέχομαι on which I **place** more **reliance**, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 2.4.}}
**III.3** rarely with _infinitive_, περιείχετο.. μένοντας μὴ ἐκλιπεῖν τὴν τάξιν **clung to his resolution** that they should stay and not leave their post, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.57.}}
**IV** _Aeolic dialect_ περρέχω, ={ὑπερέχω}, ὁπόσον τῷ ποδὶ περρέχει τᾶς γᾶς, τοῦτο χάρις, i.e. every inch of his stature is grace, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 30.3.}}

4026:
** περιίστημι**,.
in the _transitive_ tenses (with _perfect_ περιέστᾰκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Axiochus” 370d)}}, **place round**, π. τοὺς ἑαυτοῦ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.108, etc.; π. στήλην τινί 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.24; π. κύτος τῷ ζῴῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 78c; στράτευμα περὶ πόλιν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.1: _metaphorically_, π. τινὶ ἔτι πλείω κακά 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.123; κινδύνους τοῖς Καρχηδονίοις 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 12.15.7; π. ἀγῶνάς τισι 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Comp. Ag.Gracch.” 5.}}
**2** **bring round**, ὁ δῆμος εἰς ἑαυτὸν περιέστησε τὴν πολιτείαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1304a33; εἰς τοὐναντίον π. τινὰ τῷ λόγῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Ax.” l}}.with; εἰς τοσοῦτον π. τινά, ὥστε.. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Heraclides Ponticus Historicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 12.537c}}; especially into a worse state, εἰς τοῦθ᾽ ἡ τύχη τὰ πράγματα αὐτῶν περιέστησεν ὥστε.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 6.47, compare 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.82; π. εἰς μοναρχίαν τὴν πολιτείαν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.8.2}}; οἴκους εἰς πενίαν π. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 7.3.5}}; **convert**, εἰς τὸ περιφερὲς [τὸν ἀέρα] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 2p.51U.}}; **transfer**, π. τὰς ἑαυτοῦ συμφορὰς εἴς τινα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 40.20; π. τὴν αἰτίαν εἴς τινα 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 3.3.}}
**II** in _aorist 1_ _middle_, **place round oneself**, ξυστοφόρων κύκλον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.41; φρουρὰν περὶ τὸ σῶμα 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 3.4.}}
**B** _passive_ and _middle_, with _aorist 2_ (_aorist_ 1, see.below {{Refs|2)}}_perfect_, and _pluperfect_ _active_:—**stand round about**, περίστησαν γὰρ ἑταῖροι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.532}}; κῦμα περιστάθη a wave **rose around** (_Epic dialect_ _aorist_ _passive_), {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.243; περιστῆναι περί τι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 84e; τοῦ περιεστῶτος ἔξωθεν πνεύματος [prev. work] 76b}}; οἱ περιεστῶτες the **bystanders**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.14; ὄχλου πολλοῦ περιστάντος “IG” 42(1).123.25 (Epid.).}}
**B.2** with _accusative_ objecti, **encircle, surround**, χορὸν περιίσταθ᾽ ὅμιλος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.603}}; βοῦν δὲ περιστήσαντο (perhaps περίστησάν τε){{Refs 8th c.BC+|2.410, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.356}}; μή πώς με περιστήωσ᾽ ἕνα πολλοί (_Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ _subjunctive_ _aorist 2_ for -στῶσι) that their numbers **surround** me not, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.95, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.50; so περιστάντες [τὸ θηρίον] κύκλῳ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.43, compare 9.5, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 379, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 432b; π. τὸν λόφον τῷ στρατεύματι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.1.5: _metaphorically_, τὸ περιεστὸς ἡμᾶς δεινόν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.10, compare 7.70; τοσούτου πολέμου τὴν Ἀσίαν περιστάντος 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.162; χωρὶς τῆς περιστάσης ἂν ἡμᾶς αἰσχύνης 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.8; διὰ τὸν φόβον τὸν περιστάντα αὐτούς 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.137; φόβος π. τινά 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.54, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.195.}}
**B.3** with _dative_, περιισταμένους τῇ κλίνῃ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 947b}}: mostly _metaphorically_, **come round to** one, ἡμῖν.. ἀδοξία τὸ πλέον ἢ ἔπαινος περιέστη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.76; τῇ [Ἑλλάδι] δουλεία περιέστηκε 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.60; τοῦ πολέμου περιεστηκότος Θηβαίοις 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 16.28; πηλίκα τῇ πόλει περιέστηκε πράγματα [prev. author] 19.340}}; ἀνάγκη π. τινί, with _infinitive_, {{Refs|[prev. work]212: _absolutely_, of}}**circumstances**, mostly bad, τὰ περιεστηκότα πράγματα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.32, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Sententiae” 38; οἱ περιεστῶτες καιροί 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.86.7.}}
**B.II** **come round, revolve**, κύκλῳ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 217a19}}; of winds, ἐκ τῶν ἀπαρκτίων εἰς θρασκίας {{Refs|[prev. author] “Mete.” 365a6}}; of Time, περιισταμένης τῆς ὥρας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 2.11.2, compare 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ φύσιος ἀνθρώπου” 7.}}
**B.II.2** **come round to, devolve upon**, περιειστήκει ὑποψία ἐς τὸν Ἀλκιβιάδην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.61; νομίσαντες τὸ παρανόμημα ἐς τοὺς Αθηναίους τὸ αὐτὸ περιεστάναι [prev. author] 7.18}}; εἰς ὀλίγους ἡμᾶς περιέστη [ἡ στατίων] {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“IG” 14.830.8 (Puteoli, 2nd c.AD).}}
**B.II.3** of events, **come round, turn out**, especially **for the worse**, ἐξ ἀρρωστίης π. τινὶ ἐς ὕδερον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Κωακαὶ προγνώσιες” 471 (but also of persons, ἐς ὕδρωπα περιίσταντο}} **became** dropsical, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Epid.” 3.13)}}; ἐς τοῦτο περιέστη ἡ τύχη fortune **was** so completely **reversed**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.12}}; τοὐναντίον περιέστη αὐτῷ **it turned out** quite contrary for him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 6.24, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.64, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 70c; ὁ τοῦ δικαίου λόγος εἰς τοὐναντίον περιειστήκει [prev. author] “R.” 343a}}; φιλεῖ ἐς τύχας τὰ πολλὰ περιίστασθαι **come to be dependent** on chances, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.78; εἰ τὰ μὲν πράγματ᾽ εἰς ὅπερ νυνὶ περιέστη 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.201, compare 3.9; τὸ πρᾶγμ᾽ εἰς ὑπέρδεινόν μοι περιέστη [prev. author] 21.111, compare 37.10}}; ἐνταῦθα τὰ πράγματα π. ὥστε.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.59, compare 5.55}}; περιέστηκεν εἰς τοῦτο ὥστε.. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 3}}: with _infinitive_, περιειστήκει τοῖς βοηθείας δεήσεσθαι δοκοῦσιν αὐτοὺς βοηθεῖν ἑτέροις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.218, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 244d}}: with _participle_, περιέστηκεν ἡ πρότερον σωφροσύνη νῦν ἀβουλία φαινομένη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.32.}}
**B.III** later, **go round so as to avoid, shun**, τὰς ἁμαρτίας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.384 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus; τὴν ὁμιλίαν 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 1.1.4; κύνας 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Herm.” 86}} (though he censures this usage, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Sol.” 5), compare 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “de Usu Partium” 10.14, 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Abstinentia - Porphyrii Opuscula” 4.7, etc.; τὸν κίνδυνον 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de vita Pythagorica” 33.239}}; τὸ μοναρχικόν{{NT+2nd c.AD+|[prev. work] 31.189; τὴν ἀφροσύνην 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 11.93; κενοφωνίας NT.2Tim.2.16; τὸ εἰκῇ καὶ μάτην 2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 3.4; τοὺς ἡγουμένους 2nd c.AD: Artemidorus Daldianus Onirocriticus 4.59}}; π. μὴ.. **to be afraid** lest.., {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 4.6.12}}; **sneak round**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.99 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}; **circumvent**, τοὺς λογιστάς Mitteis {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Chr.” 88iv 11 (2nd c.AD)}}:—so in _passive_, περιεσταμένης τῆς λογοθεσίας {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 1019.8 (2nd c.AD).}}

4027:
** περικᾰθᾰρ-μα**, ατος, τό,
**expiation**,{{LXX|LXX.Pro.21.18.}}
**II** ={κάθαρμα}{{NT+2nd c.AD+|1.2, περικαθάρματα τοῦ κόσμου NT.1Cor.4.13, compare 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 3.22.78.}}

4028:
** περι-κᾰλύπτω**,
**cover all round**, πολέμοιο νέφος περὶ πάντα καλύπτει {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.243, compare 10.201; π. δένδρεον πίλῳ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.23}}; τινὰ ἱματίοισι or ἐν ἱματίῳ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 5.59, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.3.14: _metaphorically_, π. σωτηρία τοὺς νόμους 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 793b}}; τὸ θνητὸν περικάλυπτε τῷ θεῷ (i.e. Διονύσῳ), i.e. get drunk, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 20; τὰ πάθη 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.100f; π. καὶ ἀρνεῖσθαι [prev. work]1013e:}} —_middle_ and _passive_, **cover oneself all round**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 51d, etc.}}
**II** **put round as a covering**, αὐτῷ.. περὶ κῶμα κάλυψα **put** sleep **as a cloak around** him, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.359}}; τὸ σῶμα [ψυχῇ] {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 34b; π. τὰ Χερουβεὶν ἐπὶ τὴν κιβωτόν LXX.3Ki.8.7: _metaphorically_, π. τοῖσι πράγμασι σκότον}} **throw a veil** of darkness **over** the deeds, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1522.}}

4029:
** περίκειμαι**,
_infinitive_ -κεῖσθαι: _future_ -κείσομαι: —used as _passive_ of περιτίθημι, **lie round about**, with _dative_, εὗρε σὲ Πατρόκλῳ περικείμενον ὃν φίλον νἱόν **lying with his arms round** him, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.4}}; [γωρυτὸς τόξῳ] περίκειτο] **there was** a case **round** the bow, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.54}}; πασσάλοις (_accusative_ _plural_) κρύπτοισιν περικείμεναι.. κνάμισες {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 15; οἷς στέφανος περίκειται Pi.O. 8.76}}; τὸ σχῆμα καὶ τὸ ὄνομα τῆς βασιλείας τινὶ π. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 6.1.1; π. τινὶ τῶν πράξεων κηλίς 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Dio” 56: with _accusative_, σφέας εὐσίη καὶ γαληναίη περικέαται 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Astr.” 3}}: with a _preposition_, περὶ [τὰς φλέβας] τὸ σῶμα π. τὸ τῶν σαρκῶν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 764b30: _absolutely_, τὰ περικείμενα χρυσία}} plates of gold **laid on** (an ivory statue), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.13}}; [ὁ κημὸς] περικείμενος **put round** the horse's mouth, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 5.3.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, οὐδέ τί μοι περίκειται **there is** no **advantage** for me, I have nought **laid by**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.321.}}
**2.b** οἱ περικείμενοί τινι his **supporters**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“POxy.” 1408.24 (3rd c.AD).}}
**II** with _accusative_ _of things_, **have round one, wear**, mostly in _participle_, [τελαμῶνας] περὶ τοῖσι αὐχέσι περικείμενοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.171, compare “OGI” 56.67 (Canopus, 3rd c.BC)}}; τιάρας π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 15.3.15; στεφάνους 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Aratus” 17; πτέρυγα 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Icar.” 14; προσωπεῖον [prev. author] “Nigr.” 11, 5th-4th c.BC: Aesopus Fabularum Scriptor 360}}; στρατιωτικὴν δύναμιν π **invested with**.., {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pompeius” 51}}; ὕβριν π. **clad in** arrogance, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 23.14 (assuming variant): rarely in other moods, περίκεισο ἄνθεα}} **have** garlands **put round thee**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 11.38 (1st-2nd c.AD: Polemo Sophista)}}; περιέκειτο ξίφος, σχῆμα βασιλικόν, {{NT+2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 3.5.7, 5.4.7; τὴν ἅλυσιν ταύτην περίκειμαι NT.Act.28.20; περίκειται ἀσθένειαν NT.Heb.5.2.}}

4030:
** περικεφάλ-αιος** [ᾰ], α, ον,
**round the head**: hence,
**II** _substantive_ περικεφαλαία, ἡ, **covering for the head, helmet, cap**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Callias Comicus 1 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator, Aen. Tact. 24.6, “PPetr.” 3p.328 (3rd c.BC), etc.}}; π. σιδηρᾶ περιηργυρωμένη {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 11(2).161 B 77 (Delos, 3rd c.BC), compare 22.1478.16, 12(5).647.30 (from Ceos), 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.71.4, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 6.9.4, 2nd c.AD: Antyllus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 6.36.3.}}
**II.b** **wig**, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**II.2** **disorder of the oak**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 3.8.7.}}
**II.3** in a ship, ={ὁ στόλος ὑπὲρ τὴν στεῖραν}, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 1.86, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 3.13.4.}}
**II.4** name of a **bandage**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus “de fasciis - Opera Hippocratis et Galeni” 24.}}

4033:
** περικυκλ-όω**,
**encircle, encompass**, {{LXX+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 533b11, LXX.Gen.19.4, “PLond.” 2.681.9 (4th c.AD)}}, etc.:—more frequently in _middle_, **surround** an enemy, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.78, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.3.11, etc.; in tmesi, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 346.}}
**II** _intransitive_, **go round**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Ocyp.” 63.}}

4034:
** περι-λάμπω**,
**shine around** or **brightly**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cam.” 17; πρὸς τὴν σελήνην [prev. author] “Arat.” 21, etc.}}:—_middle_, περιλαμπομένας φύσεις ὑπερβάλλειν λαμπρότητι {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 3.12; τῷ χρυσῷ 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Ind.” 9.}}
**II** c._accusative_, **light up**, πολεμίῳ πυρὶ τὴν Ἑλλάδα π. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demades Orator 39}}; **illuminate**, τὰ κεκρυμμένα τῶν ἀδικημάτων ταῖς ἀκτῖσι {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.634}}; **shine around**, {{NT+1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cicero” 35; φῶς π. τινά NT.Act.26.13, cf. NT.Luke.2.9}}:—_passive_, **to be illumined**, φωτί, ὑπὸ τῆς φλογός, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 39, “Dio” 46; ὑπὸ τῶν ἀστέρων 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Dom.” 8.}}

4037:
** περιμένω**,
**wait for, await**, with _accusative_ _person_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.89, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 643, etc.; π. Τισσαφέρνην ἡμέρας πλείους ἢ εἴκοσι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.4.1, etc.: with _accusative_ _of things_, π. ἐξ ἀγορᾶς ἰχθύδια 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 387.8}}; τοῦ καιροῦ μὴ περιμένοντός τι as the time could not **wait for**.., {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Caesar” 17.}}
**2** **require, expect**, σχολάζουσαν φιληκοΐαν {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.172e.}}
**3** **endure, put up with**, μακρὰ λέγοντας ἡμᾶς αὐτοὺς περιεμείναμεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 890e.}}
**4** of events, **await, be in store for**, τίς με πότμος ἔτι π.; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1296 (Lyric poetry)}}; μὴ θύσαντας δεινὰ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 365a; ἃ τελευτήσαντα ἑκάτερον π. [prev. work] 614a.}}
**II** with _infinitive_, οὐ περιμενοῦσιν ἄλλους σφᾶς διολέσαι **will** not **wait for** others to destroy them, {{Refs|[prev. work] 375c}}; ἕκαστος [τῶν λόγων] π. ἀποτελεσθῆναι **awaits** its accomplishment, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tht.” 173c}}; μηδ᾽ ἐφ᾽ ἑαυτὸν [τὰ τοιαῦτα] ἐλθεῖν π. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.220; π. τὰ λοιπὰ μαθεῖν 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 1.13.}}
**III** _absolutely_, **wait, stand still**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.58, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 517, etc.; π. αὐτοῦ [prev. author] “Ach.” 815; ὀλίγον χρόνον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 38c; π. ἕως [ἂν] τὸν ὄχλον διωσώμεθα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.39; ἕως ἀνοιχθείη τὸ δεσμωτήριον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 59d, compare 116a}}; μέχρι τούτου, ἕως ἂν.. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.10}}; ἄχρι ἂν.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.3.2}}; μέχρις ἄν.. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Epictetus Philosophus “Enchiridion” 15.}}

4038:
** πέριξ**,
strengthened for περί, mostly _Ionic dialect_ Prose and Trag. (in latter usually _adverb_):
**I** _preposition_, **round about, all round**, with _genitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.179, 2.91, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.8.12, 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 2p.51U., etc.}}
**I.2** rarely with _dative_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 710.}}
**I.3** commonly with _accusative_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.196, 3.158, 4.36, +others}}: mostly before its case, but also after,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 52, 79, as also in 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 368, uncertain in 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 243.}}
**II** _adverb_ **round about**, π. ὑπορύσσοντες τὸ τεῖχος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.115; π. λαβεῖν ἄνθρωπον to surround him, [prev. work] 87}}; κύκλῳ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 418, uncertain in 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1301, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 266; [Ὠκεανὸς] π. γᾶν ἀμπέχει 2nd c.BC: Limenius Lyricus 10: _metaphorically_, πᾶν π. φρονοῦντες}} **circuitously**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 448}}: rare in _Attic dialect_ Prose, π. πολιορκεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.90}}; ὁ π. τόπος, οἱ π., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 62e, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.5.2, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 2p.47U.}}; ὁ π. χρόνος, i.e. all times save the present, {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Interpretatione” 16b18; τὸ π. ὕδωρ 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Sensu” 26; later αἱ π. πόλεις NT.Act.5.16; αἱ π. κῶμαι “SIG” 880.44 (Pizus, 3rd c.AD).}}

4040:
** περίοικος**, ον,
**dwelling round**, οἱ π. Λίβυες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.159}}; οἱ π. **neighbours**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.166, 175, 4th c.BC: Ephippus Comicus 5.6}}; ἡ π. (i.e. χώρα) {{LXX|LXX.Gen.19.25}}; τὸ π. τοῦ Ἰορδάνου{{LXX|LXX.3Ki.7.33(46)}}; τὰ π. the **neighbouring countries**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Μιθριδάτειος” 112, 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 6.2.1.}}
**II** in Laconia, οἱ π. the **inhabitants of the towns dependent on Sparta**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.58, 9.11, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.101, etc.}}; also in Crete, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1271b30; at Argos, [prev. work] 1303a8}}: hence generally, **dependent, subject**, περιοίκους τε καὶ οἰκέτας ἔχοντες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 547c; ἐξὸν.. ἅπαντας τοὺς βαρβάρους περιοίκους τῆς Ἑλλάδος καταστῆσαι 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.131.}}
**III** geographically, περίοικοι, οἱ, **those who live on the same parallel of latitude as ourselves but** 180° **East or West of us**, opposed to ἄντοικοι (see. Addenda), ἀντίποδες (which see). {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Geminus Astronomicus 16.1, 2nd c.AD: Cleomedes Astronomus 1.2.}}

4041:
** περιούσι-ος**, ον,
**having more than enough, wealthy**, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus; uncertain meaning in “PGen.” 11.17 (4th c.AD).}}
**II** **especial, peculiar**, λαός {{LXX+NT|LXX.Exo.19.5, +others, NT.Tit.2.14.}}

4042:
** περιοχ-ή**, ἡ,
(περιέχω) **a containing, enclosing**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1078b, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Hermes Trismegistus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 1.49.69.}}
**2** **compass, circumference**, σφαίρας {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Placita Philosophorum” 3 “Prooem.”, compare 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 5.4.3, 2nd c.AD: Cleomedes Astronomus 1.11, 2.3, 2nd c.AD: Diogenes Oenoandensis Epicureus 24}}; opposed to μῆκος, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 492.10 (2nd c.AD)}}; ἡ ἐκτὸς π., of the body, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Coloribus” 797b22, compare “Pr.” 870a10, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.91}}; κατὰ τὰς τῶν ἐθνῶν π. according to their **extent**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 17.58}}; **mass, body**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lysander” 12; π. τις οὐρανοῦ 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 2p.37U.}}; ἀκατάληπτος π., of the world, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Secundus Gnomologus “Sententiae - FPG” 1.}}
**3** generally, **compass, extent**, ἡ π. τῆς ὅλης περιβολῆς καὶ πράξεως {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 11.19.2}}; **aggregate**, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 88, 95 bis.}}
**3.b** **content** of a definition, etc., {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Cornutus Philosophus “Rh.” p.382H., 3rd c.AD: Alexander Aphrodisiensis Philosophus “in Aristotelis Analyticorum Priorum librum I commentarium - pars” 278.11, etc.}}
**3.c** **summary**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Hermes Trismegistus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 1.41.1}}; σύντομος π. {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “in Platonis Timaeum commentarii” 1.73}}; **periochae**, title of summaries of books of Livy.
**4** **inclusion**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 3.101}}; κατὰ περιοχήν τινος **including**.., {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.488.}}
**II** **portion circumscribed** or **marked off, section** of a book, {{NT+1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “Epistulae ad Atticum” 13.25.3, NT.Act.8.32.}}
**III** **pod, husk, shell**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 1.19.2.}}
**III.2** **fence, fortification**, {{LXX|LXX.1Ki.22.4, +others}}; πόλις περιοχῆς{{LXX|LXX.Psa.30(31).21, +others}}
**IV** **straitness**, ={θλῖψις},{συνοχή}, {{Refs 9th c.AD+|9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc.}}; especially **siege**, {{LXX|LXX.Jer.19.9}}; ἦλθεν πόλις ἐν περιοχῇ{{LXX|LXX.2Ki.24.10}}; ὕδωρ περιοχῆς{{LXX|LXX.Nah.3.14.}}
**V**=περιπέτεια, {{Refs 9th c.AD+|9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc.}}

4043:
** περιπᾰτ-έω**,
**walk up and down**, as in a cloister, opposed to βαδίζειν (take a walk), ἐν ταῖς στοαῖς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Dicaearchus Geographus cited in 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.796d}}; **walk about**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 744, “V.” 237; περιπατῶν ἀνδριάς 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 204; ἐν τῷ καταστέγῳ δρόμῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 273a; π. ἄνω κάτω 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 709; π. περιπάτους 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.13.5, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Περικειρομένη” 156}}; περιπατεῖται ἡ ὁδός the road **is for walking on**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 279.19}}: with _accusative_, **traverse**, ὅλην τὴν Αἴγυπτον {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“POxy.” 471.124 (2nd c.AD).}}
**2** **walk about while teaching, discourse**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 348c, 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 7.109}}; π. ἐς τοὺς ἀκροωμένους **dispute, argue** with them, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 1.17, compare 7.22.}}
**3** _metaphorically_, **walk**, i.e. **live**, {{NT+1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ παρρησίας” p.12 O.; κατὰ τὴν παράδοσιν NT.Mark.7.5; ἀτάκτως NT.2Thes.3.6.}}

4044:
** περιπείρω**,
**put on a spit**, π. τι περὶ λόγχην {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Galba” 27}}: _metaphorically_, **pierce**, ἑαυτοὺς π. ὀδύναις {{NT|NT.1Tim.6.10}}:—_passive_, **to be spitted** or **pierced**, ξίφεσι καὶ λόγχαις {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 16.80; Χάρακι [prev. author] 19.84; σκόλοπι 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 7.48; ὀβελοῖς 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Gall.” 2}}: _metaphorically_, **to become entangled**, δυσαναπορεύτοις βαράθροις περιπαρέντες {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.672; δίκτυα, οἷς ἀνάγκη περιπείρεσθαι [prev. author] 2.411, compare 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 250.11.}}
**II** **run into**, τοὺς ὀδόντας τῇ δειρῇ {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Descriptiones” 12.2 (_passive_)}}:—_passive_, ἄγκιστρα περιπαρέντα τοῖς ἰχθύσι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 15.10.}}

4045:
** περιπίπτω**,
_future_ - πεσοῦμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 523, etc.}}: _Epic dialect_ _perfect_ _participle_ _feminine_ περιπεπτηυῖαι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Eratosthenes Epicus 16.9}}:—**fall around**, i.e. **so as to embrace**, τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.8.28; ἐπί τινι 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Crassus” 17; εἰς τὸ στῆθος [prev. author] “Sert.” 26.}}
**2** **fall around**, i.e. **upon**, a weapon, τῷ ξίφει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 523; τῷ βέλει 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.3.6.}}
**3** [ζῶναι] πόλοις περιπεπτηυῖαι **encircling** the poles, Eratosth. [prev. cited]
**4** **fall over**, ἑκατέρωσε {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pyrrhus” 24; πλαγία περιπεσοῦσα [prev. author] “Ant.” 67.}}
**II** with _dative_, **fall in with**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.105; ἀλλήλοις 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.3.38, etc.}}; frequently of ships meeting at sea, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.41, 8.94, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.33, 103}}; π. μουσικῇ τε καὶ ταῖς μέθαις **having encountered** them in our discussion. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 682e}}; ἡ ὄψις κάμνουσα ἐν τοῖς μικροῖς τοῖς μεγάλοις ἀσμένως π. {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.9.3}}: _absolutely_, **supervene**, {{Refs|Petosiris Astrologus cited in Vett. Val. 278.8.}}
**II.2** **fall foul of** other ships, τῇσι σφετέρῃσι νηυσί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.89; περὶ ἀλλήλας of one another, [prev. work] 16}}; also π. περὶ τὴν Σηπιάδα **to be wrecked** on.., {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.188.}}
**II.3** _metaphorically_, **fall in with, fall into**, mostly of evil, with _dative_, π. ἀδίκοισι γνώμῃσι **fall in with, encounter** unjust judgements, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.96}}; π. τοιαύτῃσι τύχῃσι, δουλοσύνῃ, {{Refs|[prev. author] 6.16, 106}}; νούσοις, νοσήμασι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 3, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.2.27; λουτροῖσιν ἀλόχου 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 367; αἰσχρᾷ τύχῃ [prev. author] “Hec.” 498; ἀκουσίοις κακοῖς 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.3.7; τοιούτῳ πάθει 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.54; τοιαύτῃ συμφορᾷ περιπέπτωκεν ὑπὸ τούτου 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.96, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.51; ἀβροχίαις “OGI” 56.15 (Canopus, 3rd c.BC)}}; π. συκοφάνταις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 7.1; αἰσχύνῃ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.3.9; ταῖς μεγίσταις ζημίαις 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 7.27, compare 12.146; αἰτίᾳ 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Antonius” 67}}; also σοὶ αὐτῷ περιπίπτειν **to be caught in** your own **snare**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.108, compare 8.16, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMort.” 26.2; τοῖς ἐμαυτοῦ λόγοις περιπίπτω 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.144: with a _preposition_, ἐν σφίσι κατὰ τὰς ἰδίας διαφορὰς π. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.65}}: _absolutely_, **come to grief**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 8.36.4, 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 16.2.}}
**II.4** of events, **befall** one, ἤν μοί τι περιπίπτῃ κακόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 271: _absolutely_, δεῖ τι περιπεσεῖν 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 1.33.}}
**III** **change suddenly**, εἴς τι {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.4.5.}}

4046:
** περιποι-έω**,
**cause to remain over and above, keep safe, preserve**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.36, +others, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.25, +others, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.63, etc.}}; ἐκ κακῶν καὶ πολέμου ὑμᾶς αὐτοὺς π. {{Refs|[prev. author] 6.47.}}
**2** of money, food, etc., **save up, lay by**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 11.10; ἀπ᾽ ὀλίγων [prev. work] 2.10}}; τῶν προσόδων **part** of the revenues, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 6.38, compare “POxy.” 2148.17 (1st c.AD).}}
**3** **obtain a net product** or **residue**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Lapidibus” 58.}}
**4** generally, **procure, secure, achieve, lay up**, αἰσχύνην τῇ πόλει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.301; π. τῇ πόλει τὰ εἰς τιμὴν ἀνήκοντα “Milet.” 3 No.146.84 (3rd c.BC)}}; πολλὰ καὶ μεγάλα τῶν συμφερόντων τῇ πατρίδι {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“IGRom.” 4.1757 (Sardes, 1st c.BC)}}; δυναστείας ἑαυτοῖς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.3 (_middle_),compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 15.11}}; τὰ πράγματα ἐς ἑαυτοὺς π. **get** things into their own hands, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.48, compare 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 7.6.}}
**II** _middle_, **keep** or **save for oneself**, [τὸ παιδίον] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.110 (sed to be read -ποιήσῃς) τὸ ζῆν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1315a26; ἐλπίδας ἑαυτῷ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.240}}; **compass, acquire, gain possession of**, δύναμιν, ἰσχύν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.9, 15}}; ἑαυτῷ ὄνομα καὶ δύναμιν π. {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.6.17, cf. LXX.1Mac.6.44; παρὰ τοῦ πλήθους δόξαν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 12.19; αὑτοῖς δυναστείαν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1306a24; πρόβατα “PMich.Zen.” 87.7 (3rd c.BC)}}; [ἐκκλησίαν] διὰ τοῦ αἵματος {{NT|NT.Act.20.28}}; **saue**, τοσαῦτα ὥστε καὶ πλουτεῖν {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.7.3; χρυσίον LXX.1Ch.29.3}}; **make gain**, ἀπό τινος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.2.38}}:— _passive_, χρήματα περιποιηθησόμενα {{Refs|“Codex Justinianus [4th-6th c.AD]” 1.4.26 “Intr.”}}
**II.2** in sense of _active_ 1.1, frequently in {{LXX|LXX, as LXX.Gen.12.12, +others}}

4047:
** περιποί-ησις**, εως, ἡ,
**keeping safe, preservation**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Definitiones” 415c, LXX.2Ch.14.13(12), LXX.Mal.3.17, NT.Heb.10.39.}}
**2** concrete, **those who are saved,** {{NT|NT.Eph.1.14.}}
**II** **gaining possession of, acquisition**, {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.1Thes.5.9, NT.2Thes.2.14, “PTeb.” 317.26 (2nd c.AD), 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 85.16, 6th c.AD: Justinianus Imperator “Edict.” 13.15}}; **procuring**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 294.9.}}
**II.2** λαὸς εἰς περιποίησιν, ={λ. περιούσιος}, {{NT|NT.1Pet.2.9.}}

4048:
** περιρρήγνῡμι**
and **περιρρηγνύω** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|(1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Publicola” 6)}}, **break off all round**, τὸν γήλοφον κύκλῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 113d}}: frequently of clothes, **rend and tear off**, τὸν χιτωνίσκον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.197; τὴν χλαμύδα 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 15.33.4}}: also with _accusative_ _person_, **strip**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Parthenius Mythographus 15.3:}} —_middle_, περιερρήξατο τοὺς πέπλους **tore off her own** garments, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Antonius” 77, compare 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.44: _absolutely_, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 9.7.3, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 7.24.3, 1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 35.9; [γυναῖκες], περιερρηγμέναι [prev. author] 46.12:}} —_passive_, with _aorist 2_ -ερράγην, _intransitive_ _perfect_ περιέρρωγα, περιρρηγνυμένων φαρέων {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 328 (Lyric poetry)}}; of the case or membrane that encloses pupa or shellfish, περιρρήγνυται τὸ κέλυφος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 551a23, compare 552a6; περιερρωγέναι τὸ ὄστρακον [prev. work] 601a13}} (so in _active_, ἡ σχάδων.. τὸν ὑμένα περιρρήξας (sic) ἐκπέταται{{Refs|[prev. work] 554a30}}.—_middle_, τὰ ζῷα τὰ ἐκ τῶν σκωλήκων περιρρηγνύμενα{{Refs|[prev. work] 552a9); πέτρα περιρραγεῖσα [prev. work] 578b22}}; of dead flesh, **break away**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 26.}}
**II** **cause** a stream **to break** or **divide round** a piece of land, [Βούσιρις] τὸν Νεῖλον περὶ τὴν χώραν περιέρρηξε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 11.31}}:—_passive_, τοῦ Δέλτα κατὰ τὸ ὀξὺ περιρρήγνυται ὁ Νεῖλος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.16, compare 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 7.24}}; βρονταὶ περιερρήγνυντο **kept breaking round** a place, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Crassus” 19.}}
**III** **break** a thing **round** or **on** another, **wreck**, τὸ σκαφίδιον πρὸς πέτραν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Merc.Cond.” 2, compare 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 1.114; ἀλλήλοισι π. ἀέλλας 4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 8.61.}}
**IV** ὄρος περιερρωγός **broken all round**, i.e. **precipitous**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Nicolaus Damascenus Historicus 1 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus}}

4049:
** περι-σπάω**,
_future_ **-σπάσω**{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 20.3, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 87.15}}:—**draw off from around, strip off**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator “Epistulae” 9.10}}; τὸ χλαμύδιον αὑτοῦ π. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 19.9, etc.}}:—_middle_, **strip oneself of**, τὴν τιάραν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.1.13}} (so also in _active_, π. τὴν πορφύραν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Aemilius Paulus” 23).}}
**2** **strip bare**, περισπάσας ξίφος (Pierson χερὶ σπάσας) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 296.}}
**II** **wheel about**, of a general, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.76.5}}; _intransitive_ of the troops, {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.116.5}}; especially **wheel twice** through a right angle, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Asclepiodotus Tacticus “Tact.” 12.6,a l}}.; of a horse's bit, οὐ πάνυ π. not **pulling** it violently **round**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Merc.Cond.” 21.}}
**III** **draw off** or **away, divert**, εἰς τοὐναντίον [τὴν πολιτείαν] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1307a24; τροφὴν εἰς τὸ περικάρπιον 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 1.16.2; π. τοὺς Ῥωμαίους 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 9.22.5; τὸν πόλεμον [prev. author] 1.26.1}}; π. τὴν δύναμιν αὐτοῦ **draw** it **away**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cicero” 45; ἀπὸ τῆς πατρίδος π. τοὺς βαρβάρους 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 20.3; τὸν ἐντὸς.. θόρυβον ἐπὶ τοὺς ἔξω πολέμους 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 6.23; π. περὶ τὰς ἔξω στρατείας τὸν δῆμον [prev. author] 9.43}}: —_passive_, π. ὑπό τινων {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PStrassb.” 112.13 (2nd c.BC)}}; πάντῃ τὰς ὄψεις περισπώμενος {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DDeor.” 30.11}}; ἕως τοῦ ἔξω τόπου π. **to be drawn away and expanded**, opposed to συστέλλεσθαι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 863a5.}}
**III.b** **detach**, Συρίαν τινός {{Refs 1st c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Συριακή” 1, compare “Hann.” 56; τινὰς αὐτοῦ τῶν ἀκροατῶν 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “Academicorum Index” p.79 M.}}
**III.2** **disturb, vex**, {{LXX+4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 504, LXX.Eccl.5.19}}:—_passive_, ὑπὸ τῶν τελωνῶν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PSI” 3.384.5 (2nd c.BC).}}
**III.3** **divert, distract**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.96b, 16oc; π. [τὴν διάνοιαν] ἀπό τινος 4th c.AD: Metrodorus Epigrammaticus “Herc.” 831.4, compare 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.53 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, +others, 1st c.AD: Onosander (Onasander) Tacticus 42.6}}:—_passive_, **to be distracted** or **engaged**, π. ταῖς διανοίαις {{NT+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 15.3.4; ὑπὸ βιωτικῆς χρείας 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 2.29, compare 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Po.Herc.” 994.24, +others; μηδ᾽ ὑφ᾽ ἑνὸς περισπωμένη ἡ πόλις “IG” 22.1304.7; περί τινος LXX.Sir.41.2; περὶ πολλὴν διακονίαν NT.Luke.10.40: _absolutely_, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.10.3.}}
**III.4** **steal**, ἀργυροῦν ἢ χρυσοῦν ἀνάθημα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “de gymnastica” 45:}} —_passive_, ἅπαντα περιέσπασμαι I **have been robbed of** all, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 143.}}
**III.5** _passive_, with _infinitive_, **to be compelled** to do a thing, περιησπάσθην (sic) ἀνενεγκεῖν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PUniv.Giss.” 19.4 (1st c.AD).}}
**IV** Grammars, **pronounce** a vowel or word **with the circumflex**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 33.24, +others, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Theseus” 26, etc.}}; especially on the last syllable, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 2.52f, etc.; π. τὸν τόνον 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 87.15; τῷ τόνῳ 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 16.495}}; περισπώμεναι [λέξεις] {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 11}}; π. [προσῳδία] {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus 630.2, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.29; περισπώμενος [φθόγγος] [prev. work] 46.}}

4051:
** περίσσ-ευμα**,
_Attic dialect_ **περίττευμα**, ατος, τό, **superfluity**, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 22.7.1}}; **that which remains over**, κλασμάτων {{NT|NT.Mark.8.8}}; **abundance**, opposed to ὑστέρημα, {{NT|NT.2Cor.8.14; ἐκ τοῦ περισσεύματος τῆς καρδίας τὸ στόμα λαλεῖ NT.Matt.12.34.}}
**II** ={περίσσωμα}, **excrement**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.108, _falsa lectio_ in 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.962f.}}

4052:
** περισσ-εύω**,
_Attic dialect_ **περιττεύω**, _imperfect_ ἐπερίσσευον (περιέσσευον is condemned by {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 20)}}, (περισσός) **to be over and above** the number, μύριοί εἰσιν ἀριθμόν.., εἷς δὲ π. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Fragmenta” 160}}; περιττεύσουσιν ἡμῶν οἱ πολέμιοι the enemy **will go beyond** us, **outflank** us, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.8.11.}}
**II** **to be more than enough, remain over**, τἀρκοῦντα καὶ περιττεύοντα {{Refs|[prev. author] “Smp.” 4.35}}; τὸ π. [ἀργύριον] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Vect.” 4.7; ἂν ᾖ τι.. περιττεῦον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 855b; εἴ τι π. ἀπὸ τῶν τόκων “SIG” 672.19 (Delph., 2nd c.BC)}}; ἡ περιττεύουσα τροφή {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 619a20; τὸ π. τῶν κλασμάτων NT.Matt.14.20}}; τοσοῦτον τῷ Περικλεῖ ἐπερίσσευσε, κτλ. such **abundance** of reason **had** Pericles for his belief, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.65; τοσόνδ᾽ ἐπερίσσευσεν αὐτοῖς εὐνοίας 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 19.1.18; τὸ ἀνδρεῖον ἐπερίττευεν αὐτῇ 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 3.11.}}
**II.2** in bad sense, **to be superfluous**, τὰ περισσεύοντα τῶν λόγων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1288}}; ἵν᾽ ἐμοὶ περιττεύῃ, i. e. that I may be over-rich, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Diogenes Oenoandensis Epicureus 64.}}
**III** of persons, **abound in**, χορηγίᾳ, opposed to ἐλλείπω, {{NT+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 18.35.5, etc.; αἱ ἐκκλησίαι ἐπερίσσευον τῷ ἀριθμῷ NT.Act.16.5}}:—_middle_, with _genitive_, περισσεύονται ἄρτων **have more than enough of..,** {{NT|NT.Luke.15.17.}}
**III.2** **to be superior**, π. παρά τινα **to be better than**.., {{LXX|LXX.Eccl.3.19}}; ὑπέρ τινα{{LXX|LXX.1Mac.3.30 (variant)}}; **be better, have the advantage**, {{NT|NT.1Cor.14.12}}; π. μᾶλλον **abound** more and more, i.e. in Christian graces, {{NT|NT.1Thes.4.1, 11:—}}_middle_, περισσευόμεθα, opposed to ὑστερούμεθα, {{NT|NT.1Cor.8.8.}}
**IV** causal, **make to abound**, πᾶσαν χάριν π. {{NT|NT.2Cor.9.8; τινὰς τῇ ἀγάπῃ NT.1Thes.3.12}}:—_passive_, **to be made to abound,** {{NT|NT.Matt.13.12, 25.29.}}
**IV.2** of Time, π. τὰς ὥρας **make** them **longer**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 2.42b.}}

4053:
** περισσός**,
_Attic dialect_ **περιττός**, ή, όν, (from περί, as ἔπισσαι from ἐπί, μέτασσαι from μετά) **beyond the regular number** or **size, prodigious**, δῶρα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 399 (never in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus); μος “Tragica Adespota” 458.3}}; στάθμα, uncertain meaning, see at {ἕλκω} {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 3.}}
**2** **out of the common, extraordinary, strange**, ἔ τι περισσὸν εἰδείη if he has any **signal** knowledge, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 769}}; εἴ τι φρονεῖς καί τι περισσὸν ἔχεις Philisc.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|(“PLG” 2.327); π. λόγος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 841; ἄγρα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1197 (Lyric poetry); πάθος [prev. author] “Supp.” 791 (Lyric poetry); βίος οὐδὲν ἔχων π. ἀλλὰ πάντα σμικρά 5th c.BC: Antipho Sophista 51; οὐ γὰρ π. οὐδὲν οὐδ᾽ ἔξω λόγου πέπονθας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 437; περισσότερα παθήματα 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.4.5; τὰ π. τῶν ἔργων καὶ τερατώδη 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.77}}; ἴδια καὶ π. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 15.145; π. καὶ θαυμαστά 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1141b6}}; πρᾶξις π. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pol.” 1312a27; οὐθὲν δὴ λέγοντες π. φαίνονταί τι λέγειν [prev. author] “Metaphorical” 1053b3}}; τί π. ποιεῖτ; {{NT+4th c.BC+|NT.Matt.5.47; περιττοτάτη φύσις 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 531a9}}; συνανθρωπίζον.. πάντων περισσότατον, of the dog, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 13.611c, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Clearchus Historicus 24}}; in Literature, **striking**, τὸ περιττόν, as a quality of οἱ τοῦ Σωκράτους λόγοι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1265a11}}; τὰ σοφὰ καὶ τὰ π. **refinements**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 409}}; opposed to κοινὸς καὶ δημώδης, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 40.2}} (but also, **elaborate**, π. καὶ πεποιημένος {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.4}}; in bad sense, **far-fetched**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “Epistula ad Pompeium” 2, “Dem.” 56).}}
**3** of persons, **extraordinary, remarkable**, especially for great learning, π. ὢν ἀνήρ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 948; τοὺς.. π. καί τι πράσσοντας πλέον [prev. author] “fragment” 788}}; δυστυχεῖς εἶναι τοὺς π. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 983a2; π. γένος τῶν μελιττῶν [prev. author] “GA” 760a4}}: frequently with the manner added, π. κατὰ φιλοσοφίαν {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pr.” 953a10}}; περὶ τὸν ἄλλον βίον περιττότερος **somewhat extravagant** or **eccentric**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pol.” 1267 b24}}; τῇ φύσει π. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. author] “HA” 622b6; κάλλει 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Demetrius” 2; ἐν ἅπασι [prev. author] “Dem.” 3; τὴν ὥραν 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 1.12}}: with _infinitive_, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 18.}}
**4** with _genitive_, περισσὸς ἄλλων πρός τι **beyond** others in.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 155}}; θύσει τοῦδε περισσότερα **greater things than** this, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 6.321 (Leon. 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor); περιττότερος προφήτου}}**one greater than..,** {{NT|NT.Matt.11.9.}}
**II** **more than sufficient, superfluous**, αἱ π. δαπάναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.6.6}}; περιττὸν ἔχειν to have **a surplus**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 7.6.31}}; οἱ μὲν.. περιττὰ ἔχουσιν, οἱ δὲ οὐδὲ τὰ ἀναγκαῖα.. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Oec.” 20.1: with _genitive_, τῶν ἀρκούντων περιττά}} **more than** sufficient, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 8.2.21; τὰ π. τῶν ἱκανῶν [prev. author] “Hier.” 1.19: frequently in military sense, οἱ π. ἱππεῖς}} the **reserve** horse, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Eq.Mag.” 8.14; οἱ π. τῆς φυλακῆς [prev. work] 7.7}}; π. σκηναί **spare** tents, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 4.6.12 (but τοῖς περιττοῖς χρήσεσθαι}} **their superior numbers**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 4.8.11, compare “Cyr.” 6.3.20)}}; τὸ π. the **surplus, residue**, Inscription cited in [same work]{{Refs|“An.” 5.3.13 (but τὸ π. τοῦ Ἰουδαίου}} the **advantage** of the Jew, {{NT|NT.Rom.3.1)}}; Ἁρπυιῶν τὰ π. their **leavings,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|Anthologia Graeca 11.239 (1st c.AD: Lucillius Epigrammaticus)}}; τὸ π. τῆς ἡμέρας the **remainder** of the day, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Xenophon Ephesius Scriptor Eroticus 1.3}}; π. γράμματα **supplementary** provisions in a will, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 326ii9 (2nd c.AD).}}
**II.2** in bad sense, **superfluous, useless**, οὐδέ τι τοῦ παντὸς κενεὸν πέλει οὐδὲ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 13}}; μόχθος π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 385, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 780; π. κἀνόνητα σώματα [prev. author] “Aj.” 758; βάρος π. γῆς ἀναστρωφώμενοι [prev. author] “fragment” 945; ἄχθος [prev. author] “El.” 1241 (Lyric poetry); τὰ γὰρ π. πανταχοῦ λυπήρ᾽ ἔπη [prev. author] “fragment” 82; αὐδῶ σε μὴ περισσὰ κηρύσσειν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 1048; π. πάντες οὑν μέσῳ λόγοι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 819; π. φωνῶν [prev. author] “Supp.” 459.}}
**II.3** **excessive, extravagant**, μηχανᾶσθαι περισσά commit **extravagances**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.32}}; περισσὰ δρᾶν, πράσσειν, to be **over**-busy, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 617, “Ant.” 68}}; π. φρονεῖν to be **over**-wise, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 924 (_anapaest meter_); ἡ π. αὕτη ἐπιμέλεια τοῦ σώματος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 407b}}; μῆκος πολὺ λόγων π. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lg.” 645c}}; **redundant, overdone**, οἱ καρτεροὶ καὶ π. λόγοι {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ax.” 365c, etc.}}; of dress, ἐσθὴς π. {{NT+1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.615d; περισσοτέρα λύπη NT.2Cor.2.7}}; τοῦ τὰ δέοντ᾽ ἔχειν περιττὰ μισῶ I hate **extravagance** in comparison with moderation, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 254, etc.}}
**II.4** of persons, **over-wise, over-curious**, περισσὸς καὶ φρονῶν μέγα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 445, compare “Ba.” 429 (Lyric poetry)}}; ὁ πολυπράγμων καὶ π. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 9.1.4}}; τὴν περὶ τὸ σῶμα θεραπείαν ἀκριβὴς καὶ π. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cicero” 8}}; so, of speakers, π. ἐν τοῖς λόγοις Δημοσθένης {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.119.}}
**II.5** as a term of praise, **subtle, acute**, ἀκριβὴς καὶ π. διάνοια {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 141b13.}}
**III** Arith., ἀριθμὸς π. an **odd, uneven** number, opposed to ἄρτιος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 170.7, 5th-6th c.BC: Philolaus Philosophus 5, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 356e, etc.; π. ἡμέραι 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 4.61}}; τὸ π. καὶ τὸ ἄρτιον the nature of **odd** and even, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 451c, etc.}}; π. χῶραι the **odd** places in a verse, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|7th c.AD: Hephaestio Grammaticus 5.1; ἀρτιάκις π. ἀριθμός a number divisible by an odd number an even number of times, as 2, 6, 10, 3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra 7 “Def.” 9.}}
**IV** περισσότεροι **more in number, extra**, Carnead. cited in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 9.140.}}
**V** περιττόν, τό, ={στρύχνος μανικός, θρύον}{{Refs 4th c.BC+|11, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 9.11.6; περισσόν 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.73; περίσκον 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 12.8.56.}}
**B** _adverb_ περισσῶς **extraordinarily, exceedingly**, θεοσεβέεες π. ἐόντες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.37}}; ἐπαινέσεται π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1197 (Lyric poetry)}}; π. παῖδας ἐκδιδάσκεσθαι to have them educated **overmuch**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Med.” 295}}; περιττοτέρως τῶν ἄλλων **far above** all others, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.44; περισσότερον τοῦ ἑνός 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Pr.Im.” 14; also περισσά 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.43, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 579, etc.}}
**B.2** **remarkably**, περισσότερον τῶν ἄλλων θάψαι τινά **more sumptuously**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.129; οἴκησις π. ἐσκευασμένη 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.29.7}}; περιττότατα ἔχειν to be **most remarkable**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 589a31; κοσμουμένη π. καὶ σεμνῶς 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.145e}}; περισσότατα ἀνθρώπων θρησκεύειν **in the most singular way**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 37.17}}; ἡδέως καὶ π. **in an uncommon manner**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 3}}; εἰπεῖν στρογγύλως καὶ π. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Is.” 20}}; ἰδίως καὶ π. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Theseus” 19}}; τὰ καινῶς ἱστορούμενα καὶ π. {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.30d.}}
**B.3** **abundantly**, ἐχέτω π. τῆς κρόκης {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 3.41.}}
**B.4** with a _negative_, οὐδὲν περισσὸν τούτων nothing **more than** or **beyond** these, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.4.6; οὐδὲν τῶν ἄλλων περιττότερον πραγματεύεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 20c}}; οὐδὲν π. ἢ εἰ.. no **otherwise** than if.., {{Refs|[prev. author] “Smp.” 219c}}; περισσόν alone, **furthermore**, {{LXX|LXX.Eccl.12.12, +others}}
**B.5** τὰ περισσά **in vain,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Anthologia Graeca 12.182 (2nd c.AD: Strato Epigrammaticus).}}
**B.II** ἐκ περιττοῦ **superfluously, uselessly**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 338c, “Sph.” 265e}}; but ὑπερέχειν ἐκ π. to be **far** superior, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lg.” 734d, compare 802d}}; ἡ κάμινος ἐκαύθη ἐκ π. {{LXX+2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Theodotion LXX.Dan.3.22; ἐκ π. χρησάμενος τῇ παρρησίᾳ 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Pro Merc.Cond.” 13; compare ὑπερεκπερισσοῦ.}}

4054:
** περισσός**,
_Attic dialect_ **περιττός**, ή, όν, (from περί, as ἔπισσαι from ἐπί, μέτασσαι from μετά) **beyond the regular number** or **size, prodigious**, δῶρα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 399 (never in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus); μος “Tragica Adespota” 458.3}}; στάθμα, uncertain meaning, see at {ἕλκω} {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 3.}}
**2** **out of the common, extraordinary, strange**, ἔ τι περισσὸν εἰδείη if he has any **signal** knowledge, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 769}}; εἴ τι φρονεῖς καί τι περισσὸν ἔχεις Philisc.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|(“PLG” 2.327); π. λόγος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 841; ἄγρα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1197 (Lyric poetry); πάθος [prev. author] “Supp.” 791 (Lyric poetry); βίος οὐδὲν ἔχων π. ἀλλὰ πάντα σμικρά 5th c.BC: Antipho Sophista 51; οὐ γὰρ π. οὐδὲν οὐδ᾽ ἔξω λόγου πέπονθας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 437; περισσότερα παθήματα 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.4.5; τὰ π. τῶν ἔργων καὶ τερατώδη 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.77}}; ἴδια καὶ π. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 15.145; π. καὶ θαυμαστά 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1141b6}}; πρᾶξις π. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pol.” 1312a27; οὐθὲν δὴ λέγοντες π. φαίνονταί τι λέγειν [prev. author] “Metaphorical” 1053b3}}; τί π. ποιεῖτ; {{NT+4th c.BC+|NT.Matt.5.47; περιττοτάτη φύσις 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 531a9}}; συνανθρωπίζον.. πάντων περισσότατον, of the dog, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 13.611c, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Clearchus Historicus 24}}; in Literature, **striking**, τὸ περιττόν, as a quality of οἱ τοῦ Σωκράτους λόγοι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1265a11}}; τὰ σοφὰ καὶ τὰ π. **refinements**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 409}}; opposed to κοινὸς καὶ δημώδης, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 40.2}} (but also, **elaborate**, π. καὶ πεποιημένος {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.4}}; in bad sense, **far-fetched**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “Epistula ad Pompeium” 2, “Dem.” 56).}}
**3** of persons, **extraordinary, remarkable**, especially for great learning, π. ὢν ἀνήρ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 948; τοὺς.. π. καί τι πράσσοντας πλέον [prev. author] “fragment” 788}}; δυστυχεῖς εἶναι τοὺς π. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 983a2; π. γένος τῶν μελιττῶν [prev. author] “GA” 760a4}}: frequently with the manner added, π. κατὰ φιλοσοφίαν {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pr.” 953a10}}; περὶ τὸν ἄλλον βίον περιττότερος **somewhat extravagant** or **eccentric**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pol.” 1267 b24}}; τῇ φύσει π. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. author] “HA” 622b6; κάλλει 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Demetrius” 2; ἐν ἅπασι [prev. author] “Dem.” 3; τὴν ὥραν 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 1.12}}: with _infinitive_, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 18.}}
**4** with _genitive_, περισσὸς ἄλλων πρός τι **beyond** others in.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 155}}; θύσει τοῦδε περισσότερα **greater things than** this, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 6.321 (Leon. 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor); περιττότερος προφήτου}}**one greater than..,** {{NT|NT.Matt.11.9.}}
**II** **more than sufficient, superfluous**, αἱ π. δαπάναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.6.6}}; περιττὸν ἔχειν to have **a surplus**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 7.6.31}}; οἱ μὲν.. περιττὰ ἔχουσιν, οἱ δὲ οὐδὲ τὰ ἀναγκαῖα.. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Oec.” 20.1: with _genitive_, τῶν ἀρκούντων περιττά}} **more than** sufficient, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 8.2.21; τὰ π. τῶν ἱκανῶν [prev. author] “Hier.” 1.19: frequently in military sense, οἱ π. ἱππεῖς}} the **reserve** horse, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Eq.Mag.” 8.14; οἱ π. τῆς φυλακῆς [prev. work] 7.7}}; π. σκηναί **spare** tents, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 4.6.12 (but τοῖς περιττοῖς χρήσεσθαι}} **their superior numbers**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 4.8.11, compare “Cyr.” 6.3.20)}}; τὸ π. the **surplus, residue**, Inscription cited in [same work]{{Refs|“An.” 5.3.13 (but τὸ π. τοῦ Ἰουδαίου}} the **advantage** of the Jew, {{NT|NT.Rom.3.1)}}; Ἁρπυιῶν τὰ π. their **leavings,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|Anthologia Graeca 11.239 (1st c.AD: Lucillius Epigrammaticus)}}; τὸ π. τῆς ἡμέρας the **remainder** of the day, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Xenophon Ephesius Scriptor Eroticus 1.3}}; π. γράμματα **supplementary** provisions in a will, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 326ii9 (2nd c.AD).}}
**II.2** in bad sense, **superfluous, useless**, οὐδέ τι τοῦ παντὸς κενεὸν πέλει οὐδὲ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 13}}; μόχθος π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 385, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 780; π. κἀνόνητα σώματα [prev. author] “Aj.” 758; βάρος π. γῆς ἀναστρωφώμενοι [prev. author] “fragment” 945; ἄχθος [prev. author] “El.” 1241 (Lyric poetry); τὰ γὰρ π. πανταχοῦ λυπήρ᾽ ἔπη [prev. author] “fragment” 82; αὐδῶ σε μὴ περισσὰ κηρύσσειν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 1048; π. πάντες οὑν μέσῳ λόγοι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 819; π. φωνῶν [prev. author] “Supp.” 459.}}
**II.3** **excessive, extravagant**, μηχανᾶσθαι περισσά commit **extravagances**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.32}}; περισσὰ δρᾶν, πράσσειν, to be **over**-busy, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 617, “Ant.” 68}}; π. φρονεῖν to be **over**-wise, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 924 (_anapaest meter_); ἡ π. αὕτη ἐπιμέλεια τοῦ σώματος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 407b}}; μῆκος πολὺ λόγων π. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lg.” 645c}}; **redundant, overdone**, οἱ καρτεροὶ καὶ π. λόγοι {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ax.” 365c, etc.}}; of dress, ἐσθὴς π. {{NT+1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.615d; περισσοτέρα λύπη NT.2Cor.2.7}}; τοῦ τὰ δέοντ᾽ ἔχειν περιττὰ μισῶ I hate **extravagance** in comparison with moderation, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 254, etc.}}
**II.4** of persons, **over-wise, over-curious**, περισσὸς καὶ φρονῶν μέγα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 445, compare “Ba.” 429 (Lyric poetry)}}; ὁ πολυπράγμων καὶ π. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 9.1.4}}; τὴν περὶ τὸ σῶμα θεραπείαν ἀκριβὴς καὶ π. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cicero” 8}}; so, of speakers, π. ἐν τοῖς λόγοις Δημοσθένης {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.119.}}
**II.5** as a term of praise, **subtle, acute**, ἀκριβὴς καὶ π. διάνοια {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 141b13.}}
**III** Arith., ἀριθμὸς π. an **odd, uneven** number, opposed to ἄρτιος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 170.7, 5th-6th c.BC: Philolaus Philosophus 5, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 356e, etc.; π. ἡμέραι 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 4.61}}; τὸ π. καὶ τὸ ἄρτιον the nature of **odd** and even, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 451c, etc.}}; π. χῶραι the **odd** places in a verse, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|7th c.AD: Hephaestio Grammaticus 5.1; ἀρτιάκις π. ἀριθμός a number divisible by an odd number an even number of times, as 2, 6, 10, 3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra 7 “Def.” 9.}}
**IV** περισσότεροι **more in number, extra**, Carnead. cited in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 9.140.}}
**V** περιττόν, τό, ={στρύχνος μανικός, θρύον}{{Refs 4th c.BC+|11, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 9.11.6; περισσόν 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.73; περίσκον 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 12.8.56.}}
**B** _adverb_ περισσῶς **extraordinarily, exceedingly**, θεοσεβέεες π. ἐόντες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.37}}; ἐπαινέσεται π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1197 (Lyric poetry)}}; π. παῖδας ἐκδιδάσκεσθαι to have them educated **overmuch**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Med.” 295}}; περιττοτέρως τῶν ἄλλων **far above** all others, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.44; περισσότερον τοῦ ἑνός 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Pr.Im.” 14; also περισσά 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.43, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 579, etc.}}
**B.2** **remarkably**, περισσότερον τῶν ἄλλων θάψαι τινά **more sumptuously**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.129; οἴκησις π. ἐσκευασμένη 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.29.7}}; περιττότατα ἔχειν to be **most remarkable**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 589a31; κοσμουμένη π. καὶ σεμνῶς 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.145e}}; περισσότατα ἀνθρώπων θρησκεύειν **in the most singular way**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 37.17}}; ἡδέως καὶ π. **in an uncommon manner**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 3}}; εἰπεῖν στρογγύλως καὶ π. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Is.” 20}}; ἰδίως καὶ π. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Theseus” 19}}; τὰ καινῶς ἱστορούμενα καὶ π. {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.30d.}}
**B.3** **abundantly**, ἐχέτω π. τῆς κρόκης {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 3.41.}}
**B.4** with a _negative_, οὐδὲν περισσὸν τούτων nothing **more than** or **beyond** these, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.4.6; οὐδὲν τῶν ἄλλων περιττότερον πραγματεύεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 20c}}; οὐδὲν π. ἢ εἰ.. no **otherwise** than if.., {{Refs|[prev. author] “Smp.” 219c}}; περισσόν alone, **furthermore**, {{LXX|LXX.Eccl.12.12, +others}}
**B.5** τὰ περισσά **in vain,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Anthologia Graeca 12.182 (2nd c.AD: Strato Epigrammaticus).}}
**B.II** ἐκ περιττοῦ **superfluously, uselessly**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 338c, “Sph.” 265e}}; but ὑπερέχειν ἐκ π. to be **far** superior, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lg.” 734d, compare 802d}}; ἡ κάμινος ἐκαύθη ἐκ π. {{LXX+2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Theodotion LXX.Dan.3.22; ἐκ π. χρησάμενος τῇ παρρησίᾳ 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Pro Merc.Cond.” 13; compare ὑπερεκπερισσοῦ.}}

