**4** with _genitive_, περισσὸς ἄλλων πρός τι **beyond** others in.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 155}}; θύσει τοῦδε περισσότερα **greater things than** this, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 6.321 (Leon. 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor); περιττότερος προφήτου}}**one greater than..,** {{NT|NT.Matt.11.9.}}
**II** **more than sufficient, superfluous**, αἱ π. δαπάναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.6.6}}; περιττὸν ἔχειν to have **a surplus**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 7.6.31}}; οἱ μὲν.. περιττὰ ἔχουσιν, οἱ δὲ οὐδὲ τὰ ἀναγκαῖα.. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Oec.” 20.1: with _genitive_, τῶν ἀρκούντων περιττά}} **more than** sufficient, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 8.2.21; τὰ π. τῶν ἱκανῶν [prev. author] “Hier.” 1.19: frequently in military sense, οἱ π. ἱππεῖς}} the **reserve** horse, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Eq.Mag.” 8.14; οἱ π. τῆς φυλακῆς [prev. work] 7.7}}; π. σκηναί **spare** tents, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 4.6.12 (but τοῖς περιττοῖς χρήσεσθαι}} **their superior numbers**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 4.8.11, compare “Cyr.” 6.3.20)}}; τὸ π. the **surplus, residue**, Inscription cited in [same work]{{Refs|“An.” 5.3.13 (but τὸ π. τοῦ Ἰουδαίου}} the **advantage** of the Jew, {{NT|NT.Rom.3.1)}}; Ἁρπυιῶν τὰ π. their **leavings,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|Anthologia Graeca 11.239 (1st c.AD: Lucillius Epigrammaticus)}}; τὸ π. τῆς ἡμέρας the **remainder** of the day, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Xenophon Ephesius Scriptor Eroticus 1.3}}; π. γράμματα **supplementary** provisions in a will, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 326ii9 (2nd c.AD).}}
**II.2** in bad sense, **superfluous, useless**, οὐδέ τι τοῦ παντὸς κενεὸν πέλει οὐδὲ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 13}}; μόχθος π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 385, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 780; π. κἀνόνητα σώματα [prev. author] “Aj.” 758; βάρος π. γῆς ἀναστρωφώμενοι [prev. author] “fragment” 945; ἄχθος [prev. author] “El.” 1241 (Lyric poetry); τὰ γὰρ π. πανταχοῦ λυπήρ᾽ ἔπη [prev. author] “fragment” 82; αὐδῶ σε μὴ περισσὰ κηρύσσειν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 1048; π. πάντες οὑν μέσῳ λόγοι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 819; π. φωνῶν [prev. author] “Supp.” 459.}}
**II.3** **excessive, extravagant**, μηχανᾶσθαι περισσά commit **extravagances**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.32}}; περισσὰ δρᾶν, πράσσειν, to be **over**-busy, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 617, “Ant.” 68}}; π. φρονεῖν to be **over**-wise, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 924 (_anapaest meter_); ἡ π. αὕτη ἐπιμέλεια τοῦ σώματος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 407b}}; μῆκος πολὺ λόγων π. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lg.” 645c}}; **redundant, overdone**, οἱ καρτεροὶ καὶ π. λόγοι {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ax.” 365c, etc.}}; of dress, ἐσθὴς π. {{NT+1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.615d; περισσοτέρα λύπη NT.2Cor.2.7}}; τοῦ τὰ δέοντ᾽ ἔχειν περιττὰ μισῶ I hate **extravagance** in comparison with moderation, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 254, etc.}}
**II.4** of persons, **over-wise, over-curious**, περισσὸς καὶ φρονῶν μέγα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 445, compare “Ba.” 429 (Lyric poetry)}}; ὁ πολυπράγμων καὶ π. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 9.1.4}}; τὴν περὶ τὸ σῶμα θεραπείαν ἀκριβὴς καὶ π. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cicero” 8}}; so, of speakers, π. ἐν τοῖς λόγοις Δημοσθένης {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.119.}}
**II.5** as a term of praise, **subtle, acute**, ἀκριβὴς καὶ π. διάνοια {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 141b13.}}
**III** Arith., ἀριθμὸς π. an **odd, uneven** number, opposed to ἄρτιος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 170.7, 5th-6th c.BC: Philolaus Philosophus 5, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 356e, etc.; π. ἡμέραι 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 4.61}}; τὸ π. καὶ τὸ ἄρτιον the nature of **odd** and even, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 451c, etc.}}; π. χῶραι the **odd** places in a verse, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|7th c.AD: Hephaestio Grammaticus 5.1; ἀρτιάκις π. ἀριθμός a number divisible by an odd number an even number of times, as 2, 6, 10, 3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra 7 “Def.” 9.}}
**IV** περισσότεροι **more in number, extra**, Carnead. cited in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 9.140.}}
**V** περιττόν, τό, ={στρύχνος μανικός, θρύον}{{Refs 4th c.BC+|11, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 9.11.6; περισσόν 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.73; περίσκον 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 12.8.56.}}
**B** _adverb_ περισσῶς **extraordinarily, exceedingly**, θεοσεβέεες π. ἐόντες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.37}}; ἐπαινέσεται π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1197 (Lyric poetry)}}; π. παῖδας ἐκδιδάσκεσθαι to have them educated **overmuch**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Med.” 295}}; περιττοτέρως τῶν ἄλλων **far above** all others, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.44; περισσότερον τοῦ ἑνός 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Pr.Im.” 14; also περισσά 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.43, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 579, etc.}}
**B.2** **remarkably**, περισσότερον τῶν ἄλλων θάψαι τινά **more sumptuously**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.129; οἴκησις π. ἐσκευασμένη 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.29.7}}; περιττότατα ἔχειν to be **most remarkable**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 589a31; κοσμουμένη π. καὶ σεμνῶς 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.145e}}; περισσότατα ἀνθρώπων θρησκεύειν **in the most singular way**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 37.17}}; ἡδέως καὶ π. **in an uncommon manner**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 3}}; εἰπεῖν στρογγύλως καὶ π. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Is.” 20}}; ἰδίως καὶ π. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Theseus” 19}}; τὰ καινῶς ἱστορούμενα καὶ π. {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.30d.}}
**B.3** **abundantly**, ἐχέτω π. τῆς κρόκης {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 3.41.}}
**B.4** with a _negative_, οὐδὲν περισσὸν τούτων nothing **more than** or **beyond** these, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.4.6; οὐδὲν τῶν ἄλλων περιττότερον πραγματεύεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 20c}}; οὐδὲν π. ἢ εἰ.. no **otherwise** than if.., {{Refs|[prev. author] “Smp.” 219c}}; περισσόν alone, **furthermore**, {{LXX|LXX.Eccl.12.12, +others}}
**B.5** τὰ περισσά **in vain,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Anthologia Graeca 12.182 (2nd c.AD: Strato Epigrammaticus).}}
**B.II** ἐκ περιττοῦ **superfluously, uselessly**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 338c, “Sph.” 265e}}; but ὑπερέχειν ἐκ π. to be **far** superior, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lg.” 734d, compare 802d}}; ἡ κάμινος ἐκαύθη ἐκ π. {{LXX+2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Theodotion LXX.Dan.3.22; ἐκ π. χρησάμενος τῇ παρρησίᾳ 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Pro Merc.Cond.” 13; compare ὑπερεκπερισσοῦ.}}

4059:
** περιτέμνω**,
_Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ **περι-τάμνω**, **cut** or **clip round about**, οἴνας περιταμνέμεν **prune** them, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 570; [τὴν κεφαλὴν] π. κύκλῳ περὶ τὰ ὦτα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.64}}; of a goldsmith, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“CPR” 22.6 (2nd c.AD):}} —_middle_, βραχίονας περιτάμνονται **make incisions all over their** arms, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.71}}:—_passive_, **to be cut up**, of fish, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Mirabilia” 835a19.}}
**2** of circumcision, τῶν ἐπιγινομένων οὐ περιτάμνουσι τὰ αἰδοῖα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.104, compare 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 79.11}}; π. τοὺς παῖδας {{LXX+3rd c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.28, cf. LXX.Josh.5.2, +others, “PCair.Zen.” 76.13 (3rd c.BC)}}, etc.:— _middle_, περιτάμνονται τὰ αἰδοῖα {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.36, 104, compare 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 3.32; περιετέμοντο τὴν σάρκα LXX.Gen.34.24}}: _absolutely_, **practise circumcision**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.104}}:—_passive_, {{LXX|LXX.Gen.17.10, +others}}
**3** **cut off** the extremities, τὰ ὦτα καὶ τὴν ῥῖνα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.162; τοὺς μαστούς 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 62.7; τὰ περιττά 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Anach.” 20:}} —_passive_, περιτάμνεσθαι γῆν **to be curtailed of** certain land, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.159; πᾶσαν.. περιτεμνόμενον σοφίαν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 473 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**II** **cut off and hem in all round**:—_middle_, βοῦς περιταμνόμενον **cutting off** cattle **for oneself, 'lifling'** cattle, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.402, 24.112}}:—_passive_, **to be cut off**, ἅρματα π. ὑπὸ τῶν ἱππέων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.4.8.}}

4060:
** περιτίθημι**,
**place** or **put round, put on**, περὶ δὲ ξύλα θῆκαν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.308}}; δέραισι περθέτω (_Aeolic dialect_).. ὐποθύμιδας {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 36; π. κυνέην τινί 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.162; στεφάνους τινί [prev. author] 6.69; πιλίδια περὶ τὴν κεφαλήν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 406d; χρυσόν [prev. work] 420e}}; φωνήεσί τε καὶ ἀφώνοις π. γράμματα **attach** letters to.., {{LXX+4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Cra.” 393e, compare 414c; π. σφαῖραν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Caelo” 285b3; σκληρὸν περὶ τὸ σαρκῶδες περιέθηκεν ἡ φύσις [prev. author] “PA” 685a8; δέρματα ἐπὶ τοὺς βραχίονας LXX.Gen.27.16; περίβολον τῷ τεμένει “IG” 12(9).906.7 (Chalcis, 3rd c.AD)}}:—_middle_, **put round oneself, put on**, περὶ δὲ τρυφάλειαν.. κρατὶ θέτο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.381; περὶ δὲ ξίφος ὀξὺ θέτ᾽ ὤμῳ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.3, 4.308}}; περθέμενον χλάμυν (_Aeolic dialect_ for περι-) probably in {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 64; π. στέφανον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 984 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 380, +others; στρεπτόν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.4.6; σκευήν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 53d; δακτύλιον [prev. author] “R.” 360b; βασιλείαν “OGI” 90.44 (Rosetta, 2nd c.BC)}}; διάδημα αὐτὸς περιεθήκατο {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Μιθριδάτειος” 67.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **bestow, confer upon**, π. τινὶ βασιληΐην, κράτος, ἐλευθερίην, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.129, 3.81, 142, 4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 100}}; πόλει τὸ κάλλιστον ὄνομα, τινὶ δόξαν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.87, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.149, etc.}}; π. τινὶ ὄνειδος, ἀτιμίαν, **put** reproach, dishonour **upon** him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.18, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.89; πίστιν τισί 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.103; συμφοράν 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.3.1}}; Μηδικὴν ἀχὴν τοῖς Ἕλλησι **put** the Median yoke **round** their necks, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.43}}; ὁ πυκτικὸς.. οὐ πᾶσι τὴν αὐτὴν μάχην π. **does** not **prescribe**.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1180b11}}; τῇ Ἀθηνᾷ τὴν τέχνην **ascribe**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pol.” 1341b8; [ταῖς πράξεσι] μέγεθος π. καὶ κάλλος [prev. author] “Rh.” 1368a29}}:— _middle_, **assume**, ἰσχὺν ἑαυτῷ {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 252; σχῆμα ἀλλότριον 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.19.28.}}
**II.2** reversely, π. τινὰ ὕβρει **envelop** him with.., {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 6.33.}}

4061:
** περιτομ-ή**, ἡ,
**circumcision**, {{LXX+2nd c.BC+|LXX.Gen.17.13, 2nd c.BC: Agatharchides Geographus 61, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 16.2.37 (_plural_), 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.450, +others, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 2.82: _metaphorically_, π. καρδίας NT.Rom.2.29.}}
**II** **section** of a machine, ἡ κάτω π. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollodorus Damascenus Mechanicus “Abhandlungen der Göttinger Gesellschaft der Wissenschaften (Phil.-hist. Klasse)” 173.4.}}
**III** **circular incision**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Heliodorus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 45.6.9, 45.7.3, 6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 15.8.}}

4062:
** περιτρέπω**,
**turn and bring round, divert**, τὴν ἀναθυμίασιν εἴσω -τρέπεσθαι **is diverted** inwards, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 367a32; μὴ βούλεσθε εἰς ὑμᾶς τὴν αἰτίαν -τρέψαι 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 6.13; γυναῖκάς φασι τοῖς ἀνδράσι περιτρέπειν τὰ σφέτερ᾽ αὐτῶν ἁμαρτήματα 2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor 2.420 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus; εἰς τοὐναντίον τὸν λόγον 4th c.AD: Eusebius Myndius Philosophus 2; ἐπὶ θάτερα 2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor 1.112 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus; τὸ σφάλμα εἰς ἄλλο μακρῷ αἰσιώτερον περιετράπη 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Laps.” 15; ὁ λόγος εἰς ὄνειδος -τέτραπται 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1036f}}; π. τινὰ εἰς μανίαν, εἰς χαράν, {{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.Act.26.24, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 9.4.4; εἰς ἄλγημα 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.26 (_passive_): with}}_infinitive_, τοὐναντίον π. τιμηνύειν **bring** a thing **round** to signify the opposite, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 418b.}}
**2** **turn upside down, upset**, χειμὼν π. τὴν ναῦν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Cont.” 7}}; περιτραπεὶς Ὀδυσσεύς **capsized**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.831d}}; τῇ ῥύμῃ τοῦ ἵππου τινὰ π. {{LXX|[prev. author] “Marc.” 7: _metaphorically_, [ὕψος] περιέτρεψεν ἢ χρόνος τις ἢ φθόνος “Tragica Adespota” 547.4; π. θρόνους δικαστῶν LXX.Wis.5.23}}; π. εἰς κακοτροφίαν **pervert**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Athenaeus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus (?) 21.1}}; μή τις.. βασκανία π. τὸν λόγον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 95b}}; **refute**, π. σεαυτόν {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ax.” 37o a, cf. Aps. p.278}} H.:—_passive_, τὸ παράδειγμα περιτέτραπται {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “JTr.” 49}}; of a person, **collapse in argument**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ σημείων καὶ σημειώσεων” 29}}; **refute oneself**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 3.35; περιτρέπεται ὁ λόγος 5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 7.}}
**3** **turn away from**, δούλι᾽ ἔργα καὶ δύην π. {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC(?): Semonides Iambographus 7.58 (nisi to be read περιτρέμει).}}
**4** _passive_, **to be folded over**, of skin, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “de Usu Partium” 3.12.}}
**5** περιτρέπεται· ἰλιγγιᾷ, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**II** _intransitive_ in _aorist_ 2, **turn** or **go round**, περὶ δ᾽ ἔτραπον ὧραι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.469.}}

4063:
** περιτρέχω**,
_future_ - θρέξομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 193, - δρᾰμοῦμαι [prev. author] “V.” 138}}: _aorist_ περιέδρᾰμον {{Refs|[prev. author] “Eq.” 56}}; but _infinitive_ - θρέξαι {{Refs|[prev. author] “Th.” 657}}: _perfect_ - δεδράμηκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Clitopho” 41o a, -δέδρομα (see. below 11.1b)}}:—**run round and round**, τὸ δῶμα π., said by a drunken man, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 505; π. τὰ κυνίδια 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 13.8; π. δεῦρο 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 138}}; π. εἰς ταὐτόν **come round, return** to the same point, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 200c, compare “Clit.” [prev. cited]}}
**2** **run about everywhere**, οἱ δὲ πυππάζουσι περιτρέχοντες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 52; κύκλῳ π. καὶ βοᾶν 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 174.1; π. ὅπῃ τύχοιμι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 173a, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 30.21}}: generally, **to be in motion, circulate**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.3.24}}; ἡ περιτρέχουσα ὑγρότης flexibility **of movement**, of a surgeon operating, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.67e.}}
**3**_metaphorically_, **to be current, in vogue**, ταῦτα περιτρέχοντα πᾶσι προσφέρεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 202a}}; ἡ περιτρέχουσα ἑταιρεία **common** society, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ep.” 333e}}; ὀνόματα περιτρέχοντα **current**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Dinarcho” 2}}; τέχνη περιτρέχουσα, of Rhetoric, **comprehensive** art, **ars circumcurrens**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Quintilianus Rhetor Latinus “Inst.” 2.21.7.}}
**II** with _accusative_, **run round**, τὴν λίμνην κύκλῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 193}}; **run round searching**, τὴν Πύκνα πᾶσαν {{Refs|[prev. author] “Th.” 657}}; **run up to from all sides**, τὸν δὲ βληθέντα περιέδραμε ὅμιλος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.128.}}
**II.b** of things, especially in _perfect_ -δέδρομα, **encompass, surround**, περιδέδρομεν ἅψεα νοῦσος {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 3.676; φήμη κακὴ -δέδρομεν αὐτούς 4th c.AD(?): Manetho Astrologus 2.298; ὠκεανὸς π. γαῖαν 2nd c.AD: Dionysius Periegeta 41, compare 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus “Epigrammata” 4.5.}}
**II.2** _metaphorically_, **circumvent, take in**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 56.}}

4064:
** περιφέρω**,
**carry round**, τὸν ὀϊστὸν περιέφερε κατὰ πᾶσαν γῆν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.36}}; **carry about** with one, {{Refs|[prev. work] 64}}; παῖδ᾽ ἀγκάλαισι π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 464, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Σαμία” 29; τὴν γαλῆν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 128}}; ὀκλαδίαν probably in {{Refs|[prev. author] “Eq.” 1385:}} —_passive_, with _accusative_ loci, περιενειχθέντος τοῦ λέοντος τὸ τεῖχος **being carried round** the wall, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.84: _absolutely_, Σωκράτη.. περιφερόμενον}} **swinging about** (in a basket), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 19c; πίνειν.. σκύφον περιφερόμενον 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1324b18.}}
**2** **move round**, π. τὸν πόδα **bring** the foot **round** in mounting a horse, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 7.2}}; **hand round** at table, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 2.2.2, +others (_active_ and _passive_)}}; τὸ βλέμμα π. εἰς τοὺς παρόντας {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Agis” 18; π. κλήρους [prev. author] 2.737d (_passive_).}}
**3** **turn round**, τὴν κεφαλήν {{Refs|[prev. author] “Marc.” 20:}} —_middle_, τὰ σκέλη π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 190a.}}
**3.b** in Tactics, **wheel**, τοῦ συντάγματος περιενεχθέντος {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Asclepiodotus Tacticus “Tact.” 10.4, compare 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Tactica - Griechische Kriegsschriftsteller” 25.5.}}
**3.c** _intransitive_, **turn round**, ὡς ὁ στεφὼν περιφέρει κύκλῳ {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“GDI” 5597.8 (Ephesus, 3rd c.BC).}}
**4** **carry round, publish, make known**, π. τι πανταχόσε {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.8o f:}} —_passive_, τοῦ Πιττακοῦ.. περιεφέρετο τοῦτο τὸ ῥῆμα **was passed from mouth to mouth**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 343b, compare “R.” 402a, 402c, “Demod.” 383c; ὁ περιφερόμενος στίχος 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.9.4, etc.}}; of a person, περιενεχθῆναι εὐνοίᾳ καὶ θαυμασθῆναι παρὰ τοῖς Ἀθηναίοις {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “Academicorum Index” p.75 M.}}
**5** **carry to and fro**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Caesar” 37, compare below 111.2.}}
**6** **bring round in the end, determine, reduce, subject**, περιήνεγκεν εἰς ἑαυτὸν τὰς Ἀθήνας {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] “Per.” 15, compare “Galb.” 8; τὴν Ἰταλίαν π. ἐς λιμόν 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 5.143}}; εἰς συμφορὰς π. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. author] “Pun.” 86; εἰς ἀπάθειαν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.165b}}, compare 546c:—_passive_, ἐς Ῥωμαίους πάντα περιηνέχθη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Μιθριδάτειος” 68; τὸ σπέρμα ἐς θῆλυ περιηνέχθη 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Genit.” 6.}}
**7** **carry round** or **back** (in memory), οὔτε μέμνημαι τὸ πρῆγμα οὔτε με περιφέρει οὐδὲν εἰδέναι τούτων nor **does** any of these things **carry** me **back** to the knowledge of it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.86. β; π. τίς με καὶ μνήμη 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 180e; τοῦ πράγματος ἤδη -φέροντος αὐτὸν τῇ ὑπονοίᾳ 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.522c.}}
**8** **turn round, make dizzy, turn mad**, ἡ συκοφαντία π. σοφόν {{LXX|LXX.Eccl.7.8(7)}}:—_passive_, **to be turned giddy**, - φερόμενος τῷ μεγέθει τῶν τολμωμένων {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Caesar” 32; ψυχὴ δυνάμει -φερομένη [prev. author] “Dio” 11; κακοῦ μεγέθει -φερόμενος 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 17.5.2.}}
**9** **transfer, refer, shift**, τὴν ἀπορίαν ἐς τοὺς δικαστάς {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 1.54.}}
**II** _intransitive_, **survive, endure, hold out**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.28, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 9.12.1, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 17.6.1}}: also with _accusative_, **survive, outlast**, ἡμέραν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 2.149; τὰς εἰδούς [prev. work] 153.}}
**II.2** **come round, recover**, ἐκ τῆς νόσου {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus, 10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus}}
**III** _passive_, **go round, rotate**, ἐν τῷ αὐτῷ κύκλῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 138c; πάντα -φερόμενα ὁρᾶν 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 4.156c; ἐνιαυτοῦ -φερομένου 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.72}}; ἐν ἴσῳ χρόνῳ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Caelo” 290a5; κύκλος τῶν ἀνθρωπηΐων πρηγμάτων -φερόμενος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.207}}; of argument, εἰς ταὐτὸ π. ἀεί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 517c, compare “Lg.” 659d; εἰς τὰ πρότερα [prev. author] “R.” 456b.}}
**III.2** **wander about**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 3.5; λόγος.. ἀνοήτως π. ἐν συμποσίῳ 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.716f}}; **to be unstable**, ἡ περιφερομένη εἱμαρμένη {{Refs|[prev. author] “Aem.” 27, compare “Galb.” 6}}; περιφερόμενοι τύπτουσι **at random**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 985a14.}}

4065:
** περιφρον-έω**,
**compass in thought, speculate about**, τὸν ἥλιον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 225; τὰ πράγματα [prev. work] 741.}}
**II** **contemn, despise**, τινας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.25; τὰ δαιμόνια 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 1.71, etc.: with _genitive_, τοῦ ζῆν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Axiochus” 372b; τοῦ πιθανοῦ 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Theseus” 1, compare 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Tact. Praef.” 3, etc.}}: _metaphorically_, of diseases or difficult patients, **defy** remedies, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor Trall. 1.15, 2nd c.AD: Archigenes Medicus cited in 6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 6.8}}:— _passive_, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 4.5.2.}}
**III** _intransitive_, **to be very thoughtful**, οὐ περιφρονοῦσα ἡλικία {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Axiochus” 365b (assuming variant).}}

4069:
1. **Persian **, {{6th/5th c.BC|Aeschulus Tragicus}}, etc.
2. as _Substantive_, (.] (entry γῆ); **Persis, Persia **, now **Farsistan **, {{Refs 5th c.BC|Herdotus Historicus}}
3. (entry γυνή); **a Persian woman **, {{5th/4th c.BC|Xenophon Historicus}}
4. (entry χλαῖνα); **a Persian cloak **, {{5th/4th c.BC|Aristophanes Comicus}}
(Middle Liddel)

4072:
** πέτομαι**,
_2nd pers._ _singular_ πέτεαι {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Anacreon Lyricus 9:}}_imperfect_ ἐπετόμην, _Epic dialect_ πετ- {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.366, etc.}}: _future_ πετήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 77, compare 1126 (ἀπο-)}}; shortened πτήσομαι (ἐκ-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “V.” 208, and always in early Prose, (ἀνα-) 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 905a, +others, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.209, (ἐπι-) 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.15 (mostly in compounds, but πτήσεσθαι in later Prose, 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 2.27):}}_aorist_ ἐπτόμην, _infinitive_ πτέσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 17}}; elsewhere in compounds, (ἐπι-) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.126, (ἀν-) 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator “fragment” 58, etc.}}; frequently also ἐπτάμην, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.592, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 18, (παρ-) 7th-6th c.BC(?): Semonides Iambographus 13, (ἐς-) 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.100}}; _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ πτάτο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.880}}_infinitive_ πτάσθαι (δια-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1}}, _participle_ πτάμενος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.282, 22.362, etc. (in codices of 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus forms of ἐπτόμην}} in compounds predominate over those from ἐπτάμη; δι-έπτατο is found in codices of {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1086, ἐκ-πτόμενος}} followed by κατ-έπτατο [{{Refs|[prev. author] “Av.” 788 {πετόντεσσι}}}]; ἀν-επτάμαν is probably in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 693 (Lyric poetry), προσ-έπτατο [prev. work] 382)}}; _subjunctive_ πτῆται for πτᾶται, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.170: also}}_aorist_ of _active_ form ἔπτην, ἔπτης, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“IG” 14.2550, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Trag.” 218, ἔπτη Batrachomyomachia 208, 4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 2.223, +others, “Anacreontea” 22.3; _optative_ πταίης “Anthologia Graeca” 5.151 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus)}}; _participle_ πτᾶσα {{Refs|“hymnus ad Cererem” 398, Hdn.Gr. 1.532}}; elsewhere only in compounds, (δι-) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“IG” 3.1386, (ἐξ-) 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 98, (ἀν-) 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1307, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 440, (προς-) 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 115, (ὑπερ-) 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 113 (Trag. only in Lyric poetry):}}_perfect_ πέπτηκα only as a coinage in {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-5th c.AD: Choeroboscus Grammaticus “Scholia in Theodosii Canones” 2.79, elsewhere πεπότημαι (see. ποτάομαι)}}: _aorist_ _passive_ ἐπετάσθην {{LXX+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 624b6 (εἰς-), LXX.Psa.17(18).10, LXX.Hos.9.11 (ἐξ-), 1st-2nd c.AD: Sotion Paradoxographus p.186 W., 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 4.77 (ἐξ-)}}: _future_ _passive_ πετασθήσομαι {{LXX|LXX.Hab.1.8.}}—The only _present_ in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus and _Attic dialect_ Prose is πέτομαι}}; **πέταμαι** is used by {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 10.8, 4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 30, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 8.90, “N.” 6.48, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 90 (_anapaest meter_), “Anthologia Graeca” 11.208 (1st c.AD: Lucillius Epigrammaticus)}}, and in later Prose, as {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Incessu Animalium” 709b10, “HA” 609a14 (περι-), cf. Moer. p.311}} P.; noted as archaic by {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Pseudol.” 29}}: _aorist_ _imperative_ πέτασσαι {{Refs|“Anacreontea” 14.2}}; **ἵπταμαι** (which see) is first found in late writers, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Moschus Bucolicus 3.43, 2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 65.4, etc.}} (mostly in compounds, compare ἐξίπταμα; ἀφίπτατο in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1608 is spurious)}}, and is censured by {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Lex.” 25, “Sol.” 7}}:—**fly**, of birds, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.207, 13.62, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.147, etc.}}; of bees, gnats, etc., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.89, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.95}}; of a departing spirit, ψυχὴ ἐκ ῥεθέων πταμένη Ἄϊδόσδε βεβήκει {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.362; ἐκ μελέων θυμὸς πτάτο 23.880}}: _metaphorically_, of young children, οὐδέπω μακρὰν πτέσθαι σθενοντες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 17}}; also of arrows, javelins, etc., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.99, etc.; ὀλοοίτροχος.. ἀναθρῴσκων π. 13.140 (but ἐκ χειρῶν ἔπτατ᾽ ἐρετμά, τεύχεα}} **fell suddenly**.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.203, 24.534)}}; of any quick motion, **dart, rush**, of men, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.755, 22.143, etc.}}; of horses, μάστιξεν δ᾽ ἐλάαν, τὼ δ᾽ οὐκ ἀέκοντε πετέσθην {{Refs|5.366, compare 768}}, etc.; of chariots, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 308}}; of dancers, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 71 (Lyric poetry)}}; πέτον **fly!** i.e. **make haste!** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 321}}; ἔχρην πετομένας ἥκειν πάλαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 55; πολλοὶ ἥξουσι πετόμενοι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 567d, compare 467d}}; πέτονται.. ἐπὶ ταῦτ᾽ ἄκλητοι, of parasites, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 229.}}
**II** _metaphorically_ and proverbial usages:—**to be on the wing, flutter**, of uncertain hopes, ἐξ ἐλπίδος π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 8.90}}; π. ἐλπίσιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 487 (Lyric poetry)}}; of fickle natures, πέτει τε καὶ φρονῶν οὐδὲν φρονεῖς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 332}}; ἐφ᾽ ἕτερον π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 899}}; ὄρνις πετόμενος a bird **ever on the wing**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Av.” 169}}; πετόμενόν τινα διώκεις 'you are chasing a butterfly', {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 4a, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1009b38}}; of fame, **fly abroad**, πέταται τηλόθεν ὄνυμ᾽ αὐτῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 6.48.}}
**II.2** with _dative_, πτάμενος νοήματι **flying** in mind, {{Refs|[prev. author] “fragment” 122.4. (Cf. πίπτω}}, Sanskrit_pátati_ 'fly', 'fall', Latin _prae-pes_, etc.)

4073:
** πέτρα**,
_Ionic dialect_ and _Epic dialect_ **πέτρη**, ἡ, **rock**; frequently of **cliffs, ledges**, etc. by the sea, λισσὴ αἰπεῖά τε εἰς ἅλα πέτρη {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.293, compare 4.501, etc.}}; χῶρος λεῖος πετράων free from **rocks**, of a beach,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|5.443; π. ἠλίβατος.. ἁλὸς ἐγγὺς ἐοῦσα 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.618, etc.}}; χοιρὰς π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 10.52}}; also, **rocky peak** or **ridge**, αἰγίλιψ π. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.15, etc.; ἠλίβατος 16.35, etc.}}; λιττὰς π. {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica “Supp.” 1.30, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 796 (Lyric poetry)}}; π. Λευκάς, Ὠλενίη, etc., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.11, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.757, etc.}}; π. σύνδρομοι, Συμπληγάδες, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.209, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1264 (Lyric poetry)}}; πρὸς πέτραις ὑψηλοκρήμνοις, of Caucasus, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 4, compare 31, 56, +others}}; π. Δελφίς, π. δίλοφος, of Parnassus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 464 (Lyric poetry), “Ant.” 1126 (Lyric poetry); π. Κωρυκίς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 22}}; π. Κεκροπία, of the Acropolis, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 936.}}
**2** π. γλαφυρή a hollow **rock**, i.e. a cave, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.88, compare 4.107}}; σπέος κοιλῇ ὑπὸ π. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 301}}; δίστομος π. **cave in the rock** with a double entrance, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 16, compare 937}}; κατηρεφεῖς αὐτῇ τῇ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 116b; π. ἀντρώδης 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.3.11; τόπος κύκλῳ πέτραις περιεχόμενος “IG” 42(1).122.21 (Epid.)}}; ἕως τῆς π. down to **virgin rock,** {{NT+3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Zenon Papyri du Caire 172.14 (3rd c.BC), “OGI” 672 (Egypt, 1st c.AD),cf. NT.Matt.16.18.}}
**3** **mass of rock** or **boulder**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.243, 484, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 675; πέτρας κυλινδομένα φλόξ 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.23; ἐκυλίνδουν πέτρας 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.2.20, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.53.4.}}
**4** **stone** as material, π. λαρτία, Τηΐα, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“SIG” 581.97 (Crete, 3rd-2nd c.BC), 996.13 (Smyrna, 1st c.AD): distinguished from πέτρος (which see)}}, which is variant in X.[same place]; πέτρᾳ should be read in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 272}}; the distinction is minimized by {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 12.194.}}
**II** proverbial, οὐκ ἀπὸ δρυὸς οὐδ᾽ ἀπὸ πέτρης, etc. (see. δρῦς); as a symbol of firmness, ὁ δ᾽ ἐστάθη ἠΰτε π. ἔμπεδον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.463}}; of hard-heartedness, ἐκ πέτρας εἰργασμένος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 244; ἁλίαν π. ἢ κῦμα λιταῖς ὢς ἱκετεύων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 537 (_anapaest meter_); compare πέτρος 1.2. (Written πε-τε-ρα}} in a text with musical accompaniment, {{Refs|“Paean Delphicus [2nd c.BC]” 5.)}}

4074:
** πέτρος**, ὁ
(in later Poets ἡ, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 7.274 (1st c.BC(?): Honestus Epigrammaticus), 479 (3rd c.BC: Theodoridas Epigrammaticus))}}, **stone** (distinguished from πέτρα, which see); in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, used by warriors, λάζετο πέτρον μάρμαρον ὀκριόεντα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.734; βαλὼν μυλοειδέϊ πέτρῳ 7.270, compare 20.288, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 1128 (never in 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey”); ἔδικε πέτρῳ 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).72; ἄγαλμ᾽ Ἀΐδα ξεστὸν π. ἔμβαλον στέρνῳ [prev. author] “N.” 10.67; νιφάδι γογγύλων πέτρων 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 199.7; ἐκ χερῶν πέτροισιν ἠράσσοντο [prev. author] “Pers.” 460; λευσθῆναι πέτροις 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 435; πέτρους ἐπεκυλίνδουν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.5.20, etc.}}; ἐν πέτροισι πέτρον ἐκτρίβων, to produce fire, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 296}}; of a **boulder** forming a landmark, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. author] “OC” 1595; τόνδ᾽ ἀνέθηκα π. ἀειράμενος “IG” 42(1).125 (Epid., 3rd c.BC).}}
**2** proverbial, πάντα κινῆσαι πέτρον 'leave no stone unturned', {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 1002, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 843a}}; of imperturbability, καὶ γὰρ ἂν πέτρου φύσιν σύ γ᾽ ὀργάνειας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 334, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 28.}}
**II** a kind of **reed,** {{Refs|Peripl.M.Rubr. 65.}} —The usual Prose word is λίθος.

4077:
** πηγή**,
_Doric dialect_ **πᾱγά**, ἡ, **running water**, used by {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} always in _plural_, **streams**, πηγαὶ ποταμῶν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.9, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.189, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 89, 434 (Lyric poetry), “Pers.” 311, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1297, “Rh.” 827 (Lyric poetry); κρουνὼ δ᾽ ἵκανον καλλιρρόω, ἔνθα δὲ πηγαὶ δοιαὶ ἀναΐσσουσι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.147: _sing._, καλλιρρόου ἔψαυσα π. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 202, compare 613.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, of tears, πηγαὶ κλαυμάτων, δακρύων, **streams**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ag.” 888, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 803, “Tr.” 852 (Lyric poetry): _absolutely_, παρειὰν νοτίοις ἔτεγξα παγαῖς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 402 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 1068, etc.}}; also πηγαὶ γάλακτος, βοτρύων, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 895, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 496 (Lyric poetry)}}; πόντου πηγαῖς with sea-**water**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “IT” 1039}}; πηγαὶ τροφῆς τῷ γεννωμένῳ, of mother's milk, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 237e; π. μαστῶν “Inscription Cos” 218.8.}}
**II** **fount, source**, τοῦ Νείλου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.28, 4.53 (_plural_), “OGI” 168.9 (Syene, 2nd c.BC), 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 17.1.52 (_plural_)}}; πηγαὶ ἡλίου **the fount** of light, i. e. the South, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 809}}; πηγαὶ νυκτός the North, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 956; παγὰ ἐπέων 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.299}}; πυρὸς παγαί{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1.22, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 110, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 79d}}; πηγὴ ἀργύρου, of the silver-mines at Laureion, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 238}}; τῆς ἀκουούσης π. δι᾽ ὤτων, i.e. the sense of hearing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1387}}; ἀπὸ μιᾶς ἀρχῆς καὶ π. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 668a15, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.856e.}}
**II.2** _metaphorically_, **source, origin**, mostly in _singular_, κακῶν π. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 743}}; αἱ τέχναι, ἃς πηγάς φασι τῶν καλῶν εἶναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.2.13; π. καὶ ἀρχὴ κινήσεως 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 245c}}; π. ἡδονῶν, τοῦ φρονεῖν, νοσημάτων, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Phlb.” 62d, “Lg.” 808d, “Ti.” 85b, etc.; ἀρχαὶ καὶ π. τῶν στάσεων 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1301b5, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 690d; π. τῆς κακοπραγμοσύνης 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 18.40.3}}; βέβηκα π. εἰς ἐμάς I have returned to **the source** of my existence, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|“epigram Gr.” 463 (from Crommyon), compare 5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 95, +others}}
**II.3** **inner canthus** of eye, supposed source of tears, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 2.71, 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus (both _plural_).}}

4078:
** πήγνῡμι**,
_3rd.pers._ _plural_ πηγνύουσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.72 (variant), 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 6.6.9, but πηγνῦσι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus [prev. cited] codices plural, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης” 2.60; _optative_ πήγνυτο 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 118a}} codices; _infinitive_ πηγνύειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 6.7, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.95}}: _imperfect_ πήγνυον {{Refs 4th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Lithica” 567 (περι-), 4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 5.50}}: late form of _present_ **πήσσω** (which see): _future_ πήξω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.283}}; _Doric dialect_ πάξω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.3:}}_aorist_ ἔπηξα, _Epic dialect_ πῆξα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.15, etc.}}; _Aeolic dialect_ _participle_ πάξαις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10 (II). 45:}}_perfect_ πέπηχα, only _pluperfect_ ἐμ-πεπήχεσαν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 40.40}}:—_middle_ in _transitive_ sense, πήγνῠμαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 809}}: _future_ πήξομαι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 10.388}}: _aorist_ ἐπηξάμην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 455, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.12, etc.:}} —_passive_ πήγνῠμαι: _future_ πᾰγήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 437, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.92}}; πήξομαι (as _passive_) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 8:}}_aorist 1_ ἐπήχθην, _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ πῆχθεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.298}}, _Doric dialect_ _subjunctive_ παχθῇ {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 23.31}}, _participle_ πηχθείς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 302: more frequently}}_aorist 2_ ἐπάγην [ᾰ], _Epic dialect_ πάγην, _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ πάγεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.572}}; _participle_ παγείς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 190, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 395:}}_perfect_ πέπηγμαι (κατα-, συμ-) {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 5.46, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 2.21.1}}: _pluperfect_ ἐπέπηκτο {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 3.123b}}; but in the best authors, πέπηγα is used as the _perfect_ _passive_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.135, etc.}}; _Aeolic dialect_ πέπᾱγα {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 34; _optative_ πεπαγοίην 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 435}}: _pluperfect_ ἐπεπήγειν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.442, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.23:}}
**I** **stick** or **fix in**, ἐν δὲ μετώπῳ πῆξε [τὴν αἰχμήν] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.460, etc.; ἔνθα οἱ ἔγχος ἔπηξε 13.570; ἐν γαίῃ π. ἐρετμόν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.276 (or γαίῃ 11.129); π. ἐπὶ τύμβῳ ἐρετμόν 11.77 (or τύμβῳ 12.15)}}; [γύην] ἐν ἐλύματι π. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 430; ἔπαξε διὰ φρενῶν ξίφος 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.26}}; **fix in the earth, plant**, σκῆπτρον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 420, compare “Aj.” 821}}; σκηνήν, σκηνὰς π., **pitch** a tent, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.30, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 817c (in _middle_, σκηνὰς πηξάμενοι}} **pitching themselves** tents, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.12)}}; σταύρωμα π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.66; τὰς σχαλίδας π. ὑπτίας 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 6.7}}; **plant** seeds or cuttings, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 6.6.9, 7.4.10: _intransitive_}}_perfect_ and _passive_, δόρυ δ᾽ ἐν κραδίῃ ἐπεπήγει the spear **stuck fast** in his heart, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.442; [δοῦρα] ἐν χροῒ πήγνυτο 15.315; [ὀϊστοὶ] ἐν χροῒ πῆχθεν 8.298; δοῦρα ἐν σάκεϊ πάγεν 11.572; [ξίφος] πέπηγεν ἐν γῇ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 819; σκηνὴ ἔσκε πεπηγυῖα ἑτοίμη 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.119; κυρβασίας ὀρθὰς πεπηγυίας [prev. work] 64, compare 70:}} —_middle_, ἐν ἀλλήλοις χείλεα πηξάμενοι, of kissing, {{Refs|“Anthologia Graeca” 5.254 (Paul. Sil.).}}
**I.2** **stick** or **fix on**, κεφαλὴν ἀνὰ σκολόπεσσι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.177; σκόλοψι δέμας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1430; κρᾶτα πήξασ᾽ ἐπ᾽ ἄκρον θύρσον [prev. author] “Ba.” 1141:}} —_passive_, ἀμφὶ βουπόροισι πηχθέντας μέλη ὀβελοῖσι **having their** limbs **fixed** on spits, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyc.” 302}}; ὑπὸ ῥάχιν παγέντες **impaled**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 190.}}
**I.3** **fix upon** an object, κατὰ χθονὸς ὄμματα π. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.217}}: _intransitive_ _perfect_, πρὸς ἀστρονομίαν ὄμματα πέπηγεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 53o d, compare 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator [prev. cited] (_passive_)}}; πεπηγυῖα τὰς τῶν ὀμμάτων βολὰς ἐς τὰ τῆς ψυχῆς ἀπόρρητα {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Philostratus Junior Sophista “Imagines” 11: with}}_infinitive_, ἡ σοφία ἀρέσκειν πέπηγε **is bent upon** pleasing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 605a: _absolutely_, τὸ πεπηγὸς ὄμμα}} **immovable** eye, **fixed** gaze, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προρρητικόν” 1.46, compare 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 16.610.}}
**II** **fasten** [different parts] **together, fit together, build**, νῆας πῆξαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.664}}; ἴκρια π. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.163:}} —_middle_, πήξασθαι ἄμαξαν **build oneself** a wagon, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 455; νέας πηξάμενοι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.83}}:—_passive_, **to be joined** or **put together**, ψυχὴ καὶ σῶμα παγέν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 246c.}}
**III** **make solid** or **stiff**, especially of liquids, **freeze**, θεὸς.. πήγνυσι πᾶν ῥέεθρον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 496}}; τοὺς ποταμοὺς ἔπηξε (i.e. ὁ θεός) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 139; βορρᾶς πηγνὺς τοὺς ἀνθρώπους 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.5.3}}; **curdle**, γάλα {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.95:}} —_middle_, τυροὺς πήγνυσθαι **to make oneself** cheese (by curdling the milk), {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “VH” 1.24}}:—_passive_ and _intransitive_ _perfect_, **become solid, stiffen**, γοῦνα πήγνυται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.453; ἄρθρα πέπηγέ μου 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1395}} (but also, **become firm** or **set**, of limbs, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 2.11; πεπηγυῖα ὑγιεινὴ κατάστασις 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “Θρασύβουλος - Scripta Minora” 7)}}; of liquids, **freeze**, ἡ θάλασσα πήγνυται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.28}}; ἅλες πήγνυνται salt **crystallizes**, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|[prev. work] 53, compare 6.119; φόνος πέπηγεν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 67 (Lyric poetry); πεπάγαισιν ὐδάτων ῤόαι 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 34, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.4.3}}; κρύσταλλος ἐπεπήγει οὐ βέβαιος **was** not **frozen** so as to bear, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.23; ἁνίκα [χιὼν] παχθῇ 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 23.31}}; ὄστρακον [ᾠοῦ] π. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 752a35}}; γάλα π. {{Refs|[prev. author] “PA” 676a14}}; ὀφθαλμῶν οἱ μὲν ὑγιεῖς, οἱ δὲ πεπηγότες **blind**, of buds, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 5.12.10}}: _metaphorically_, **to be petrified, struck dumb**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 166.7.}}
**IV** _metaphorically_, **fix**, ὅρους τοῖς βαρβάροις {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 73, compare 4th c.BC: Aristopho Comicus 9.7}}: Astrology texts, **fix, determine** a nativity, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Scholia 2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 103:}} —_middle_, ὄφρα ἐν φρασὶ πάξαιθ᾽, ὅπως.. that **he might keep it fixed in his** heart, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 3.62}}; **establish**, χορούς {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Himerius Sophista “Orationes” 16.6}}:— _passive_ and _intransitive_ _perfect_, **to be irrevocably fixed, established**, εἷς ὅρος ἡμῖν παγήσεται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.92}}; πῆγμα (Aurat. for πῆμα) γενναίως παγέν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1198; κακῶς παγέντας ὅρκους 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 395; ὀρθὰς παγείσας φρένας 5th c.BC: Carcinus Tragicus 6.2; μὴ γὰρ ὡς θεῷ νομίζετ᾽ ἐκείνῳ τὰ παρόντα πεπηγέναι πράγματα ἀθάνατα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.8; τὰ καλῶς πεπηγότα τῇ φύσει [prev. author] 25.90}}. (Cf. Latin _pango._)

4079:
** πηδάλι-ον** [ᾰ], τό,
(πηδόν) **steering-paddle, rudder**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, only in 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey”; π. μετὰ χερσὶ.. νηὸς ἔχοντα 3.281; π. ποιήσατο, ὄφρ᾽ ἰθύνοι 5.255; πηδαλίῳ ἰθύνετο τεχνηέντως ἥμενος [prev. work] 270; π. δὲ ἐκ χειρῶν προέηκε [prev. work] 315}}; π. δὲ ἓν ποιεῦνται (i.e. Αἰγύπτιοι) καὶ τοῦτο διὰ τῆς τρόπιος διαβύνεται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.96}}; Greek ships had a pair, hence in _plural_, of a single ship, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 4.110, 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 139, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 542, 4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 43.11}}; πηδάλια ζεύγλαισι (cross-bars) παρακαθίετο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1536}}; ἀνέντες τὰς ζευκτηρίας τῶν π. {{NT|NT.Act.27.40}}; πηδάλια εἶχε τέτταρα τριακονταπήχη, of the τεσσαρακοντήρης of Ptolemy IV, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC(?): Callixinus Historicus 1: _metaphorically_ in Comedy texts, [γυνὴ].. οὐδὲ μικρὸν πείθεται ἑνὶ πηδαλίῳ Theophilus Comicus 6: proverbial, π. κρεμάσαι}} to retire from a seafaring life, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 711.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, ἱππικὰ π., of reins, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 206 (Lyric poetry); νώμα δικαίῳ π. στρατόν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.86; τὰ π. τῆς διανοίας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Clitopho” 408b.}}
**II** in _plural_, of **the oars** by which the nautilus is said to steer himself, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 622b13}}; of **the long hind legs** of the locust and grasshopper, {{Refs|[prev. work] 532a29, 535b12,compare “IA” 710a3.}}
**III** ={πολύγονον ἄρρεν}, Ps.-{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.4.}}

4080:
** πηλίκ-ος** [ῐ], η, ον,
_interrogative_ _correlative_ to τηλίκος, ἡλίκος, **how great** or **large?** πηλίκη τις ἔσται ἡ γραμμή; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 82d, compare 83e}}; πηλίκον τινὰ οἴεσθε μέγεθο; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 82}}; after τηλικοῦτος, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.284}}; πόσα καὶ πηλίκα **of what** number and **magnitude**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.2.8: with _Article_, ὁ πηλίκος}}; **quantulus?** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 69.4}}; τὸ π. **magnitude**, opposed to τὸ ποσόν (quantity), {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Nicomachus Gerasenus Mathematicus “Arithmetica Introductio” 1.2; ὁ χρόνος.. ἐστὶ μῖγμα πηλίκου καὶ ποσοῦ 5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 371}}. _adverb_ -κως {{Refs|Hdn.Gr. 2.925.}}
**II** **of what age**, π. ἦσθ᾽ ὅθ᾽ ὁ Μῆδος ἀφίκετ; {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 22.5.}}
**II.2** _indefinite_, **of a certain age**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1134b11}}: _comparative_ -ώτερος, _falsa lectio_ for{ἀπηλικέστερος}, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων ὀξέων παθῶν” 2.11.}}

4081:
** πηλός**
(_Doric dialect_ **πᾱλός** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 32, 3rd c.BC: Cercidas Iambographus 3.3, “IG” 5(1).1447.16 (Messene, 3rd-2nd c.BC)), ὁ, Syrac. ἡ 2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 38}}:— **clay, earth**, used by masons and potters, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.36, 136, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1143, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.76, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 147a, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 15.35.2; πηλὸν ὀργάζειν 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 248, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 482, compare 510, 787, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 839}}; π. ἠχυρωμένος **clay** mixed with chaff for use as mortar, {{LXX|“IG” 22.463.42, compare 5(1)[same place], LXX.Gen.11.3}}; εὐώδεϊ πηλῷ, of **earth** on which wine has been poured, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd-4th c.AD: Tryphiodorus Epicus 349}}; Βρομιώδεα π. φύρησαν.. Χάριτες, of a drinkingcup, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 11.27 (1st c.AD(?): Macedonius Epigrammaticus)}}: _metaphorically_, **clay** from which man was made: hence ὁ π. ὁ Προμηθεῖος, of man, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Fragmenta” 87, compare 133, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 686; ἐκ ποίου πηλοῦ πεφύρητ᾽ εἰδότα 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 2.29.}}
**2** **mud, mire**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.5, 4.28, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 248, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.4, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 363d, etc.: proverbial, ἔξω κομίζειν πηλοῦ πόδα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 697}}; κάσις πηλοῦ ξύνουρος, i. e. dust, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ag.” 495: _metaphorically_, ἀνέρες ὧν τὸ κέαρ παλῶ σέσακται 3rd c.BC: Cercidas Iambographus [prev. cited]}}
**II** poetical, **thick** or **muddy wine, lees**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 783}}; of wine spilt on the floor, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.463a, 2nd c.AD(?): Charito Scriptor Eroticus 1.3}}; compare Πηλεύς near the end
**III** _metaphorically_, **dolt, blockhead, Comedy texts Adesp.**{{Refs|890.}}

4083:
** πῆχυς**
(_Aeolic dialect_ πᾶχυς {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 33)}}, ὁ, _genitive_ πήχεος {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 2, +others, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.178, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus1.126d, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Mirabilia” 813a10, LXX.Exo.25.9, +others, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 10.44.2, 3rd-2nd c.BC: Philo Mechanicus “(Belopoeica) Excerpte aus Philons Mechanik - Ath. Berl. Akad.” 73.42, πήχεως 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 606a14 (variant -εος), “PCair.Zen.” 484.10 (3rd c.BC), πήχως (condemned by 2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 222) corrected to πήχεος “PCair.Zen.” 665.1 (3rd c.BC)}}: _genitive_ _plural_ πήχεων {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 12.314.39, 22.1673.15, “PCair.Zen.” 353.10 (3rd c.BC)}}; later _contraction_ πηχῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.7.16 codices, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1302b37, “PCair.Zen.” 54.4 (3rd c.BC), “PStrassb.” 85.20 (2nd c.BC), 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ σημείων καὶ σημειώσεων” 2, 2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 222}}, Moer.p.327 P.:—**forearm**, from wrist to elbow, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 2, 3, +others, 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 2.140}}; opposed to βραχίων, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 75a, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 12.5}}: in Poets, generally, **arm**, ἀμφὶ δ᾽ ἑὸν φίλον υίὸν ἐχεύατο πήχεε λευκώ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.314, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.38, 23.240}}; λευκὸν ἀντείνασα π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus “fragment” 13.4, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1466 (Lyric poetry)}}; λαιὸν ἔπαιρε π. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Heracl.” 728.}}
**2** Anatomy texts, **ulna**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Rufus Medicus “περὶ ὀνομασίας” 80, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “de Usu Partium” 2.2, 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus “de signis fracturarum - Physici et Medici Graeci Minores” 20.}}
**II** **centrepiece, which joined the two horns of the bow**, τόν ῥ᾽ [ὀϊστὸν] ἐπὶ πήχει ἑλὼν ἕλκεν νευρήν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.419; ὁ δὲ τόξου πῆχυν ἄνελκε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.375, 13.583.}}
**III** in _plural_, **horns of the lyre**, opposed to ζυγόν (the bridge), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.192; πήχεις ἐναρμόσας καὶ ζυγώσας 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DDeor.” 7.4.}}
**III.2** also, ={ζυγόν}, **crosspiece** or **bridge** in which the horns were fitted, {{Refs|Artemo Hist. 12.}}
**IV** in the balance, **beam,** {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae 22.1013.32, “Theologumena Arithmeticae” 29.}}
**V** as a measure of length, **distance from the point of the elbow to that of the middle finger**, = 6 παλασταί = 24 δάκτυλοι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 2.158; π. μέτριος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.178}}; π. ἰδιωτικός, κοινός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Cat.” 16; but π. βασιλήϊος, = 27 δάκτυλοι, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.178, 7.117; ὁ Αἰγύπτιος π. τυγχάνει ἴσος ἐὼν τῷ Σαμίῳ [prev. author] 2.168, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista [prev. cited]}}; for later measurements, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “Deff.” 131, “Geometry texts” 4.2, +others}}
**V.2** **cubit-rule**, as we say 'foot-rule', {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 799, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 1.47; π. ἀκαμπής “Anthologia Graeca” 6.204 (1st c.AD: Leonidas Alexandrinus Epigrammaticus)}}; as epithet of Nemesis, {{Refs|“APl.” 4.223, 224.}}
**V.3** _metaphorically_ of any small amount (compare πήχυιος), {{NT|NT.Matt.6.27}}; κατὰ πῆχυν **little** by **little**, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Marinus Biographus “Procl.” 26.}}
**VI** πήχεις, οἱ, the **cubits** (of inundation), represented in pictures as children one cubit high playing round the Nile, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Rh.Pr.” 6, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Philostrati majoris imagines” 1.5}}. (Cf. Sanskrit _bāhú_-, Avest. **bāzu**- (_masculine_) 'arm', ONorse **bógr** 'shoulder'.)

4084:
** πῐέζω**,
_imperfect_ ἐπίεζον, _Epic dialect_ πίεζον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.174, etc.}}: _future_ πιέσω {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 18.3}}; _Epic dialect_ πιέσσω {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 4.146}}: _aorist_ ἐπίεσα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 6, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.63, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.52, etc.}} (but _subjunctive_ πιέξῃς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 5}}, _infinitive_ πιέξαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 42(1).123.116 (Epid., 4th c.BC)}}_participle_ πιέξας (variant{πιάξας}) {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Alexipharmaca” 224)}}: _perfect_ πεπίεκα {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Demetrius Lacon Philosophus “Herc.” 1012.44}}:—_passive_, _future_ πιεσθήσομαι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 11.317}} (δια-), Heliod cited in {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 10.18.15}}: _aorist_ ἐπιέσθην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.336, 6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 13.37, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.11, etc.; ἐπιέχθην 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 5, etc.}}: _perfect_ πεπίεσμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Mundo” 392b33, 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Hypotyposis astronomicarum positionum” 5.49, conjecture in 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 3.55. etc.; πεπίεγμαι 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 5.}}—From **πῐεζέω** we have πιεζέουσι variant in {{Refs|[prev. author] “Fract.” 31:}}_imperfect_ πιέζευν variant in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.174, 196}}; _participle_ πιεζεῦντα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “κατ᾽ ἰητρεῖον” 25, “Fract.” 9, πιεζεῦσαν 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 8.47}}:—_passive_, _participle_ πιεζεύμενος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.146, 6.108, 8.142}} (always with variant -όμενος), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ φύσιος παιδίου” 21, πιεζούμενος 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.74.2; _imperative_ πιεζείσθω “IG” 4.364.7 (Corinth, 4th c.AD)}}: _imperfect_ ἐπιεζοῦντο {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 11.33.3}}; so in later Gr., as {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Theseus” 6, Alcaeus2, etc.}}; _Doric dialect_, _Aeolic dialect_, and later Gr. **πῐάζω** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 44, 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 148}}: _aorist 1_ ἐπίᾰσα {{LXX+NT+3rd c.BC+|LXX.Cant.2.15, NT.John.8.20; ἐπίαξα 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 4.35, (ἀμφ-) “Ep..” 6:}}_passive_, _future_ πιασθήσομαι {{LXX|LXX.Sir.23.21}}: _aorist_ ἐπιάσθην {{NT|NT.Rev.19.20}}: _perfect_ πεπίασμαι {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“POxy.” 812 (1st c.BC), 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.15, “Hippiatrica” 34}}:—**press tight, squeeze**, χειρὶ ἑλὼν ἐπίεζε βραχίονα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.510, compare 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 497}}; ἀστεμφέως ἐχέμεν μᾶλλόν τε π. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.419; μ᾽ ἐν δεσμοῖσι δέον μᾶλλόν τε πίεζον 12.196, compare 164}}; π. τὰ χείλεα **compress** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 22}}; ῥύγχος εἰς ὄξος π. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Axionicus Comicus 8.5}}; π. τοὺς ὑπευθύνους **squeezing** them (like figs), to try if they are ripe, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 259; σφόδρα π. αὐτοῦ τὸν πόδα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 117e; π. [τὴν δεξιὰν] ἐμπαθῶς 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 31.24.9: _absolutely_, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.10.13, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1361b17}}:—_passive_, **to be pressed tight**, ἐν δεσμοῖς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.336, compare 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 25, +others}}; of wrestlers, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alcibiades” 2}}; πιέζεται ὅσα πόρους ἔχει κενούς **are compressible**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 386b1.}}
**II** **press** or **weigh down**, of a heavy weight, Σικελία αὐτοῦ π. στέρνα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.19, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1032}}:—and in _passive_, ὁ δ᾽ ὦμος.. πιέζεται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ra.” 30, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.11}}: _metaphorically_, **oppress, distress**, π. τινὰ ἡ δαπάνη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.35; λιμός 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 250; καὶ πρὸς π. χρημάτων ἀχηνία (Abresch for προσπιέζει) [prev. work] 301}}; συμφορὰ δ᾽ἑτέρους ἑτέρα π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 894 (Lyric poetry); αὐχμὸς π. τὰς ἀμπέλους 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1120}}; π. ἡ ἀνάγκη{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 437, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.52}}:—frequently in _passive_, ὑπὸ νούσοισι {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 13.37; ὑπὸ λιμοῦ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.126; πολέμῳ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.11, 6.34; τῇ νούσῳ 6th c.BC: Pherecydes Lerius Historicus cited in 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 1.122, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.47; ταῖς εἰσφοραῖς 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 28.3; ταῖς συμφοραῖς 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.2.20; σπάνει σίτου [prev. author] “HG” 5.4.56, etc.: _absolutely_, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.121, etc.}}; of a river, **to be exhausted** from the heat of the sun, {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.25.}}
**II.2** **press hard**, of a victorious army, τοὺς ἐναντίους {{Refs|[prev. author] 9.63:}} —_passive_, τὴν πιεζομένην μάλιστα τῶν μοιρέων{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 60; εἴ πῃ πιέζοιντο 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.49, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.4.34; ὑπό τινων [prev. work] 7.1.43.}}
**II.3** **bear hardly upon**, τινα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 409a; τῷ λόγῳ 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alcibiades” 6; ὑπὸ τῶν ἐλέγχων πιέζεσθαι 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “D.” 3.8}}; of a point in the argument, **hold fast to**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 965d}}; **press** it, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.21.3, 2nd c.BC: Demetrius Lacon Philosophus}} [same place], etc.; **lay stress on**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.31e}}: with _dative_, **insist upon**, τοῖσι περιπάτοισι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Insomn.” 88.}}
**II.3.b** **determine precisely**, ἀποστήματα {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Hypotyposis astronomicarum positionum” 5.19, compare 49 (_passive_)}}; π. δεῖ πῶς ἓν ἐκεῖνο καὶ ἕτερον {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “Sententiae ad intelligibilia ducentes” 36.}}
**II.4** **repress, stifle**, ἐν θυμῷ χόλον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.37; τὸν τῦφον 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alcibiades” 4.}}
**II.5** **outweigh**, τἀγαθῷ τὸ δυστυχές {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 637, compare “Supp.” 249 (assuming variant).}}
**III** later, **lay hold of**, ταῦρον.. πιάξας τᾶς ὁπλᾶς **by** the hoof, {{NT+3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 4.35; αὐτὸν τῆς χειρός NT.Act.3.7, cf. NT.John.7.30, etc.}}

