**III** later, **lay hold of**, ταῦρον.. πιάξας τᾶς ὁπλᾶς **by** the hoof, {{NT+3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 4.35; αὐτὸν τῆς χειρός NT.Act.3.7, cf. NT.John.7.30, etc.}}

4087:
** πικρ-αίνω**,
**make sharp** or **keen**, especially to the taste, π. τὴν κοιλίαν **make** it **bitter,** {{NT|NT.Rev. 10.9}}:— _passive_, τὸ στόμα πικραίνεται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης ὀξέων” 30: opposed to γλυκαίνεσθαι, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 244b20.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, **embitter, irritate**, τὴν ψυχήν {{LXX|LXX.Job.27.2}}; τὴν ἀκοήν **affect** it **harshly**, opposed to γλυκαίνω, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 12, 15}}; **make harsh**, νόμους conjecture in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 693}}:—_passive_, **to be exasperated, embittered**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 731d, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.120}}; ὁ ζωγράφος πονεῖ τι καὶ π. **vexes himself**, {{LXX+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 144.3; π. ἐπί τισι LXX.Exo.16.20}}; ἔν τισι{{LXX|LXX.Ruth.1.20}}; also ἐπικράνθη μοι it **grieved** me, {{Refs|[prev. work] 13.}}
**3** of style, **make harsh** or **rugged**, διάλεκτον {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Demosthene” 55, compare 34.}}

4088:
** πικρ-ία**, ἡ,
**bitterness**:
**1** of taste, {{LXX+4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 6.10.7, “Od.” 32, LXX.Jer.15.17, “Placita Philosophorum” 3.16.2, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.61, etc.}}
**2** of temper, τὴν ἀπὸ τῆς ψυχῆς π. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.204, compare 25.84, “Ep.” 3.33, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Virtutibus et Vitiis” 1251a4, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.56W.}}; ἡ ἐπὶ τοῖς γεγονόσι π. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 15.4.11; πρὸς τὸν δῆμον 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Ccr.” 15}}; ἡ ἐν τοῖς λόγοις π. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 16.88; λόγος π. ἔχων μεμιγμένην χάριτι 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lycurgus” 19.}}
**3** of circumstances, ἡ τοῦ καιροῦ π. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 417.5 (2nd-3rd c.AD).}}

4089:
** πικρός**, ά, όν,
_poetry_ also ός, όν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.406}}:—properly **pointed, sharp, keen**, ὀϊστός {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.118, +others; βέλεμνα 22.206; γλωχίς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 681: _metaphorically_, γλώσσης πικροῖς κέντροισι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1288.}}
**II** generally, **sharp** to the sense:
**II.1** of taste, **pungent**, ῥίζα [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.846; ἅλμη 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.323; δάκρυον (variant for{πυκνόν}) 4.153}}]; of salt water, opposed to γλυκύς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.52, compare 7.35}}; ἁλμυρὸς καὶ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 705a}}; πριγλία π. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 82.8 (3rd c.BC)}}; ἀπ᾽ ὄμφακος πικρᾶς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 970}}; ὑγρότης π., opposed to ὀξεῖα, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Meno Medicus “Iatrika” 5.13}}; also of smell, **pungent**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.406; πικρὸν ὀδωδώς 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 3.59. (This sense prevails in the derived and _compound_ words.)}}
**II.2** of feeling, **sharp, keen**, ὠδῖνες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.271, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 41.}}
**II.3** of sound, **piercing, shrill**, οἰμωγά {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ph.” 189 (Lyric poetry); φθόγγος [prev. author] “OC” 1610}}; γόοι, ὄδυρμα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 883, “Tr.” 1227 (Lyric poetry); πικροτάτη ὄψ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 805 (Lyric poetry).}}
**III** _metaphorically_,
**III.1** of things, **bitter**, especially of what yields pain instead of expected pleasure, frequently in threats, μὴ τάχα πικρὴν Αἴγυπτον καὶ Κύπρον ἵκηαι (variant{ἴδηαι}) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.448, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1045, “Th.” 883 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 399, “IA” 955, “Ba.” 357, “Cyc.” 589; π. Σίγειον κατηγόμην 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 355; τὸ πὰρ δίκαν γλυκὺ πικροτάτα μένει τελευτά 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 7(6).48, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 745 (Lyric poetry)}}; τιμωρία, ἀγῶνες, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pers.” 473, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1239}}; δύαι, χεῖμα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 180 (Lyric poetry), “Ag.” 198 (Lyric poetry); πικρότερ᾽ ἀχέων [prev. author] “Supp.” 875 (Lyric poetry); λόγοι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 481; πικροτάτου χρυσοῦ φύλαξ [prev. author] “Hec.” 772; τὸ δὴ λεγόμενον γλυκὺ πικρῷ μεμειγμένον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 46c; ἔχει τι τὸ π. τῆς γεωργίας γλυκύ 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 795}}: with _infinitive_, μὴ λίαν πικρὸν εἰπεῖν ᾖ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.26.}}
**III.2** so of persons, probably in {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 4.1 (_comparative_)}}; γλυκὺν ὧδε φίλοις ἐχθροῖσι δὲ π. {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 13.5, compare 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 301, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 234, “Eu.” 152 (Lyric poetry), etc.; ἔς τινας 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.123: _absolutely_, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 739, “Th” 940(Lyric poetry)}}; π. θεοῖς **hateful** to them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 254; π. πολίταις 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 224, compare “Supp.” 1222}}; ἐμοὶ π. τέθνηκεν ἢ κείνοις γλυκύς his death is **matter of sorrow** to me, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 966}}; δαίμων π., of **untimely** death (Latin _acerbus),_ {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae 3.1338.}}
**III.3** **embittered, angry**, πικρὰ ὄρνις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 423.}}
**III.4** **relentless**, οὐδὲν πικρότερον τῆς ἀνάγκης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.2.4}}; **spiteful, mean, vindictive**, βάσκανον καὶ πικρὸν καὶ κακόηθες οὐδέν ἐστι πολίτευμα ἐμόν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.108; π. καὶ συκοφάντης [prev. author] 25.45, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1368b21, “EN” 1126a19}}: in Comedy texts of old men, σκυθρός, π., φειδωλός {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 10, compare 825, 843, “Georg.fragment” 3.}} _adverb_ -ρῶς **pedantically**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Lysia” 6}}; **with rigid accuracy**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Apollonius Citiensis Medicus 3, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.659f.}}
**IV** _adverb_ -ρῶς **harshly, bitterly, vindictively**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 197, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 990; π. ἐξετάσαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.27, 18.265}}; π. ἔχειν τισί, πρός τινας, {{NT+5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 10.54, “Ep.” 3.10; π. φέρειν τι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 610, compare “Andr.” 190; ἔκλαυσε π. NT.Matt.26.75}}: _comparative_ **-ότερον**{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Μονόστιχοι - Fragmenta Comicorum Graecorum” 659, etc.}}: _superlative_ **-ότατα**{{Refs 8th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.72.3. [ῑ in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} and _Epic dialect_; ῐ frequently in Trag., as {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 473, “Ag.” 970, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 500, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 772, and in 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 8.74}}: ι therefore is not long by nature as in μικρός.]

