4092:
** πίμπρημι**,
_present_ and _imperfect_ non-thematic; _imperative_ πίμπρη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 527, 974}}; _participle_ _nominative_ _plural_ πιμπράντες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.94}}; _infinitive_ πιμπράναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 810, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 81, (ἐμ-) 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 11.5.6, etc.}}: _imperfect_ ἐν-επίμπρην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.94}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἐνεπίμπρασαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.5.32:}} —other tenses formed from πρήθω (which see): _future_ πρήσω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 434, (ἐμ-) 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.242, etc.}}: _aorist_ ἔπρησα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2.415, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 390, etc.}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ shortened ἔπρεσε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 856:}}_perfect_ πέπρηκα (ὑπο-, ἐμ-, κατα-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιστολαί” 17, 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 1.32, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 59.16}}:—_middle_, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Alexipharmaca” 345:}}_aorist_ ἐπρησάμην (ἐν-) {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 5.485}}:—_passive_, _future_ πρησθήσομαι {{LXX|LXX.Num.5.27}}; πεπρήσομαι (variant{πρήσομαι} (ἐμ-)) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.9}}: _aorist_ ἐπρήσθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ γυναικείης φύσιος” 10, 4th c.BC: Amphis Comicus 30.10 (uncertain), (ἐν-) 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.55, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 469e}}: _perfect_ (ἐμ-) πέπρησμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.144, 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 2.5.5}}; but πέπρημαι is the _Attic dialect_ form _accusative_ to [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc. see at {σέσωται}, and ἐμ-πέπρημαι is found in 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 36 codex Rav.; _imperative_ πέπρησο 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 80.}}]—Collat. _present_ **ἐμ-πιπράω** (see. ἐμπίμπρημι).—In the _compound_ ἐμπίμπρημι (which see; more frequently in Prose) the second μ is sometimes dropped, as ἐμπίπρημ; but returns with the augment, as ἐνεπίμπρασα; compare πίμπλημι:—**burn, burn up**, γῆν.. πυρὶ πρῆσαι κατάκρας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 201, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 81; πρῆσαι δὲ πυρὸς.. θύρετρα 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.415, compare 9.242 (variant)}}; without πυρί or πυρός, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 856; πρήσω πόλιν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 434, compare “Pers.” 810; δῶμα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 390, etc.:}} —_passive_, πίμπραμαι **to be burnt**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 341}}; πέπρησο **burn with fever**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 80, compare “SIG” 1180.10 (from Cnidus)}}; of wounds, **to be inflamed**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Theriaca” 306}} (but _intransitive_ in _active_ πίμπρησι δὲ χείλη {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] “Al.” 438): _metaphorically_, ἐπί τινι πίμπρασθαι 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Jud.Voc.” 8; ἐπὶ Ῥωμαίοις 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Ἰταλική” 3.}}
**II**= πρήθω {{Refs|1.1}}, **blow up, distend**, in _passive_, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ γυναικείης φύσιος” 10, “Flat.” 8, 2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Alexipharmaca” 477, NT.Act.28.6 (variant); ἐπέπρητο ὅλα “IG” 42(1).122.123 (Epid.)}}:—_active_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 522b28, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.32}}. (Cf. Russ. **prèt**' 'sweat', 'stew'.)

4094:
** πίναξ** [ῐ], ᾰκος, ὁ,
**board, plank**, πίνακάς τε νεῶν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.67; εὐγόμφοισιν.. πινάκεσσιν 2nd-3rd c.AD: Oppianus Anazarbensis Epicus “Halieutica - Poetae Bucolici et Didactici” 1.194}}, compare πινακηδό; πίνακος κουρά sawdust, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus: hence of things made of flat wood, metal, etc.}}
**1** **drawing**- or **writing-tablet**, ={δέλτος}, γράψας ἐν π. πτυκτῷ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.169; πίναξιν.. ἐγγεγραμμένα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 946; πινάκων ξεστῶν δέλτοι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 778; ἐν χρυσῷ π. γράψαντες 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 120c, compare “R.” 501a}}; of a **votive tablet** hung on the image of a god, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 463, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1341a36, “IG” 42(1).121.24(Epid., 4th c.BC, _plural_), 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 4.19, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 8.6.15 (_plural_), etc.}}; Πίνακες **tables** or **catalogues** of authors, name of a work by Callimachus, [{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 8.86, compare 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 6.244a, 13.585b, 10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus see {Καλλίμαχος}}}]; **lists** of philosophers, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Sulla” 26; αἵ τ᾽ ἀναγραφαὶ τῶν π. αἵ τε βυβλιοθῆκαι 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ τῶν Στωικῶν” 339.13.}}
**2** **trencher, platter**, κρειῶν πίνακας παρέθηκεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.141, compare 16.49}}; ἐπ᾽ ἀργυροῦ π. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Philippides Comicus 9.4; π. χαλκοῦς 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 4.128d}}; **salver**, πίνακα.. μέγαν, ἔχοντα μικροὺς πέντε πινακίσκους {{Refs|Lynceus Comicus 1.5, compare 17, 19}}; πίνακες ὑέλινοι {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 7.106.}}
**3** **board** for painting on, **picture**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 178, 4th c.BC: Anaxandrides Comicus 33.2; π. οἱ γραφόμενοι 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 5.7.4, compare “IG” 11(2).161 A 75 (Delos, 3rd c.BC).}}
**4** generally, **plate with anything drawn** or **engraved on it**, χάλκεος π., of a map, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.49, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Theseus” 1}}; π. γεωγραφικός, first made by Anaximander, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 1.1.11.}}
**5** **board** or **tablet** on which astronomical tables were drawn, ἡ περὶ πίνακα μέθοδος the art of casting nativities, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Romulus” 12}}; ἀγυρτικοὶ π. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Comp. Arist.Cat.” 3, compare πινάκιον 11.3.}}
**5.b** proverbial, ἐκ πίνακος καὶ πυλαίας, of a trivial fiction, {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.386b.}}
**6** **public notice-board** or **register**, π. ἐκκλησιαστικός {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 44.35, etc.}}; but δαμόσιος π. public **archive**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“SIG” 671 A 15 (Delph., 2nd c.BC).}}
**7** **strop**, to sharpen knives on, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 5.5.1.}}
**8** **toy-theatre** for marionettes, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “Automatopoetica” 23.1, +others}} (Cf. OSlavonic _p[icaron]n[icaron]_ 'tree-stump'.)

4095:
** πίνω** [ῑ],
_Epic dialect_ _infinitive_ πινέμεναι and -έμεν, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.346, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.220}}: _Ionic dialect_ _imperfect_ πίνεσκον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.226}}: _future_ πίομαι {{Refs 6th c.BC+|13.493, 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 962, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 578, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 622, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1289, 1401, “fragment” 311; later πῐοῦμαι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1370b18, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 12.49, etc.}}; also as _falsa lectio_ in earlier authors, πιεῖσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τῶν ἐντὸς παθῶν” 12, πιεῖσθε 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 4.7, but rejected by 2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 23, 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 10.446d}}; _2nd pers._ _singular_ πίεσαι {{LXX+NT|LXX.Deu.28.39, NT.Luke.17.8:}}_aorist_ ἔπῐον, _Epic dialect_ πίον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.2, etc.}}; _2nd pers._ _singular_ _subjunctive_ πίῃσθα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|6.260; _imperative_ πίε 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.347, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 151, “Carmina Popularia” 33, (ἐκ-) 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 563, 5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Fragmenta” 32 b iii; also πῖθι 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 141, Ion Trag. 27, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1489, 5th-6th c.BC: Amipsias Comicus 18, 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 163.1, etc., (ἔκ-) 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 570}}; πίει, πίεις, Kretschmer {{Refs|“Griech. Vaseninschr.” p.195}}; _infinitive_ πιεῖν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.70, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.172, etc.}}; later _contraction_ πεῖν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 11.140 (1st c.AD: Lucillius Epigrammaticus), “Mim.Oxy.” 413.66, “PMag.Lond.” 121.738, “PFlor.” 101.8 (1st c.AD), etc.}}; _Epic dialect_ πῐέμεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.378, πιέειν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.263, πιέναι}} _falsa lectio_ for{ὑπιέναι} in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 5.18}}; _participle_ πῐών, πῐοῦσα, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.102, etc., πῐέουσα 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 7.11}}:—_middle_, _subjunctive_ πινώμεθα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hermippus Comicus 25; _imperative_ πίνεο 2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Theriaca” 912}}: **πίομαι** [ῑ] as _present_ _middle_, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Ibycus Lyricus 17 (assuming variant), 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.86, and so ἐκπίομαι [ῑ] 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 199, ἐμπίομαι [ῐ] 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1129}} (_passive_ in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 5.43 (2nd-3rd c.AD: Rufinus Epigrammaticus))}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.312, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 9, etc.: _Epic dialect_}}_imperfect_ πίνετο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.45.}} —Other tenses are from πω- or πο-, _perfect_ πέπωκα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 821, etc.:}} —_passive_, _future_ ποθήσομαι (κατα-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1502, (ἐκ-) 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.240e:}}_aorist_ ἐπόθην (ἐξ-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 66, (κατ-) 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 111d}}: _perfect_ _infinitive_ πεπόσθαι {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 477}}: _Aeolic dialect_ _present_ **πώνω** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 20, 52, “Supp.” 20.3:}}_aorist_ _imperative_ πῶθι, τῶ, {{Refs|“Etymologicum Magnum” 698.52}}. [ῑ always in πίνω. πίνομα; ῐ always in _aorist_ ἔπιον, hence πίε must be read for πῖνε in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 11.19 (2nd c.AD: Strato Epigrammaticus), and ἔπῑνον for ἔπιον in “Anacreontea” 5.5: 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus has ἐθέλουσι δὲ πῑέμεν ἄμφω 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.825, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.3; but καὶ φαγέμεν πῐέμεν τε 15.378}}; in _imperative_ πῖθι, ῑ always.—In _future_ πίομαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus and Trag. use ῑ, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.493, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 578, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 622, compare 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 962, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1289, 1401, “fragment” 311; but ῐ in lon Lyric poetry 2.10 (nisi to be read πιέτω), (ἐκ-) 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 9, 5th-6th c.BC: Amipsias Comicus 22}}; also in later Poetry, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 11.8,25.5 (1st c.AD: Apollonides Epigrammaticus)}}; for _present_ _middle_ πίομαι, see above]: —**drink**, frequently from {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus downwards, with _accusative_, π. οἶνον, ὐρόν, αἷμα, etc., 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.391, 17.225, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 622, etc.}}; π. ὕδωρ Αἰσήποιο **drink** its water, i.e. live on its banks, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.825, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.86 (_middle_)}}: with _genitive_ _partitive_, **drink of** a thing, π. οἴνοιο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.11}}; εἰς οἶνον.., ἔνθεν ἔπινον **whereof**..,{{Refs|4.220; αἵματος ὄφρα πίω 11.96, compare 15.373}}; also πίνειν κρητῆρας οῐνοιο **to drink** bowls of wine, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.232; κύπελλα ὄνου 4.346}}; π. ἀπὸ κρήνης **drink** of a spring, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 959 (but κρήνης 962)}}; π. ἀπ᾽ αὐτοῦ (i.e. δέπαος) αἴθοπα οἶνον **from** it, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.226; δέπα ἔνθεν ἔπινον 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.62; ἐκ κεράμων μέθυ πίνετο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.469; ἐκ τῆς χειρὸς διδοῖ πιεῖν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.172; ἐκ ταὐτοῦ.. ποτηρίου 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1289; ἐξ ἀργύρου ἢ χρυσοῦ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 417a; ἀπὸ τοῦ ποταμοῦ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.5.4}}; σκύφος ᾧ περ ἔπινεν **with** which.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.112}}; π. κερατίνοις ποτηρίοις variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.1.4}}; τὰ φάρμακα π. παρὰ τοῦ ἰατροῦ draughts sent by him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 467c.}}
**2** _absolutely_, **drink**, ἐσθιέμεν καὶ πινέμεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.305; ὁ πῖνε καὶ ἦσθε 5.94, 6.249, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.476, etc.}}; μῆλα πιόμεν᾽ ἐκ βοτάνης **going to drink** after pasture,{{Refs 7th c.BC+|13.493; πρὸς βίαν πώνην 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 20; πῖνε, πῖν᾽ ἐπὶ συμφοραῖς 4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 14; π. πρὸς ἡδονήν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 176e; εἰς μέθην [prev. author] “Lg.” 775b; διδόναι πιεῖν 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 124; πιεῖν αἰτεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.3.41}}; τινὶ πιεῖν ἐγχέας{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1.3.9; πιεῖν τις ἡμῖν ἐγχεάτω 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 9: in}}_perfect_ πέπωκα, **to be drunk**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 536}}; πίνοντά τε καὶ πεπωκότα **drinking** and **having finished drinking**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 117c.}}
**II** **celebrate by a carouse**, νίκην {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “de gymnastica” 54.}}
**III** _metaphorically_, **drink up**, as the earth does rain, τὸ ὕδωρ, ὄμβρον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.117, 4.198; πιοῦσα κόνις μέλαν αἷμα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 979 (Lyric poetry), compare “Th.” 736 (Lyric poetry), 821, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1401}}; of plants, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 2.25}}; of a lamp, π. τοὔλαιον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Cat.” 27; λύχνος.. πολλὰ πιὼν μέλη “Anthologia Graeca” 5.196 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus, uncertain reading). (I.- 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}} **pōy**- and **pī**-, cf. Sanskrit _pāy-áyati_ 'cause to drink', **pīti**- 'a drink', Latin _pōtus_, etc.)

4096:
** πῑότης**, ητος, ἡ,
**fattiness**, τῆς σαρκός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 21,compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 520a23, “PA” 651b9, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 9.1.3.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **wealth, prosperity**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.299, 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 1146.10.}}

4097:
Included with: ** πέρνημι**,
_3rd.pers._ _plural_ περνᾶσι {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1215, 6th c.BC: Hipponax Iambographus 46 (_2nd pers._ _sing._ περνᾷς uncertain in 52)}}; _participle_ περνάς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.45, 6th c.BC: Hipponax Iambographus 52 (variant), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 271}}: _Epic dialect_ Iterat. _3rd.pers._ _singular_ πέρνασκε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.752}}: _Epic dialect_ _future_ _infinitive_ περάαν [{{Refs|21.454 (but _present_ περάω is wrongly inferred from forms like{ἐπέρασσα}):}}]_aorist_ ἐπέρασσα{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 40, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.387}}; _participle_ περάσαντες {{Refs|“hymnus ad Cererem” 132}}; _aorist_ _optative_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ περάσειε [ᾰ] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.297}}; _aorist_ _subjunctive_ _2nd pers._ _plural_ περάσητε {{Refs|15.453, compare ἀποπέρνημι}}; also _aorist_ ἔπρησα {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“Schwyzer” 714 (Samos, 6th c.BC)}}: _perfect_ πέπρᾱκα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 146.1, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 7.31, etc. (see. below):}}_pluperfect_ ἐπεπράκειν [ᾱ] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.23}}:—_passive_, _present_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ πέρνᾰται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 176, 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}: _imperfect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἐπέρναντο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 2.7}}; _participle_ περνάμενος [ᾰ] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.292}}: _perfect_ _participle_ once πεπερημένος {{Refs|21.58}}; elsewhere the _perfect_ is _Attic dialect_ πέπρᾱμαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 132, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 978, etc.}}, _infinitive_ πεπρᾶσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 734, “Pax” 1011, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.73, etc.}}; _Ionic dialect_ πέπρημαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.56, _imperative_ πεπρήσθω “SIG” 45.35 (Halic., 5th c.BC)}}_infinitive_ πεπρῆσθαι{{Refs|[prev. work] 38}}: _pluperfect_ ἐπέπρᾱτο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 522:}}_aorist_ Trag. and _Attic dialect_ ἐπράθην [ᾱ] {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 36.7, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 915, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.133, etc.}}; _Ionic dialect_ ἐπρήθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.156, “SIG” 229.5 (Erythrae, 4th c.BC), etc.: _Attic dialect_}}_future_ πεπράσομαι [ᾱ] {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 179, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.1.36, later πρᾱθήσομαι 4th c.BC: Sopater Comicus 6, LXX.Lev.25.23, etc., cf. Moer. p.294}} P.: _Aeolic dialect_ _present_ _infinitive_ **πόρναμεν** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}} (perhaps πορνάμεναι): _present_ _participle_ _passive_ πορνάμεναι{{Refs|[prev. author]}} —In _Attic dialect_ the usual _present_ in _active_ sense is πωλέω, _future_ ἀποδώσομαι, _aorist_ ἀπεδόμην: from πέπρᾱμαι, ἐπράθην, etc. is formed the later _present_ _passive_ **πιπράσκομαι**, first found in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 18.20, interpolated in 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 69b, “Sph.” 224a}}, and from this the _present_ _active_ **πιπράσκω** first found in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Asin.” 32}}: _imperfect_ ἐπίπρασκον {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.178c, “Per.” 16}}; _Ionic dialect_ **πιπρήσκω** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Iambi” 1.93, variant in 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιστολαί” 17}}:—**export for sale**, in _Epic dialect_ usually of **exporting** captives to foreign parts **for sale** as slaves, πολλοὺς ζωοὺς ἑλον ἠδὲ πέρασσα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.102, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.297}}; π. τινὰ Αῆμνον **sell** one **to** Lemnos, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.40; ἐς Αῆμνον [prev. work] 58 (_passive_), 78; σέ γε.. νηυσὶν λάβον ἠδ᾽ ἐπέρασσαν τοῦδ᾽ ἀνδρὸς πρὸς δώμαθ᾽ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.387}}; κατ᾽ ἀλλοθρόους ἀνθρώπους{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 453; δήσειν καὶ περάαν νήσων ἔπι τηλεδαπάων 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.454; κτείνων καὶ περνὰς ν. ἔ. τ. 22.45; πέρνασχ᾽ ὅν τιν᾽ ἕλεσκε πέρην ἁλὸς.. ἐς Σάμον 24.752; οὐδ᾽ ἡμᾶς περνᾶσι 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus [same place], compare 6th c.BC: Hipponax Iambographus 46}}; later also of other merchandise, ὡς χαραδριὸν περνάς [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 52 (variant{μῶν.. περνᾷ;; τοῖς ξένοις τὰ χρήματα περνάντα σ᾽ εἶδον} 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyc.”}}][same place]:—_passive_, κτήματα περνάμεν᾽ ἵκει {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.292, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 2.7; πάντα.. πέρναται 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 176.}}
**2** simply, **sell** (as always in the _Attic dialect_ forms), τὰ κτήματα πέντε ταλάντων πεπρακότας {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator}} [same place]:—_passive_, **to be sold**, especially for exportation, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus [same place], 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.156, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 915, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 310}}; ἐς Αιβύην, ἐς Θεσπρωτούς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.54, 56; ἐπ᾽ ἐξαγωγῇ “SIG” 45.38 (Halic., 5th c.BC)}}; ὠνούμενά τε καὶ πιπρασκόμενα interpolated in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 69b; τὸ ὠνηθὲν ἢ πραθέν [prev. author] “Lg.” 850a; πραθείσης ὀλίγου [τῆς πεντηκοστῆς] the tax of 2}} per century. **having been sold** or **let** for a small sum, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.133.}}
**II** **sell** for a bribe, of political leaders, τοὺς πεπρακότας αὑτοὺς ἐκείνῳ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 10.63, compare 17.13; τὰ ὅλα πεπρακέναι [prev. author] 18.28; τἆλλα πλὴν ἑαυτοὺς οἰομένοις πωλεῖν πρώτους ἑαυτοὺς πεπρακόσιν αἰσθέσθαι [prev. work]46; τὴν πατρῴαν γῆν πεπρακέναι 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.71; πεπρακότες τὴν τοῦ βίου παρρησίαν 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 146.1: _metaphorically_ in}}_passive_, πέπραμαι **I am bought and sold !**, i. e. **betrayed, ruined**, πέπραμαι κἀπόλωλα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 978; εὐμορφίᾳ πραθεῖσα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 936. (Cogn. with περάω (A), πέραν}}, OIr. **renaid** 'he sells'.)

4098:
** πίπτω**,
_Aeolic dialect_ **πίσσω**, _accusative_ to Grammars in Hilgard **Exc. ex libris Herodiani** p.28 (cf. Hdn.Gr.2.377 note); _poetry_ _subjunctive_ πίπτῃσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 153.5}}: _Epic dialect_ _imperfect_ πῖπτον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.67, etc.}} (for the quantity of ι{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|compare 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus Gr. 2.10)}}; _Ionic dialect_ πίπτεσκον (συμ-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 59.2}}: _future_ πεσοῦμαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 971 (Lyric poetry), etc.}}; _Ionic dialect3rd.pers._ _plural_ πεσέονται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.824}}, _3rd.pers._ _singular_ πεσέεται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.163, 168:}}_aorist_ ἔπεσον, _infinitive_ πεσεῖν, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.178, etc.}}; _2nd pers._ _singular_ _optative_ πεσοίης {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Polemo Sophista “Callimachus” 10.14}}; _Aeolic dialect_ and _Doric dialect_ ἔπετον {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 60, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.69, “P.” 5.50, (κάπετον) “O.” 8.38, (ἐμ-) “P.” 8.81, compare 3rd c.BC: Isyllus Lyricus 8, “IG” 14.642 (from Thurii)}}; in later writers ἔπεσα, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Argonautica” 521, LXX.Lev.9.24, +others, _falsa lectio_ in 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 291}} (προς-): _perfect_ πέπτωκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 147, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 970, etc.}}; _Epic dialect_ _participle_ πεπτεώς, εῶτος (the εω forming one syllable by synizesis), {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.503, etc.}}; also πεπτηώς, ηυῖα, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.354, 4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 183.7, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 1.69, 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.1298, “Anthologia Graeca” 7.427 (Antip. Sid.), compare πτήσσω}}; Trag. _participle_ πεπτώς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 828, “Ant.” 697}}. (Redupl. from πετ-, which appears in _Aeolic dialect_ and _Doric dialect_ _aorist_ ἔ-πετ-ον (see. above), and the _poetry_ form πίτ-ν; _cognate_ with πέτομαι, which see)
**A** Radical sense, **fall down**, and (when intentional) **cast oneself down**, πρηνέα πεσεῖν, ὕπτιος πέσεν, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.307, 15.435, etc.; νιφάδες.. π. θαμειαί 12.278; ὀπίσω πέσεν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.410; etc.:—Constr., with Preps., in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus almost always ἐν.., ἐν κονίῃσι π}}. **fall** in the dust, i.e. to rise no more, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.425, compare 13.205; ἐν αἵματι καὶ κονίῃσι πεπτεῶτας 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.384}}; π. ἐν ἀγκοίνῃσί τινος **fall into** his arms, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Fragmenta” 142.5}}; ἐν χθονὶ πεπτηώς Simon. [prev. cited] (compare πτήσσω) π. ἐν δεμνίοις {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 35, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 125 (Lyric poetry) (see. below 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 1): rare in Prose, π. ἐν ποταμῷ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 1.32: with _dative_ only, πεδίῳ πέσε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.82}}; δεμνίοις π. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 88 (assuming variant); π. ἐπὶ χθονί 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.535; οὐδέ οἱ ὕπνος πῖπτεν ἐπὶ βλεφάροις 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Fragmenta” 188.4; ἐπὶ γᾷ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 134 (Lyric poetry); πρὸς πέδῳ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 605; πρὸς ἀγκάλαις [prev. author] “Ion” 962; ἀμφὶ σώμασίν τινων 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 326}}: with a _preposition_ of motion first in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus, Πληϊάδες π. ἐς πόντον “Op.” 620; [ποταμὸς] εἰς ἅλα “Th.” 791; εἰς ἄντλον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1025 (Lyric poetry); ἐπὶ γᾶν π. αἷμα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1019 (Lyric poetry); ἐπὶ στόμα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 10.13; πρὸς οὖδας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 405.}}
**2** in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus with Advs. of motion as well as of rest, χαμάδις π. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.16, 15.714, etc.; χαμαὶ π. 4.482, compare 14.418, etc.}}; π. ἔραζε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|12.156, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.280.}}
**3** with Preps. denoting the point from which one falls, ἀπ᾽ ὤμων χαμαὶ πέσε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.803; ἀπ᾽ οὐρανοῦ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 44.3; ἀπό τινος ὄνου 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 701d; ἐκ χειρῶν π. ἡνία 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.583; π. ἐκ νηός 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.417; πεσὼν ἐκ νηὸς ἀποφθίμην ἐνὶ πόντῳ 10.51.}}
**4** Geometry texts, of perpendiculars or parts of applied figures, π. ἐπί τι **fall** upon, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra 3.11, 3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ τῶν ὀχουμένων” 2.8, +others, 3rd-2nd c.BC: Apollonius Pergaeus Geometra “Conica” 1.2}}; but π. ἐπί τι, ποτί τι, **intersect, meet**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ κωνοειδέων καὶ σφαιροειδέων” 16, “Spir.” 15}}; π. διά τινος **pass** through, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. author] “Con.Sph.” 17; π. κατά τινος [prev. author] “Sph.Cyl.” 1 “Def.” 2; ἐπί τι κατά τινα 3rd-2nd c.BC: Apollonius Pergaeus Geometra “Conica” 1.2.}}
**B** Special usages:
**B.I** πίπτειν ἔν τισι **fall violently upon, attack**, ἐνὶ νήεσσι πέσωμεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.742 (but ἐν νήεσσι πεσόντες}} **tumbling** into the ships, {{Refs|2.175)}}; ἐν βουσὶ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 375 (Lyric poetry)}}; Ἔρως, ὃς ἐν κτήμασι π. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ant.” 782 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἐπ᾽ ἀλλήλοισι, of combatants, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 379, compare 375; πρὸς μῆλα καὶ ποίμνας 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1061; πρὸς πύλαις 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 462.}}
**B.I.2** **throw oneself down, fall down**, πρὸς βρέτη θεῶν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 185; ἀμφὶ σὸν γόνυ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 787}}; ἐς γόνατα on one's knees, of a wrestler, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 156; ἐς τὸν ὦμον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 571.}}
**B.II** **fall** in battle, πῖπτε δὲ λαός {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.67, etc.}}; οἱ πεπτωκότες **the fallen**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.24; νέκυες πίπτοντες 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.200; νεκροὶ περὶ νεκροῖς πεπτωκότες 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 881; πεσήματα.. πέπτωκε δοριπετῆ νεκρῶν [prev. author] “Andr.” 653; π. ὑπὸ Ἀθηναίων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.67; ὡς.. θάμνοι πρόρριζοι πίπτουσι.., ὣς ἄρ᾽ ὑπ᾽ Ἀτρεΐδῃ πῖπτε κάρηνα Τρώων 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.157, compare 500, etc.; τὸ Περσῶν ἄνθος οἴχεται πεσόν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 252.}}
**B.II.2** **fall, be ruined**, δόμον δοκοῦντα κάρτα νῦν πεπτωκέναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ch.” 263, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 22 e; πεσεῖν.. πτώματ᾽ οὐκ ἀνασχετά 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 919, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 181b; στάντες τ᾽ ἐς ὀρθὸν καὶ πεσόντες ὕστερον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 50}}; ἀβουλίᾳ, ἐξ ἀβουλίας π., {{Refs|[prev. author] “El.” 429, 398; ἀπὸ σμικροῦ κακοῦ [prev. author] “Aj.” 1078}}; of an army, μεγάλα πεσόντα πρήγματα ὑπὸ ἡσσόνων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.18, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.89; ὁ Ξέρξεω στρατὸς αὐτὸς ὑπ᾽ ἑωυτοῦ ἔπιπτε 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.16}}; of a city, π. δορί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 5.}}
**B.II.3** **fall, sink**, ἄνεμος πέσε the wind **fell**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.202 (but in 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 547, Βορέαο πεσόντος is used for ἐμπεσόντος}}, **falling on, blowing on** one): metaph, πέπτωκεν κομπάσματα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 794, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 474: with _dative_, ταῖς ἐλπίσι πεσεῖν}} **fail** in one's hopes, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.87.1.}}
**B.II.4** **fall short, fail**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 100e}}; of a playwright, **fail**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 540.}}
**B.III** πίπτειν ἔκ τινος **fall out of, lose** a thing, unintentionally, σοι ἐκ θυμοῦ πεσέειν **fall out of, lose** thy favour, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.595}}; ἐξ ἐλπίδων π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 420.5; τοὔμπαλιν π. φρενῶν [prev. author] “Hipp.” 390}}; also of set purpose, ἐξ ἀρκύων π. **escape** from.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 147; ἔξω τῶν κακῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 970.}}
**B.III.2** reversely, πολλὴν ἐς κακότητα π. {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 42; εἰς ἄτην 6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 13.68; εἰς δουλοσύνην [prev. author] 9.4; ἐς δάκρυα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.21; ἐς νόσον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 478}}; εἰς ἔρον, ἔριν, ὀργήν, φόβον, ἀνάγκας, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1172, “fragment” 578.8, “Or.” 696, “Ph.” 69, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.82}}; also ἐν γυιοπέδαις π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.41; ἐν μέσοις ἀρκυστάτοις 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1476; ἐν φόβῳ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1418 (Lyric poetry); ἐν σολοικισμῷ 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Sol.” 3; πρὸς τόλμαν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ichn.” 11: with _dative_ only, π. δυσπραξίαις [prev. author] “Aj.” 759; αἰσχύνῃ [prev. author] “Tr.” 597, etc.}}; οὐκ ἔχω ποῖ γνώμης πέσω I know not which way **to turn**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 705.}}
**B.III.3** εἰς ὕπνον π. **fall** asleep, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ph.” 826; but ἐν ὕπνῳ 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 4(3).23}}; simply ὕπνῳ, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 68.}}
**B.III.4** π. εἰς (ἰατρικὴν) χρῆσιν **to be applied** to (medicinal) use, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 5.19,151, +others}}
**B.III.5** π. ὑπ᾽ αἴσθησιν **to be accessible** to perception, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de communi mathematica scientia” 8, “in Nic.” p.7 P.}}
**B.IV** πίπτειν μετὰ ποσσὶ γυναικός **to fall** between her feet, i.e. to be born, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.110.}}
**B.V** of the dice, τὰ δεσποτῶν εὖ πεσόντα θήσομαι I shall count my master's lucky **throws** my own, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 32; ἀεὶ γὰρ εὖ πίπτουσιν οἱ Διὸς κύβοι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 895; ὥσπερ οἱ κύβοι· οὐ ταὔτ᾽ ἀεὶ πίπτουσιν 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 34}}; ὥσπερ ἐν πτώσει κύβων πρὸς τὰ πεπτωκότα τίθεσθαι τὰ πράγματα according to **the throws**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 604c}}; ὄνασθαι πρὸς τὰ νῦν π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 718}}; πρὸς τὸ πῖπτον as matters **fall out**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “El.” 639}}; of tossing up with oystershells, κἂν μὲν πίπτῃσι τὰ λεύκ᾽ ἐπάνω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 153.5}}; of lots, ὁ κλῆρος π. τινί or παρά τινα, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 619e, 617e; ἐπί τινα NT.Act.1.26: Astrology texts, π. καλῶς ὁ οἰκοδεσπότης Vett. Val. 7.15.}}
**B.V.2** generally, **fall, turn out**, εὖ πίπτειν **to be lucky**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 603}}; παρὰ γνώμαν π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 12.10}}; of a battle, καραδοκήσοντα τὴν μάχην τῇ πεσέεται to wait and see how **it would fall**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.163, compare 8.130}}; λόγων κορυφαὶ ἐν ἀλαθείᾳ π. **turn out** true, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.69; συμφοραὶ παντοῖαι πίπτουσαι παντοίως 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 709a.}}
**B.V.3** **fall to** one, i.e. to his lot, especially of revenues, **accrue**, τῷ δήμῳ πρόσοδος ἔπιπτε {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 30.31.7; φησιν.. ἑξακισχίλια τάλαντα τοῖς Λακεδαιμονίοις πεσεῖν [prev. author] 2.62.1}}; τὴν πεπτωκότα (sic) μοι οἰκίαν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 251.12 (2nd c.AD)}}; τὰ πίπτοντα διάφορα ἐκ τῶν μυστηρίων {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“IG” 5(1).1390.45 (Andania, 1st c.BC)}}; τὸ πεσὸν ἀπὸ τῆς τιμῆς ἀργύριον {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 20.17}}; **to be paid**, τῶν εἰς Καίσαρα πίπτειν ὀφειλόντων ἐξεταστής {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 17.1.12; τὰ πεπτωκότα εἰς τὸ.. ἱερόν “PEleph.” 10.2 (3rd c.BC)}}; π. ἐπὶ τράπεζαν {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 236.7 (3rd c.BC), “PLond.” 3.1200.1 (2nd c.BC)}}; μὴ πιπτόντων τῶν τόκων {{Refs|“BMus.Inscription” 1032.40 (from Teos)}}; πέπτωκεν ἁλικῆς διά τινος.. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Ostr.Bodl.” i3 (3rd c.BC) (but τὰ ἀπὸ τῶν προσόδων πίπτοντα}} **deficiencies**, {{Refs|“IPE” 12.32 B 75 (from Olbia)).}}
**B.VI** **fall**, of a date or period of Time, π. κατὰ τὴν ρκθ᾽ Ὀλυμπιάδα {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.5.1; οἱ χρόνοι οἱ πίπτοντες ὑπὸ τὴν ἡμετέραν ἱστορίαν [prev. author] 4.2.2.}}
**B.VII** **fall under, belong to** a class, εἰς γένη ταῦτα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1005a2, +others}}; ἐπὶ τὴν αὐτὴν ἐπιστήμην{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 982b8; ὑπὸ τὴν αὐτὴν μέθοδον [prev. author] “Top.” 102a37, compare 151a15; ὑπὸ τέχνην οὐδεμίαν [prev. author] “EN” 1104a8; ἔξω τῶν διῃρημένων γενῶν [prev. author] “PA” 681b1; τὸ μακάριον ἐνταῦθα πεπτωκέναι 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.28U.; ὅσα πέπτωκεν ὑπὸ τὴν.. ἱστορίαν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.14.7.}}
